WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.376
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen,
please welcome

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:16.282
C.E.O. Google Sundar Pichai.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:25.191
[ Applause ]

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:25.725
&gt;&gt; Sundar: Thank you.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:27.060
Thank you all for joining today.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.895
It's great to be here.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.897
It's an exciting moment for us.

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:34.534
We've evolved a long
way as a company.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:37.137
I still remember looking
at our early data centers,

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:40.740
and I look at the scale
at which we are today,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.777
which essentially represents
the way we have scaled our

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.712
infrastructure up.

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:48.848
We've also come a long way
as an enterprise company.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.050
You know, I remember the
early days with Google search

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.554
appliance being our only
product and today we serve our

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.157
Enterprise customers
much more deeply.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:01.161
We are at the beginning of what
is possible with the cloud,

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:03.797
which is why it's
special moment for us.

00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:08.334
It feels like a special time,
the confluence of right people,

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:12.305
right technology
and the right time.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:16.476
For us it got particularly
special in December because we

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:21.247
welcomed Diane Greene, a leader
with deep IT experience and the

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.983
pedigree of an entrepreneur.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.554
She joined Google to run
all of Cloud and Apps,

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:30.490
and I can't imagine a
better person to do this.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.660
She's already made many positive
changes and you'll hear about

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.895
all of them today.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:41.167
When she started VMware, Diane
helped change the industry by

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:44.304
getting businesses start
off virtual machines.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:49.375
Virtualization, the practice
of running several operating

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:52.645
systems at the same time,
on a single computer,

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:57.383
was an entirely new way of using
computers more efficiently.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.553
It helped businesses
save a lot of money.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:05.225
Today, we are at another
inflection point.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:10.964
As you know, businesses are just
starting to adopt the concept of

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.099
a public cloud.

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:16.302
But what exists today is
largely a part of decades-old

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:21.474
technology, essentially virtual
machines to a scale server end

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:24.978
run, but in the future almost
everything will be done in the

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:29.916
cloud, because it simply is
a better way to do computing.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:34.254
For years we have been investing
in scaling up our infrastructure

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:35.555
to do this.

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:38.892
Because the things we care
about as a business are the same

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:42.795
things many of you care about as
you're building your businesses.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:47.800
Because of our investments,
we have scaled many products.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.303
In fact, we have
over seven products,

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:53.072
each of which has a
billion users each.

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:58.545
And to put the scale in
perspective, in Gmail alone,

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:03.650
every single day we handle over
1.4 petabytes of information,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.887
that's like 1400 Libraries
of Congress every single day.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:10.890
We do this with
99.7% availability.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:16.496
We do this across a private
network by having virtual

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.799
presence in every
corner of the world.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:22.802
So we have scaled up our
infrastructure a lot.

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:26.706
And we primarily built it for
use by Google for our services

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.342
to serve our users.

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:33.746
But we are at a point where we
are opening it up so that every

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:36.849
business can build what's next.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:40.853
And we want this to work
for all kinds of businesses,

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:45.625
whether you're a large business
like SnapChat or Spotify,

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:49.929
or a small business, or a
business of any size in between.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:52.298
We don't think of this as
technology just for Silicon

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:53.366
Valley.

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:57.303
We want this to be technology
for every developer and every

00:03:57.303 --> 00:04:01.641
business no matter
what size or location.

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:05.311
For example, in India, an
up-and-coming CGI animation

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:10.216
studio in Mumbai, Assemblage, it
used the Google Cloud Platform

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:14.354
to work on the recent animated
movie, "Norm of the North.".

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:18.191
It shows that you no longer need
to be a Google software engineer

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:21.027
to get access to
Google infrastructure.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.463
And when we say
Google infrastructure,

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:27.133
we mean best
security, reliability,

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:32.105
analytics and computer research,
all of which you'll hear about

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.273
today.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:38.011
As I said at the start,
this is a special moment.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:42.048
We have set up with the right
people, the right technology,

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.783
and at the right time.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:46.953
I know you want to get into more
details and we want to share

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:50.156
what's next, and
so to talk more,

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:53.793
I'm very happy to introduce
Diane Greene who needs no

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.795
introduction when it
comes to Enterprise.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:56.796
Thank you.

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:57.797
[ Applause ]

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:02.802
[ Music ]

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:15.682
&gt;&gt; Diane: So welcome
and thank you, Sundar.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:18.051
You know, one of the things
about I've discovered since

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:21.054
coming to Google is just the
excellence in experience brought

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:24.057
to bear on absolutely
everything everybody does.

00:05:24.057 --> 00:05:27.627
I'm going to talk about that
in terms of our cloud today,

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:31.230
but I'm also just going to
mention this event and how

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.299
amazingly well
it's come together.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:36.135
You know, yesterday those
beautiful windows on the bay

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:42.608
were garage doors, and there's
a kiosk out there with a giant

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.044
touch screen where you can
indicate your interest and it

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:49.182
will tell you different partners
that have relevant technologies.

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:50.883
Check it out.

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.919
I'm really looking forward
to scaling that up.

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:56.522
So welcome.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:59.292
We've got several thousand
people here today,

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.294
here in San Francisco
on the wharf,

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.730
one of my favorite
places in the world,

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.532
and tens of thousands
more on live stream.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:11.270
And we have -- and then at watch
parties we have people on six

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.039
different continents.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.276
I also want to welcome the
190-plus press and analysts from

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.812
all over the world
that are here.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:23.349
To all of you in the room
and everywhere, just welcome.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:24.550
It's great to have you here.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:30.123
[ Applause ]

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:31.991
Yeah.

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:33.926
Okay.

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:37.396
So I founded VMware
and, you know,

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:40.133
what we had there was just a
better way of doing things,

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.769
saved people lots
of time and money.

00:06:42.769 --> 00:06:45.471
You know, I could go into any
business and it was so easy to

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:48.407
show the values, the benefits.

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:53.613
And what we have here at Google
Cloud is just so much more.

00:06:53.613 --> 00:06:57.350
And as Sundar said, it's all
just a better way to do things.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:02.488
This has kind of been a voyage
of discovery for me over the

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:06.225
last couple of years as I've
gotten to know everybody and

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:08.294
learn about all
the technologies.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:12.431
You know, Google's
technical infrastructure,

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:15.134
just amazing inventions.

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:17.970
That's what allows Google to
power the biggest search engine

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:19.305
in the world.

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:23.309
And more recently taking our
machine learning technologies

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:26.579
and infusing them into our
products and more which you'll

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:27.580
hear about today.

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:34.253
Google's had no choice but
to do this with amazingly

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.723
sophisticated security.

00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:42.962
But what's I think most exciting
to me is Google now's just so

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:47.567
all-in on what we can
do for the Enterprise.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:52.205
All that we can bring and help
organizations of all sizes all

00:07:52.205 --> 00:07:54.707
over the world.

00:07:54.707 --> 00:07:59.478
I also want to add that Google
is strengthened by a pretty

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.781
broad portfolio
in the Enterprise.

00:08:01.781 --> 00:08:04.584
You know, we've got Google maps.

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:05.985
We have chrome.

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:06.986
Android.

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:08.988
Google analytics just
launched last week.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:14.827
And then our beyond productivity
office suite, Google Apps,

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:18.297
that all of Google runs on.

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:19.699
It's behind our culture.

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:21.834
We built it.

00:08:21.834 --> 00:08:23.903
The collaboration and docks.

00:08:23.903 --> 00:08:29.976
The video conferencing,
Hangouts, Drive, Gmail.

00:08:29.976 --> 00:08:32.345
It's what lets us
be so communicative,

00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:35.514
so transparent and
so collaborative.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:43.589
Now, when I joined Google back
in December, as a Noogler,

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:47.526
you know, wearing one of those
propeller heads -- propeller

00:08:47.526 --> 00:08:52.064
hats, I got -- on my very first
day I got to go visit Google's

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:56.936
biggest data center in Iowa, and
all of us that went were just

00:08:56.936 --> 00:09:01.674
completely awestruck
by the lack of people,

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:04.877
mostly security guards,
the amazing security,

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:09.248
the HVAC systems,
and how green it was.

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:15.154
The incredible passion around
being energy efficient.

00:09:15.154 --> 00:09:18.124
And that's kind of why I've
chosen pictures of data centers

00:09:18.124 --> 00:09:21.360
as the backdrop for
most of my slides.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.731
Our data centers and our
system's infrastructure are

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:28.134
invisible to our
customers and partners,

00:09:28.134 --> 00:09:30.436
but they really underpin
everything we do.

00:09:34.707 --> 00:09:37.043
So why -- you know, it's
been really exciting.

00:09:37.043 --> 00:09:40.613
Customers -- we're seeing
real acceleration in customers

00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.516
choosing us, and why
are they doing that?

00:09:43.516 --> 00:09:48.454
Well, I think it could be put
into three main reasons: Better

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:51.657
value in terms of
price and performance.

00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:57.396
Reduced risk in terms of
security, open source software.

00:09:57.396 --> 00:10:02.268
And one of my favorite, the
access to the innovation.

00:10:02.268 --> 00:10:05.171
I'll spend a few minutes going
to each one and I hope all of

00:10:05.171 --> 00:10:09.375
you do a deep dive over the next
two days to learn more details

00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:10.376
about this.

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:17.650
Our cloud is faster from
the BIS to the processors,

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:23.122
to the time it takes to bring
up some queued virtual machine.

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:26.225
A simple feature like snapshots
is one to two ordinates of

00:10:26.225 --> 00:10:32.264
magnitude faster than the secure
copy which is the alternative.

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:34.734
And we're delivering on that
performance with the most

00:10:34.734 --> 00:10:36.035
competitive price.

00:10:38.671 --> 00:10:41.941
The efficiency with which we can
run our infrastructure lets us

00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:46.846
offer this price performance
often 50% better, or better,

00:10:46.846 --> 00:10:53.452
and our pricing is
customer friendly too.

00:10:53.452 --> 00:10:57.957
We give you discounts
automatically the more you use.

00:10:57.957 --> 00:11:01.961
We do things like provide
configurable virtual machines so

00:11:01.961 --> 00:11:04.530
that you can configure
whatever machine you want,

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:09.001
be it 7 gigabytes, 6
cores, whatever you want.

00:11:12.438 --> 00:11:15.007
We don't require it
to be a power of two,

00:11:15.007 --> 00:11:17.977
which as you might imagine is
-- causes some pretty wasteful

00:11:17.977 --> 00:11:22.448
overprovisioning, so better
value through a combination of

00:11:22.448 --> 00:11:24.050
price and performance.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:29.055
Oops.

00:11:32.825 --> 00:11:33.826
Can you go back?

00:11:33.826 --> 00:11:34.927
I -- I got confused.

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:35.928
Thank you.

00:11:35.928 --> 00:11:38.798
Reduced risk.

00:11:38.798 --> 00:11:41.233
Reduced risk, first
from security.

00:11:41.233 --> 00:11:46.939
You know, something I've learned
since coming to Google is that

00:11:46.939 --> 00:11:52.311
just an unbelievable tax that we
have on making all -- ensure all

00:11:52.311 --> 00:11:56.982
our customers' data is secured.

00:11:56.982 --> 00:11:59.085
You know, we've been hardened
over the years because we're

00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:04.557
just in the crosshairs of all of
the best hackers in the world.

00:12:04.557 --> 00:12:07.660
And so when you come
to Google Cloud,

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:10.996
you benefit from literally
thousands of people-years of

00:12:10.996 --> 00:12:14.066
engineering that
we've put into this.

00:12:14.066 --> 00:12:18.170
It's not just security, but it's
also the privacy and compliance

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:21.307
and the high bar we hold
ourselves to in terms of

00:12:21.307 --> 00:12:26.946
protecting the privacy and
security of our customers.

00:12:26.946 --> 00:12:30.015
But the other area of risk
reduction comes from our just

00:12:30.015 --> 00:12:32.551
absolute commitment
to open source.

00:12:34.753 --> 00:12:37.923
We open source -- we
take our innovations,

00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:41.627
we put them in our products,
we open source them,

00:12:41.627 --> 00:12:46.565
and then we provide an API, and
we're more than happy to compete

00:12:46.565 --> 00:12:49.602
based on the quality
of our implementations.

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:53.239
We've open sourced Kubernetes,
our Container Engine for

00:12:53.239 --> 00:12:55.908
managing server instances.

00:12:55.908 --> 00:12:59.044
We've open sourced dataflow,
our batch and streaming data

00:12:59.044 --> 00:13:01.147
pipeline.

00:13:01.147 --> 00:13:08.087
It's currently in incubation as
an Apache Beam, on Apache Beam,

00:13:08.087 --> 00:13:10.723
and we've open
sourced tensor flow.

00:13:10.723 --> 00:13:15.261
That's our second generation
deep learning algorithms.

00:13:15.261 --> 00:13:18.197
We're using them to power
services like image search,

00:13:18.197 --> 00:13:21.734
or the smart reply in inbox.

00:13:21.734 --> 00:13:23.936
And then of course,
once you open source,

00:13:23.936 --> 00:13:28.941
you get a community, a vibrant
community where things can

00:13:28.941 --> 00:13:32.611
improve more and more quickly.

00:13:32.611 --> 00:13:37.116
The last reason,
access to innovation.

00:13:37.116 --> 00:13:41.620
Our customers are already
benefiting from our approach.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.622
We want to have
NoOps, automated IT.

00:13:43.622 --> 00:13:47.693
It's based on our
serverless architecture.

00:13:47.693 --> 00:13:50.563
Today we have App Engine,
BigQuery, and Container Engine,

00:13:50.563 --> 00:13:53.999
they just work, without you ever
having to think about whether

00:13:53.999 --> 00:13:57.603
you've got enough servers or
if things are working right.

00:13:57.603 --> 00:14:00.873
And we're well on your way to
a full NoOps where you can just

00:14:00.873 --> 00:14:01.907
write and run.

00:14:04.176 --> 00:14:07.913
One of our best-known
customers is SnapChat.

00:14:07.913 --> 00:14:11.650
They went from zero to more than
100 million users without even

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.053
formally hiring an ops team.

00:14:14.053 --> 00:14:16.322
Tim Sehn, their VP of
engineering said, yeah,

00:14:16.322 --> 00:14:18.324
it was just sort of
me and one other guy.

00:14:18.324 --> 00:14:19.325
It's just work.

00:14:19.325 --> 00:14:21.660
We've scaled up.

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.196
You see this in our
big data services.

00:14:24.196 --> 00:14:25.564
There's really nothing close.

00:14:25.564 --> 00:14:29.134
You type in the query, it's
instantly distributed across

00:14:29.134 --> 00:14:32.204
thousands of cords and
magically it just happens.

00:14:32.204 --> 00:14:36.609
What makes all this work is a
very dense network bandwidth,

00:14:36.609 --> 00:14:39.778
we have widths in
between our clusters.

00:14:39.778 --> 00:14:43.482
Our whole platform is designed
as an integrated architecture,

00:14:43.482 --> 00:14:46.986
and without that, the kind of
highly distributed processing we

00:14:46.986 --> 00:14:48.053
do is impossible.

00:14:50.289 --> 00:14:53.993
One of the Spotify engineers
put it best in a tweet after we

00:14:53.993 --> 00:14:56.829
announced they were using GCP.

00:14:56.829 --> 00:15:00.599
"Finally I can tell the world
that BigQuery is the best thing

00:15:00.599 --> 00:15:03.569
that's ever happened
to me." Now,

00:15:03.569 --> 00:15:06.905
people don't say that unless
you're saving them an incredible

00:15:06.905 --> 00:15:09.108
amount of time and effort.

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:15.281
So for us this access to
innovation is harvesting our R&amp;D

00:15:15.281 --> 00:15:19.251
investments in new productions
that everybody regardless of

00:15:19.251 --> 00:15:21.687
your size, can use.

00:15:21.687 --> 00:15:23.689
We'll have more to say
about innovation today.

00:15:23.689 --> 00:15:25.691
You'll be hearing
about security,

00:15:25.691 --> 00:15:29.495
our machine learning platform,
new approaches to operations for

00:15:29.495 --> 00:15:34.300
hybrid cloud, and have since to
platform as a service plans and

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:37.803
our regional expansion plans.

00:15:37.803 --> 00:15:40.439
And now I'll just take a few
minutes to talk about what's

00:15:40.439 --> 00:15:42.641
ahead at a fairly high level.

00:15:42.641 --> 00:15:45.344
You know, I've seen Google's
development accelerate over the

00:15:45.344 --> 00:15:47.513
past 18 months.

00:15:47.513 --> 00:15:49.782
I think it's nothing compared to
what we're going to be able to

00:15:49.782 --> 00:15:52.418
do over the next two years.

00:15:52.418 --> 00:15:55.521
But I'm going to highlight three
of the areas that we consider

00:15:55.521 --> 00:16:01.527
particularly important:
Security, machine learning,

00:16:01.527 --> 00:16:05.264
and the transition to the
next generation of cloud.

00:16:05.264 --> 00:16:06.265
How we go to market.

00:16:06.265 --> 00:16:09.134
How we help our
customers be successful.

00:16:09.134 --> 00:16:11.070
First, security.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.105
There's a lot more we
can do going forward.

00:16:13.105 --> 00:16:16.275
We can take the tools that
Google's developed internally

00:16:16.275 --> 00:16:19.144
and we can make them
available to our customers.

00:16:19.144 --> 00:16:20.145
Machine learning.

00:16:20.145 --> 00:16:26.118
Machine learning is pretty much
revolutionizing every industry.

00:16:26.118 --> 00:16:28.954
It's really gotten to the
point where if your customer is

00:16:28.954 --> 00:16:32.324
embracing machine learning,
it would be prudent for you to

00:16:32.324 --> 00:16:33.325
embrace it too.

00:16:33.325 --> 00:16:37.596
And you'll be able to expect
constant upgrades in our machine

00:16:37.596 --> 00:16:42.334
learning and you'll be able to
expect more and more train data

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:44.570
in different verticals
supported over time.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:49.708
Moving to cloud.

00:16:49.708 --> 00:16:53.545
What we want to do here is make
it really nondisruptive for

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:57.549
everybody to move to our cloud,
to transition in a way that

00:16:57.549 --> 00:17:00.953
saves them time and money and
actually uplevels what your

00:17:00.953 --> 00:17:02.287
businesses can do.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.693
It's really key to
our market strategy.

00:17:07.693 --> 00:17:09.995
And just say that
in many, many ways,

00:17:09.995 --> 00:17:13.665
cloud is so different from
selling Enterprise hardware and

00:17:13.665 --> 00:17:15.000
software.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:20.205
We're early in understanding
exactly how to sell well and

00:17:20.205 --> 00:17:21.907
grow our customers.

00:17:21.907 --> 00:17:23.909
Some of the basics are the same.

00:17:23.909 --> 00:17:27.513
We're definitely setting up
programs to enable our partners,

00:17:27.513 --> 00:17:31.884
providing training,
certification, solutions,

00:17:31.884 --> 00:17:35.354
also for our customers,
and programs to ensure our

00:17:35.354 --> 00:17:37.456
customers' success.

00:17:37.456 --> 00:17:40.459
You know, one area that's really
getting some reinvention is

00:17:40.459 --> 00:17:43.128
pricing models, you
know -- you know,

00:17:43.128 --> 00:17:47.366
the cloud vendors do the --
should be the ones doing the

00:17:47.366 --> 00:17:51.036
capacity planning, not the
customers with committing

00:17:51.036 --> 00:17:52.137
long-term out.

00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:55.073
That's the spirit of the cloud.

00:17:55.073 --> 00:17:59.411
But what we're really seeing is
the technical expertise and the

00:17:59.411 --> 00:18:03.615
advance planning required of us
to help our customers get ready

00:18:03.615 --> 00:18:06.985
to move to the cloud, take
advantage of the cloud,

00:18:06.985 --> 00:18:10.289
explore more optimal
ways to do things.

00:18:10.289 --> 00:18:15.694
You know, we can see that we're
going to function as an applied

00:18:15.694 --> 00:18:19.465
R&amp;D team for our
customers, in effect.

00:18:19.465 --> 00:18:21.767
And it's because of this that we
think we'll be able to give our

00:18:21.767 --> 00:18:25.437
customers a step function
boost in their velocity.

00:18:25.437 --> 00:18:29.775
And what do I mean by this?

00:18:29.775 --> 00:18:31.944
What is this lift?

00:18:31.944 --> 00:18:34.913
Well, I've learned almost
everything I know from racing

00:18:34.913 --> 00:18:38.283
sailboats so I'm going to
use a boat metaphor here,

00:18:38.283 --> 00:18:40.652
and with the exception
of planing, you know,

00:18:40.652 --> 00:18:44.323
boats are kind of constrained
by how fast they can go by the

00:18:44.323 --> 00:18:48.227
friction on the hull which is
proportional to the square root

00:18:48.227 --> 00:18:51.063
of the water line length.

00:18:51.063 --> 00:18:54.233
Not unlike -- this is not unlike
the friction existing between

00:18:54.233 --> 00:18:57.302
computer architectures
and systems imposed on the

00:18:57.302 --> 00:19:02.508
businesses, but I think there
it's an inverse function of the

00:19:02.508 --> 00:19:07.880
ability to hire skilled
systems and operations people.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:11.316
So we've all seen a revolution
over the last few years.

00:19:11.316 --> 00:19:15.254
We saw it on San Francisco
bay in sailboats.

00:19:15.254 --> 00:19:19.858
Hydrofoiling catamarans that can
go 55 miles an hour and making

00:19:19.858 --> 00:19:22.895
its way to sailboats,
kite boards.

00:19:22.895 --> 00:19:27.266
So we built the IT equivalent so
you don't have to spend the time

00:19:27.266 --> 00:19:31.770
and money to do it yourself, and
it's at scale so we can serve

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.306
billions of users and
millions of businesses;

00:19:34.306 --> 00:19:37.109
we can give you
this sort of lift,

00:19:37.109 --> 00:19:41.713
this sort of force multiplier,
permanently accelerate your

00:19:41.713 --> 00:19:44.182
progress and on
an ongoing basis.

00:19:47.419 --> 00:19:49.454
So GCP is growing.

00:19:49.454 --> 00:19:52.190
We're seeing potentially huge
growths in our core storage

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:56.795
services, our core compute
services and we're excited to

00:19:56.795 --> 00:20:00.132
see the new innovations that
we're bringing out are just kind

00:20:00.132 --> 00:20:01.333
of taking off.

00:20:01.333 --> 00:20:06.238
Google Container Engine usage
has been doubling every 90 days,

00:20:06.238 --> 00:20:08.774
growing faster than
virtual machines,

00:20:08.774 --> 00:20:11.577
and we're expecting the
same from some of our newer

00:20:11.577 --> 00:20:13.679
innovations that we'll
be announcing today.

00:20:16.415 --> 00:20:19.451
We're serious about
this business.

00:20:19.451 --> 00:20:24.456
Alphabet invested $9.9
billion in CAP X in 2015,

00:20:24.456 --> 00:20:30.295
and that investment expands our
already-considerable ability to

00:20:30.295 --> 00:20:35.300
provide cloud infrastructure
services to you today.

00:20:38.537 --> 00:20:41.373
We're excited to make
our innovation engine,

00:20:41.373 --> 00:20:45.978
our technology work for you,
for companies of every size,

00:20:45.978 --> 00:20:48.280
and we really want to
make you a success.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:49.281
Your success.

00:20:49.281 --> 00:20:52.351
You know, it's so
exciting to us.

00:20:52.351 --> 00:20:55.587
So it's terrific
to have you here.

00:20:55.587 --> 00:20:59.424
Enjoy the conference, and
now it's just my incredible

00:20:59.424 --> 00:21:05.631
privilege to invite up our next
speaker, our Alphabet chairman,

00:21:05.631 --> 00:21:06.632
Eric Schmidt.

00:21:06.632 --> 00:21:10.068
[ Applause ]

00:21:12.371 --> 00:21:14.706
&gt;&gt; Eric: Thank you.

00:21:14.706 --> 00:21:19.711
Thank you very much, Diane.

00:21:19.711 --> 00:21:22.648
And thank you guys
for being here.

00:21:22.648 --> 00:21:26.118
I was thinking about it; imagine
what an Internet operating

00:21:26.118 --> 00:21:28.620
system really should be.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.756
It should take
care of everything.

00:21:30.756 --> 00:21:35.227
And it occurred to me that I've
been doing this for 45 years,

00:21:35.227 --> 00:21:38.063
and that this notion of
increasing abstraction has been

00:21:38.063 --> 00:21:41.933
in front of me my entire
career, literally.

00:21:41.933 --> 00:21:47.539
So when I started, I was an IBM
360 hex assembler programmer.

00:21:47.539 --> 00:21:48.707
Sorry.

00:21:48.707 --> 00:21:50.642
And C was invented.

00:21:50.642 --> 00:21:53.412
And I just thought it
was the neatest thing,

00:21:53.412 --> 00:21:55.847
because programming
and assembly was hard,

00:21:55.847 --> 00:21:59.184
and I kept arguing are the
computers fast enough, right?

00:21:59.184 --> 00:22:01.186
Because I need a
really fast computer.

00:22:01.186 --> 00:22:04.656
And eventually they convinced
me that C was fast enough.

00:22:04.656 --> 00:22:07.859
I know this sounds archaic,
but the fact of the matter is,

00:22:07.859 --> 00:22:11.329
the same has been true
for layer after layer,

00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:14.466
and so this went from C and
C++ and then Java, of course,

00:22:14.466 --> 00:22:17.602
which we announced -- I in fact
announced in 1994 next door at

00:22:17.602 --> 00:22:20.806
Moscone, then into the Web
and then with infrastructure,

00:22:20.806 --> 00:22:22.808
and it's also been
true of hardware,

00:22:22.808 --> 00:22:24.810
and all the ways
everybody here knows here.

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.546
Soldering on chips, I literally
used to do this to many

00:22:27.546 --> 00:22:30.615
computers to then the PC
architecture and now systems on

00:22:30.615 --> 00:22:31.583
a chip.

00:22:31.583 --> 00:22:37.089
The 1980s, we announced at Sun
the network is the computer.

00:22:37.089 --> 00:22:42.260
And in 1997, I announced
the network computer, right?

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:44.696
So we've been working on this
for a very, very long time,

00:22:44.696 --> 00:22:47.766
but each of these layers is
a new layer of abstraction,

00:22:47.766 --> 00:22:51.737
and the whole idea is to
automate the tedious detail and

00:22:51.737 --> 00:22:52.838
empower people, right?

00:22:52.838 --> 00:22:54.940
That's really what it's about.

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:58.310
So along the way, Google
invents Google III.

00:22:58.310 --> 00:23:02.914
Now, I'm often asked why
did we invent Google III,

00:23:02.914 --> 00:23:05.484
and the answer is
because we had Google II,

00:23:05.484 --> 00:23:09.855
and when it had an error, it
would say, grow up, horsy,

00:23:09.855 --> 00:23:11.656
and then it would stop.

00:23:11.656 --> 00:23:12.924
This was a problem.

00:23:12.924 --> 00:23:14.926
And if you wanted
to delete files,

00:23:14.926 --> 00:23:18.497
you had to make sure that the
mod file in the file security

00:23:18.497 --> 00:23:23.135
handle changed without your
permission from 977 to 0,

00:23:23.135 --> 00:23:25.137
and aside from that,
it was a great product.

00:23:25.137 --> 00:23:29.541
So Google III was invented
roughly 2002, 2003,

00:23:29.541 --> 00:23:34.980
internally at Google, all right,
in order to -- in order to sort

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:39.151
of build what you would think of
as the network operating system

00:23:39.151 --> 00:23:40.652
that we needed.

00:23:40.652 --> 00:23:42.654
And what's interesting
that this platform,

00:23:42.654 --> 00:23:44.890
which we used obviously
incredibly successfully,

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:48.627
and at a time we were investing
four times more capital than the

00:23:48.627 --> 00:23:52.798
sum of all of the other computer
companies in existence at the

00:23:52.798 --> 00:23:56.168
time, just to sort of build out
the data structures and build

00:23:56.168 --> 00:23:59.371
out these platforms, and so
what would happen if you came to

00:23:59.371 --> 00:24:01.673
Google in a programmer
in sort of early 2000s,

00:24:01.673 --> 00:24:05.010
you didn't have to think
about network segments,

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:09.881
denial service attacks, beat-off
IP addresses, scaling, SSL,

00:24:09.881 --> 00:24:11.216
machine resources.

00:24:11.216 --> 00:24:13.518
What happened was the machine
took care of it automatically

00:24:13.518 --> 00:24:15.921
and you could
build Gmail, right?

00:24:15.921 --> 00:24:17.789
Or you could build docs.

00:24:17.789 --> 00:24:21.326
Or you could build the
next enhancement of search.

00:24:21.326 --> 00:24:26.832
So in 2008, we announced App
Engine, and I figured weird up,

00:24:26.832 --> 00:24:28.300
right?

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.435
I mean App Engine
is beautiful, right?

00:24:30.435 --> 00:24:33.405
And many, many people
in this audience use it.

00:24:33.405 --> 00:24:37.175
And what happened was App Engine
was like the APIs that went into

00:24:37.175 --> 00:24:38.143
Google III.

00:24:38.143 --> 00:24:39.144
All right.

00:24:39.144 --> 00:24:41.146
so today, if you're
using App Engine,

00:24:41.146 --> 00:24:43.982
you're using the same interfaces
that have been used internal at

00:24:43.982 --> 00:24:44.983
Google.

00:24:44.983 --> 00:24:47.686
I figured okay, we're done, it's
going to be hugely successful,

00:24:47.686 --> 00:24:52.691
and along the way, here is what
we did: We built MapReduce,

00:24:52.691 --> 00:24:54.693
you guys can use what
is called data proc,

00:24:54.693 --> 00:24:56.695
which is sort of a more
sophisticated version.

00:24:56.695 --> 00:24:57.696
We built Dremel, right?

00:24:57.696 --> 00:25:00.765
You guys can use BigQuery, a
more sophisticated version.

00:25:00.765 --> 00:25:02.834
We built something
internally called Colossus,

00:25:02.834 --> 00:25:06.872
because we ran out of big names,
and you can -- you now know that

00:25:06.872 --> 00:25:08.773
as Google Cloud Storage.

00:25:08.773 --> 00:25:11.877
We built Stubby, not my
favorite product name.

00:25:11.877 --> 00:25:15.447
You can use GRPC, also not
my favorite product name.

00:25:15.447 --> 00:25:18.283
We built Bigtable;
you can use Bigtable.

00:25:18.283 --> 00:25:21.386
Now that's a product
name that I like.

00:25:21.386 --> 00:25:23.455
So the point is
it's really easy.

00:25:23.455 --> 00:25:26.558
Just use -- just use
App Engine, right?

00:25:26.558 --> 00:25:27.559
We're done.

00:25:27.559 --> 00:25:32.497
Ah, and along the same way, we
got very interested in doing

00:25:32.497 --> 00:25:34.966
sharing applications and
built this docs architecture,

00:25:34.966 --> 00:25:37.302
and I remember sitting with the
executive in charge of docs,

00:25:37.302 --> 00:25:39.137
and I said, "What's different?

00:25:39.137 --> 00:25:40.138
What's different?

00:25:40.138 --> 00:25:42.474
You got to show me what's
different." And he scratches

00:25:42.474 --> 00:25:45.243
his head for awhile and
he said "sharing", and I,

00:25:45.243 --> 00:25:48.780
in my typical stupid way said,
"Who cares about sharing?"

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:51.316
And he said, "Do you
share calendars?"

00:25:51.316 --> 00:25:56.488
I said, "yeah." Well, sharing is
now what has driven Google docs,

00:25:56.488 --> 00:25:58.757
apps, architectures,
all of that,

00:25:58.757 --> 00:26:03.862
to 60% of the Fortune 500
are customers for this stuff.

00:26:03.862 --> 00:26:07.265
We have more than 2 million
paying Enterprise customers.

00:26:07.265 --> 00:26:10.702
It's a huge business for us,
and growing very dramatically,

00:26:10.702 --> 00:26:12.904
so very, very proud of it.

00:26:12.904 --> 00:26:15.640
But there was something
fundamentally wrong with my

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:18.843
conception in 2008 of
what we were doing,

00:26:18.843 --> 00:26:23.481
which was it's not realistic
to expect people to go deNovo,

00:26:23.481 --> 00:26:27.986
right, from an original
architecture into App Engine,

00:26:27.986 --> 00:26:34.259
so we decided that we had to
change our strategy, 2009, 2010,

00:26:34.259 --> 00:26:36.261
to basic -- you
know, it didn't work.

00:26:36.261 --> 00:26:38.463
They didn't give the right
stepping time to the cloud.

00:26:38.463 --> 00:26:39.464
It was too hard.

00:26:39.464 --> 00:26:40.799
It didn't make any sense.

00:26:40.799 --> 00:26:43.902
We decided to meet
you where you are,

00:26:43.902 --> 00:26:47.205
as opposed to where we
think you should be.

00:26:47.205 --> 00:26:48.406
Okay?

00:26:48.406 --> 00:26:49.641
Makes sense.

00:26:49.641 --> 00:26:51.643
First thing you've got to do
is you've got to do a virtual

00:26:51.643 --> 00:26:52.644
machine, right?

00:26:52.644 --> 00:26:53.645
And we do that.

00:26:53.645 --> 00:26:56.848
And that becomes the initial
part of GCP as you know it

00:26:56.848 --> 00:26:59.884
today, and we -- and I figured,
okay, well, we're done now.

00:26:59.884 --> 00:27:01.353
We've done Internet OS.

00:27:01.353 --> 00:27:02.354
Okay.

00:27:02.354 --> 00:27:04.356
Well, that's not
quite true either.

00:27:04.356 --> 00:27:06.324
You know, VM is sort
of pretty naked.

00:27:06.324 --> 00:27:08.326
You've got to do an
awful lot of work.

00:27:08.326 --> 00:27:10.729
Very difficult to sort of
control especially in a dynamic

00:27:10.729 --> 00:27:11.730
environment.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:12.998
We have resource competition.

00:27:12.998 --> 00:27:14.299
How do you share files?

00:27:14.299 --> 00:27:16.401
You have all these
issues around security.

00:27:16.401 --> 00:27:19.504
In fact, oh, my God, you have to
go through the same list again

00:27:19.504 --> 00:27:21.506
that you just went
through with Google III,

00:27:21.506 --> 00:27:23.541
but now you have to
do it with the VM.

00:27:23.541 --> 00:27:24.542
This is like a disaster.

00:27:24.542 --> 00:27:28.880
It's like going back
in computer science.

00:27:28.880 --> 00:27:32.517
So then we built containers, and
the core idea of a container is

00:27:32.517 --> 00:27:35.387
that you can have a little
protected area where your code

00:27:35.387 --> 00:27:38.189
runs inside the container, uses
all the resources and off you

00:27:38.189 --> 00:27:40.525
go, and it works, right?

00:27:40.525 --> 00:27:43.228
Today think Docker, right?

00:27:43.228 --> 00:27:45.263
Docker on top, Linux
with extensions,

00:27:45.263 --> 00:27:48.366
those extensions allowed the
containers to be properly

00:27:48.366 --> 00:27:50.068
instantiated and turned on.

00:27:50.068 --> 00:27:52.837
We had this funny product
called Borg, and I kept saying,

00:27:52.837 --> 00:27:54.172
"What's the Borg?"

00:27:54.172 --> 00:27:56.174
And everyone would
say, "That's the joke".

00:27:56.174 --> 00:27:57.175
All right.

00:27:57.175 --> 00:28:00.545
So a Borg was the manager
of all of these services.

00:28:00.545 --> 00:28:03.548
So today this container
management and scheduling

00:28:03.548 --> 00:28:07.118
management system that we've
built is the natural successor

00:28:07.118 --> 00:28:10.021
to what we invented a
decade ago and it works,

00:28:10.021 --> 00:28:13.625
and it works at a scale Google's
computer cluster is the largest

00:28:13.625 --> 00:28:16.428
I think in the world, in
the universe, in the planet,

00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:18.563
and everywhere else it combines.

00:28:18.563 --> 00:28:20.698
It works at scale you've
never seen before.

00:28:20.698 --> 00:28:23.501
It's mind-boggling
when I see it.

00:28:23.501 --> 00:28:26.271
So then the problem was, now
we've got these containers,

00:28:26.271 --> 00:28:28.273
how do we actually distribute
them and manage them,

00:28:28.273 --> 00:28:31.242
and we invent Kubernetes,
and we open source it.

00:28:31.242 --> 00:28:33.645
It's another name I'm a little
confused about but you'll get

00:28:33.645 --> 00:28:35.413
it, I'm sure.

00:28:35.413 --> 00:28:39.651
We finally got there, and the
reason I'm here is I want to say

00:28:39.651 --> 00:28:43.655
to you right now,
45 years later,

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:46.558
20 years after -- 50 years
after the Internet was invented,

00:28:46.558 --> 00:28:50.061
20 years after the network
computer was invented,

00:28:50.061 --> 00:28:52.530
we've finally invented the
Internet operating system by

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:54.899
meeting you where
you are, right?

00:28:54.899 --> 00:28:58.436
The path is now clear into this.

00:28:58.436 --> 00:29:00.939
And what we've done is we
will now allow you to build an

00:29:00.939 --> 00:29:05.110
abstraction layer at the cloud,
whatever you have to intersect

00:29:05.110 --> 00:29:06.111
with.

00:29:06.111 --> 00:29:08.179
And the reason Diane is here,
and doing a brilliant job,

00:29:08.179 --> 00:29:10.415
is because now we have to
actually make that happen.

00:29:10.415 --> 00:29:11.916
We've got the architecture.

00:29:11.916 --> 00:29:13.785
We were joking about this.

00:29:13.785 --> 00:29:16.554
The hard part is what
we have just done now.

00:29:16.554 --> 00:29:21.459
The relatively easier part is
now getting the technical links

00:29:21.459 --> 00:29:24.696
between it, but you've got to
get the abstraction right and

00:29:24.696 --> 00:29:26.664
you've got to get the
scalability right,

00:29:26.664 --> 00:29:28.900
and we have done
that, and I'm very,

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:32.003
very proud of this
as you can see.

00:29:32.003 --> 00:29:36.941
Now, if you're not completely
with me -- let me give you a

00:29:36.941 --> 00:29:38.143
simple example.

00:29:38.143 --> 00:29:44.015
Take a 1960s car and try to
remember how to use the choke.

00:29:44.015 --> 00:29:45.283
Okay?

00:29:45.283 --> 00:29:46.284
Easy.

00:29:46.284 --> 00:29:47.285
1970s cars, right?

00:29:47.285 --> 00:29:48.820
I used to drive these things.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:49.854
I still do.

00:29:49.854 --> 00:29:51.623
You lost control on ice.

00:29:51.623 --> 00:29:53.024
You had sudden braking.

00:29:53.024 --> 00:29:54.058
No seat belt.

00:29:54.058 --> 00:29:57.695
You could bump other cars
when parking the car, right?

00:29:57.695 --> 00:30:02.800
You had to go to the Triple
A and get a paper map,

00:30:02.800 --> 00:30:04.569
which was free, by the way.

00:30:04.569 --> 00:30:08.873
You had to remember when
you had to service your car.

00:30:08.873 --> 00:30:12.043
And you had to have
these big V6-V8 engines,

00:30:12.043 --> 00:30:14.279
which of course they
sold you at a huge price.

00:30:14.279 --> 00:30:19.551
The 2000s car tracks and
control, antilock, air bags,

00:30:19.551 --> 00:30:22.754
rear bumper sensors,
navigation, service computer,

00:30:22.754 --> 00:30:25.857
and turbo charging
to deal with scale.

00:30:25.857 --> 00:30:29.427
It's exactly analogous, right?

00:30:29.427 --> 00:30:31.429
Think about driving a car today.

00:30:31.429 --> 00:30:33.431
You jump in a car;
it just works.

00:30:33.431 --> 00:30:35.433
You don't have to
think about it.

00:30:35.433 --> 00:30:37.435
It alerts you, so
forth and so on.

00:30:37.435 --> 00:30:38.436
Go back to that 1960s car.

00:30:38.436 --> 00:30:41.806
I'm not suggesting that the
platform that the car will drive

00:30:41.806 --> 00:30:43.508
itself -- oh, yes, I am.

00:30:43.508 --> 00:30:47.478
In fact in 2020, right?

00:30:47.478 --> 00:30:48.479
[ Applause ]

00:30:48.479 --> 00:30:50.348
The car drives itself, right?

00:30:50.348 --> 00:30:54.619
So remember the choke, right?

00:30:54.619 --> 00:30:58.156
Just remember the choke.

00:30:58.156 --> 00:31:00.158
Now, the serious nerds
in the room here,

00:31:00.158 --> 00:31:02.894
really liked the choke.

00:31:02.894 --> 00:31:04.762
Nobody else did.

00:31:04.762 --> 00:31:05.763
Okay?

00:31:05.763 --> 00:31:08.566
That's the secret.

00:31:08.566 --> 00:31:13.004
This cloud platform we're
building will abstract out all

00:31:13.004 --> 00:31:15.740
of those examples
like the choke.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:19.377
What it will do is it will give
you a platform that you can

00:31:19.377 --> 00:31:20.378
really express.

00:31:20.378 --> 00:31:24.148
Any times -- and the reason
it's so important to me,

00:31:24.148 --> 00:31:27.719
is it's timed perfectly for
what's going to happen next.

00:31:27.719 --> 00:31:30.521
And you're going to hear a
little bit about this in a

00:31:30.521 --> 00:31:32.924
minute from a couple
of other people.

00:31:32.924 --> 00:31:39.731
This platform is not the
end, it's the bottom.

00:31:39.731 --> 00:31:42.400
And there's something above it.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:44.902
And the thing that is above
it is machine learning.

00:31:44.902 --> 00:31:49.307
Both narrow AI as it's
called as well as general AI.

00:31:49.307 --> 00:31:52.377
And this is the
next transformation.

00:31:52.377 --> 00:31:54.545
I just want to spend as much
time as I can on it because I

00:31:54.545 --> 00:31:58.449
think it's so profound, and
it changes -- I'm basically a

00:31:58.449 --> 00:32:01.819
programmer who sort of got
lucky, if you will, in Google.

00:32:01.819 --> 00:32:08.226
Sorry, and the -- the
programming paradigm changes.

00:32:08.226 --> 00:32:11.696
Instead of programming
a computer,

00:32:11.696 --> 00:32:15.600
you teach a computer to learn
something and it does what you

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:16.601
want.

00:32:16.601 --> 00:32:18.603
It's a fundamental
change in programming.

00:32:18.603 --> 00:32:22.940
And we have just taken
the initial steps.

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:26.144
We -- the neatest example that
most people can understand about

00:32:26.144 --> 00:32:28.479
machine learning are
involving vision.

00:32:28.479 --> 00:32:31.149
We built a library, called
vision ML which we announced

00:32:31.149 --> 00:32:32.884
which will be demoed today.

00:32:32.884 --> 00:32:35.887
Vision ML does the
classification of photos.

00:32:35.887 --> 00:32:36.888
All right.

00:32:36.888 --> 00:32:37.889
It's great fun.

00:32:37.889 --> 00:32:40.158
And if you get confused over
why this is so important,

00:32:40.158 --> 00:32:41.559
use Google photos.

00:32:41.559 --> 00:32:44.062
Spend a little bit of time
playing with Google photos.

00:32:44.062 --> 00:32:46.064
It's not the fact that it
uploads it to the cloud,

00:32:46.064 --> 00:32:48.066
it's not the fact, that
it does face recognition,

00:32:48.066 --> 00:32:50.635
all of which used to be amazing;
it's the fact that it then

00:32:50.635 --> 00:32:53.371
classifies all your photos
into what you're doing them in.

00:32:53.371 --> 00:32:56.474
And how it does that
is beyond me, right?

00:32:56.474 --> 00:32:58.476
I literally still
don't understand.

00:32:58.476 --> 00:33:02.513
But perhaps the greatest example
is what happened in Korea a week

00:33:02.513 --> 00:33:04.582
ago with AlphaGo.

00:33:04.582 --> 00:33:09.954
AlphaGo as most of you probably
-- Go is the hardest game known.

00:33:09.954 --> 00:33:14.359
It's incomputable in its
traditional -- and the team

00:33:14.359 --> 00:33:16.361
invented neural network,
of which there are two,

00:33:16.361 --> 00:33:18.496
a value network and
a policy network,

00:33:18.496 --> 00:33:21.299
that could be used to
learn how to play the game.

00:33:21.299 --> 00:33:24.001
So the most interesting thing
about AlphaGo is not that

00:33:24.001 --> 00:33:26.003
they're brilliant -- brilliant
people or brilliant programmers,

00:33:26.003 --> 00:33:31.876
but that they had to design,
as part of their software,

00:33:31.876 --> 00:33:34.679
learning algorithms that were
tailored to the way the game

00:33:34.679 --> 00:33:38.015
actually worked, and
that's the next layer.

00:33:38.015 --> 00:33:39.917
It's the layer above.

00:33:39.917 --> 00:33:43.688
And 20 years from now, people
will say remember the choke,

00:33:43.688 --> 00:33:47.725
and then they'll say, yeah,
yeah, and in 2016, yeah, yeah,

00:33:47.725 --> 00:33:49.727
we have all of those VMs
and all of that other stuff,

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:51.729
we don't think
about that anymore.

00:33:51.729 --> 00:33:54.632
But somebody took care of it
and now all we're doing is doing

00:33:54.632 --> 00:33:57.135
various forms of
learning, cognition,

00:33:57.135 --> 00:33:59.670
and modification
improving of code.

00:34:01.739 --> 00:34:04.242
So how do I
translate this today?

00:34:04.242 --> 00:34:07.678
So I figured I would offer
sort of -- and put this in

00:34:07.678 --> 00:34:10.615
perspective and finish up by
saying I think there's ten

00:34:10.615 --> 00:34:12.650
things that you all
can do right now.

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:14.652
Obviously, thank
you all for coming.

00:34:14.652 --> 00:34:16.087
And I'll read them now.

00:34:16.087 --> 00:34:18.022
What's the list?

00:34:18.022 --> 00:34:22.527
If I were right now working
on this right now today,

00:34:22.527 --> 00:34:24.228
what would I do?

00:34:24.228 --> 00:34:27.098
Well, I'd use Linux, I'd use
the Google Cloud and I'd use

00:34:27.098 --> 00:34:29.367
Kubernetes, right, to write
applications that treat the

00:34:29.367 --> 00:34:32.170
cloud the way I described.

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:35.173
In development, I would
use modern, portable,

00:34:35.173 --> 00:34:36.174
scalable languages.

00:34:36.174 --> 00:34:43.080
The one I like is obviously the
language Go, Python, Node.JS,

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:46.617
JavaScript -- Java, and then
use the Google App Engine.

00:34:46.617 --> 00:34:50.221
If I had a clean sheet,
I would start there.

00:34:50.221 --> 00:34:52.790
By the way, for
security, use two factor.

00:34:54.859 --> 00:34:56.093
Let's try again.

00:34:56.093 --> 00:34:58.696
For security, use two factor.

00:34:58.696 --> 00:35:00.164
Right?

00:35:00.164 --> 00:35:01.699
Don't use one factor.

00:35:01.699 --> 00:35:05.102
And if you are sufficiently
paranoid, use three.

00:35:05.102 --> 00:35:06.103
Okay?

00:35:06.103 --> 00:35:08.973
Have fun, right?

00:35:08.973 --> 00:35:09.974
[ Laughter ]

00:35:09.974 --> 00:35:11.476
More factors are better.

00:35:11.476 --> 00:35:13.945
But if you use our
infrastructure,

00:35:13.945 --> 00:35:16.380
which essentially deals with the
distributed denial-of-service

00:35:16.380 --> 00:35:20.751
attack, you will be protected
from this enormous explosion in

00:35:20.751 --> 00:35:23.955
cyber issues that
everybody is dealing with.

00:35:23.955 --> 00:35:26.090
Start with machine learning.

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:27.358
I mentioned Vision ML.

00:35:27.358 --> 00:35:30.828
You're going to hear a whole
bunch more in just a minute.

00:35:30.828 --> 00:35:33.197
We released a product
called TensorFlow,

00:35:33.197 --> 00:35:35.967
which is the initial -- think
of it as nature's multiplication

00:35:35.967 --> 00:35:40.538
platform for it, and it's the
foundation -- clear to me that

00:35:40.538 --> 00:35:43.207
this is going to be the
foundation for this next layer

00:35:43.207 --> 00:35:47.578
of programming, not just at
Google but everywhere else.

00:35:47.578 --> 00:35:48.646
Google used SQL, right?

00:35:48.646 --> 00:35:50.648
Used H base and H base is now
integrated in such a way that

00:35:50.648 --> 00:35:52.650
you think you're using H base
but you're really getting the

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:53.651
power of our infrastructure.

00:35:53.651 --> 00:35:55.653
So again, we've taken
something you use,

00:35:55.653 --> 00:35:57.655
which is pretty good by the way,
SQL, been around for 50 years,

00:35:57.655 --> 00:35:59.657
roughly, invented
in roughly 1970.

00:35:59.657 --> 00:36:01.659
Works very well
for what it does.

00:36:01.659 --> 00:36:03.661
Use it, but use it
in the right way.

00:36:03.661 --> 00:36:05.663
And then you get all the
benefit of the architecture.

00:36:05.663 --> 00:36:06.664
We've extracted that.

00:36:06.664 --> 00:36:07.665
Try cause people.

00:36:07.665 --> 00:36:08.666
Right?

00:36:08.666 --> 00:36:10.668
See if you can get
the power of that,

00:36:10.668 --> 00:36:12.670
and watch the space
for more from us.

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:13.671
What about data warehousing?

00:36:13.671 --> 00:36:15.673
I think analytics should cost
less than other transactions,

00:36:15.673 --> 00:36:16.674
right?

00:36:16.674 --> 00:36:18.676
Try BigQuery, as Diane
mentioned, right?

00:36:18.676 --> 00:36:20.645
There is no noncloud
equivalent of BigQuery.

00:36:20.645 --> 00:36:21.646
Storage.

00:36:21.646 --> 00:36:23.648
Start by deleting nothing
unless you're forced to, right?

00:36:23.648 --> 00:36:24.649
It's so cheap.

00:36:24.649 --> 00:36:26.651
I estimate that storage is
now a cent a gigabyte a month.

00:36:26.651 --> 00:36:28.853
By the way, how did this happen?

00:36:28.853 --> 00:36:30.855
The physicists have
been busy, right,

00:36:30.855 --> 00:36:32.857
making discs that
are extraordinary.

00:36:32.857 --> 00:36:34.926
Use Google Cloud
storage and those APIs.

00:36:34.926 --> 00:36:35.927
Data processing.

00:36:35.927 --> 00:36:38.129
As Diane mentioned, we have
something called dataflow.

00:36:38.129 --> 00:36:40.131
Dataflow is the natural
successor to MapReduce, right?

00:36:40.131 --> 00:36:44.635
Because it allows -- instead
of sort of back and forth,

00:36:44.635 --> 00:36:50.575
which is the way to
think of MapReduce,

00:36:50.575 --> 00:36:55.580
dataflow is more of
an updating process;

00:36:57.081 --> 00:37:01.018
it keeps you constantly,
constantly on,

00:37:01.018 --> 00:37:06.023
and constantly -- especially
for cloud services,

00:37:12.263 --> 00:37:17.268
it's the only way, and then
finally I mentioned before use

00:37:25.042 --> 00:37:26.043
GRPC.

00:37:26.043 --> 00:37:27.044
Right.

00:37:27.044 --> 00:37:29.714
Use a proper RPC mechanism and
do it in such a way that the

00:37:29.714 --> 00:37:30.915
infrastructure supports it.

00:37:32.917 --> 00:37:38.356
Why is it important
now to talk about this?

00:37:38.356 --> 00:37:42.760
Because the industry
builds these platforms,

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:44.762
and another platform
and another platform,

00:37:44.762 --> 00:37:48.132
and then have you this enormous
amount of code math that has to

00:37:48.132 --> 00:37:50.101
be brought into it.

00:37:50.101 --> 00:37:53.337
I'm now convinced that we've
figured out a way to meet you

00:37:53.337 --> 00:37:55.873
where you are, right?

00:37:55.873 --> 00:38:00.044
And we're investing as a
corporation thousands of people

00:38:00.044 --> 00:38:03.314
enormous amounts of money over
the next few years to build the

00:38:03.314 --> 00:38:07.251
infrastructure and the partners
to bring you wherever you are

00:38:07.251 --> 00:38:10.254
into that model.

00:38:10.254 --> 00:38:13.791
But there's another phenomena,
so that's getting to the cloud,

00:38:13.791 --> 00:38:14.792
right?

00:38:14.792 --> 00:38:16.794
Getting to -- You
can imagine the song,

00:38:16.794 --> 00:38:20.765
get to the cloud
as fast as you can.

00:38:20.765 --> 00:38:23.668
But there's another thing,
which is this layer above.

00:38:23.668 --> 00:38:26.837
And I've become convinced that
there's a new architecture

00:38:26.837 --> 00:38:27.838
emerging.

00:38:27.838 --> 00:38:29.507
I'll describe it like this.

00:38:29.507 --> 00:38:36.247
What happens -- I spent a lot of
time talking about innovation.

00:38:36.247 --> 00:38:38.249
It's what I care a lot about,
and I talked to a lot of people

00:38:38.249 --> 00:38:39.950
doing new things.

00:38:39.950 --> 00:38:43.487
Those of you that are doing
startups and new models that are

00:38:43.487 --> 00:38:46.090
not dealing with some of the
infrastructure and significant

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:49.393
issues that are the majority
of our focus are doing the

00:38:49.393 --> 00:38:50.561
follow-on.

00:38:50.561 --> 00:38:52.763
If you're building hardware,
you're building in a rapid

00:38:52.763 --> 00:38:53.764
evaluation model.

00:38:53.764 --> 00:38:55.166
You're iterating every week.

00:38:55.166 --> 00:38:57.802
Didn't used to be
possible to do that.

00:38:57.802 --> 00:38:59.804
When you're building
knowledge systems,

00:38:59.804 --> 00:39:03.107
you're using crowd sourcing
to get the data that you need.

00:39:03.107 --> 00:39:05.643
I use the Google
photos as an example.

00:39:05.643 --> 00:39:07.645
And you're putting
into the Google Cloud,

00:39:07.645 --> 00:39:11.015
using Google Analytics to do
some kind of very interesting

00:39:11.015 --> 00:39:15.653
data analysis, and then
finally, in the next year,

00:39:15.653 --> 00:39:18.489
you're going to use machine
learning to take that data and

00:39:18.489 --> 00:39:22.593
do something that's better
than what the humans are doing.

00:39:22.593 --> 00:39:25.696
Literally, you're going to take
human crowd source data and

00:39:25.696 --> 00:39:27.698
you're going to
compute something new.

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:29.867
You're going to
discover something new.

00:39:29.867 --> 00:39:34.872
And I'm convinced that little
model I described, rapid eval,

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:37.675
odd computing, using
Google Cloud Platform,

00:39:37.675 --> 00:39:41.045
machine learning and crowd
sourcing the data will be the

00:39:41.045 --> 00:39:47.184
basis and fundamentals of every
successful huge IPO win in five

00:39:47.184 --> 00:39:48.185
years.

00:39:48.185 --> 00:39:51.455
In the same sense that the
transition to apps five years

00:39:51.455 --> 00:39:53.858
ago, which all of
us participated in,

00:39:53.858 --> 00:39:58.996
created the modern corporations
of Uber, SnapChat, and others.

00:39:58.996 --> 00:40:02.666
So it's not just that we
meet you where you are,

00:40:02.666 --> 00:40:05.236
and it's not just that
you're taking the cloud,

00:40:05.236 --> 00:40:09.273
the noncloud data and putting
it into the cloud in these

00:40:09.273 --> 00:40:12.176
complicated ways which will make
things get a higher level of

00:40:12.176 --> 00:40:14.945
traction, but there's
one more thing,

00:40:14.945 --> 00:40:18.783
and that one more thing is the
invention of the use of crowd

00:40:18.783 --> 00:40:21.185
sourcing and machine
intelligence and rapid

00:40:21.185 --> 00:40:27.224
evaluation to create huge new
platforms, companies, IPOs,

00:40:27.224 --> 00:40:30.694
wealth, and enormous things
going on in the future.

00:40:30.694 --> 00:40:32.997
It's a great time
to be in the cloud.

00:40:32.997 --> 00:40:34.565
It's a great time to be here.

00:40:34.565 --> 00:40:36.567
Thank you, Diane,
for inviting me.

00:40:36.567 --> 00:40:38.569
And I look forward to
seeing you all soon.

00:40:38.569 --> 00:40:44.608
Thank you very much.

00:40:44.608 --> 00:40:45.609
[ Applause ]

00:40:45.609 --> 00:40:48.312
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome Senior Vice President

00:40:48.312 --> 00:40:52.616
Technical Infrastructure
Urs Holzle.

00:40:54.785 --> 00:40:59.957
&gt;&gt; Urs: Thank you, Eric,
and good morning, everyone,

00:40:59.957 --> 00:41:03.727
and welcome to NEXT
in San Francisco.

00:41:03.727 --> 00:41:08.132
Since I started at
Google 17 years ago,

00:41:08.132 --> 00:41:10.534
we built a whole lot
of infrastructure,

00:41:10.534 --> 00:41:14.805
lots of data centers in 14
locations around the world,

00:41:14.805 --> 00:41:19.510
and our goal when we started,
which is the same goal today,

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:22.112
was to build infrastructure
that makes it possible to build

00:41:22.112 --> 00:41:26.517
amazing applications
and services.

00:41:26.517 --> 00:41:29.220
So whether we were
indexing the entire Web,

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.857
or building a 3-D model of the
entire world down to the street

00:41:32.857 --> 00:41:37.261
level, or predicting what users
are going to type next in a

00:41:37.261 --> 00:41:41.832
search box, the product
ambitions have forced us to

00:41:41.832 --> 00:41:47.304
rethink pretty much everything
in infrastructure; hardware,

00:41:47.304 --> 00:41:50.407
software, and even the
way we work together.

00:41:50.407 --> 00:41:54.478
And so this continues pressure
from product innovation,

00:41:54.478 --> 00:41:57.214
and to stay ahead of that
product innovation has been

00:41:57.214 --> 00:42:01.785
driving what's next for
cloud, namely the Google Cloud

00:42:01.785 --> 00:42:04.221
Platform, GCP.

00:42:04.221 --> 00:42:09.393
So today I'm going to talk
about three things: First GCP,

00:42:09.393 --> 00:42:14.632
what it is now, how we built
it, and what's coming up.

00:42:14.632 --> 00:42:19.436
Second, what's just over the
horizon, so what's coming next,

00:42:19.436 --> 00:42:25.843
so that you can coordinate what
you're going to do next with us.

00:42:25.843 --> 00:42:29.880
And then finally, some of the
companies that are building on

00:42:29.880 --> 00:42:33.517
GCP, and I realize that
includes a lot of you,

00:42:33.517 --> 00:42:36.720
so thank you for your business
and thank you for partnering

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:37.721
with us.

00:42:37.721 --> 00:42:42.426
[ Applause ]

00:42:42.426 --> 00:42:46.897
So before I talk about
that, about what's next,

00:42:46.897 --> 00:42:51.268
I want to share quickly some
of the over 300 improvements we

00:42:51.268 --> 00:42:53.604
made, just in the
last 12 months,

00:42:53.604 --> 00:42:56.106
so you can see the pace
of our development.

00:42:56.106 --> 00:43:00.177
Together we built a highly
differentiated platform that has

00:43:00.177 --> 00:43:02.680
core infrastructure,
development tools,

00:43:02.680 --> 00:43:07.084
and premium data services,
all built into it.

00:43:07.084 --> 00:43:09.887
There's so many highlights,
and I can pick just a few,

00:43:09.887 --> 00:43:10.888
but here we go.

00:43:10.888 --> 00:43:13.457
Custom machines, Diane
already mentioned,

00:43:13.457 --> 00:43:18.996
they let you choose any virtual
size machine and pay for just

00:43:18.996 --> 00:43:20.030
what you need.

00:43:20.030 --> 00:43:22.199
So there's no more
routine overprovisioning,

00:43:22.199 --> 00:43:27.471
and the users who are using it,
our users are saving on average

00:43:27.471 --> 00:43:30.941
19% off of their VM bill.

00:43:30.941 --> 00:43:37.281
Dataproc is a fully-managed
Hadoop and Spark cluster,

00:43:37.281 --> 00:43:41.752
lets you spin them up in 90
seconds, resize them up or down,

00:43:41.752 --> 00:43:44.088
and you only pay by the minute.

00:43:44.088 --> 00:43:47.591
And even better
dataflow, cloud dataflow,

00:43:47.591 --> 00:43:51.295
lets you build pipelines,
you write the code once,

00:43:51.295 --> 00:43:54.631
and you get both the streaming
and the batch analytics

00:43:54.631 --> 00:43:58.168
pipeline, and you don't even
have to worry about building the

00:43:58.168 --> 00:43:59.169
cluster.

00:43:59.169 --> 00:44:03.207
Or about how to correctly handle
missing or late datapoints.

00:44:03.207 --> 00:44:06.043
The system does that for you.

00:44:06.043 --> 00:44:10.547
Archival storage has
traditionally meant trading

00:44:10.547 --> 00:44:12.883
money for access time.

00:44:12.883 --> 00:44:15.552
So you have to wait hours
to get your data back.

00:44:15.552 --> 00:44:16.553
Well, no more.

00:44:16.553 --> 00:44:20.591
We launched Nearline storage
that combines the price of tape

00:44:20.591 --> 00:44:24.962
with nearly the speed of discs,
and it's using the same APIs

00:44:24.962 --> 00:44:27.297
than regular storage, so you
don't have to change your

00:44:27.297 --> 00:44:31.235
application after you've
archived your data.

00:44:31.235 --> 00:44:33.604
So no more hours of waiting.

00:44:33.604 --> 00:44:34.905
It's just seconds away.

00:44:34.905 --> 00:44:41.512
VM startup times are down to
about 43 seconds on average and

00:44:41.512 --> 00:44:42.746
falling further.

00:44:42.746 --> 00:44:47.985
And even better, you can get
more than 100 VMs in our cloud

00:44:47.985 --> 00:44:51.055
in about the time that it takes
you to get one VM in other

00:44:51.055 --> 00:44:52.189
clouds.

00:44:52.189 --> 00:44:56.260
That's the difference between
driving and flying -- or

00:44:56.260 --> 00:44:58.896
actually it's the difference
between biking and flying.

00:44:58.896 --> 00:45:03.801
High performance VM networking.

00:45:03.801 --> 00:45:09.306
You can get up to two gigabytes
per second throughput to other

00:45:09.306 --> 00:45:13.243
VMs or to storage on
every VM at Google.

00:45:13.243 --> 00:45:19.349
So even data intensive
applications are never going to

00:45:19.349 --> 00:45:25.789
be starved for data, and you get
that feature on Google in every

00:45:25.789 --> 00:45:27.791
VM at no extra cost.

00:45:27.791 --> 00:45:31.695
No other cloud offers such
high performance networking.

00:45:31.695 --> 00:45:34.131
So you get networking.

00:45:34.131 --> 00:45:37.868
As Diane mentioned, you can
connect directly to our network

00:45:37.868 --> 00:45:40.137
in more locations than
in any other cloud,

00:45:40.137 --> 00:45:44.575
and on that network, you get
network function as fully

00:45:44.575 --> 00:45:49.513
managed services that are
easy-to-use, but very powerful.

00:45:49.513 --> 00:45:54.017
So, for example, our load
balancer lets you balance across

00:45:54.017 --> 00:45:59.923
any region in the world, and yet
it spins up from zero traffic to

00:45:59.923 --> 00:46:04.094
million requests per
second instantly.

00:46:04.094 --> 00:46:07.564
So in just the past year, we've
enhanced our platform with over

00:46:07.564 --> 00:46:11.135
300 launches and that doesn't
even include what you're going

00:46:11.135 --> 00:46:13.804
to hear about today.

00:46:13.804 --> 00:46:17.908
And of course, we are still
the price performance leader.

00:46:17.908 --> 00:46:22.246
Some of our customers see over
50% savings versus other clouds,

00:46:22.246 --> 00:46:26.116
because we allow you to pay for
only what you need when you need

00:46:26.116 --> 00:46:27.184
it.

00:46:27.184 --> 00:46:31.722
And that makes it a lot easier
for you to work with us.

00:46:31.722 --> 00:46:35.259
And it's not just something
that is nice to have.

00:46:35.259 --> 00:46:39.163
If you have a job that only
runs for a few minutes,

00:46:39.163 --> 00:46:42.733
but you have to pay for an hour,
or if you have a job that needs

00:46:42.733 --> 00:46:46.370
20 gigabytes, but you
have to pay for 32,

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:48.772
or if you have a
bunch of unused VMs,

00:46:48.772 --> 00:46:51.775
but you signed a contract
for them a few months ago,

00:46:51.775 --> 00:46:55.679
so right there there's some
serious savings that you can get

00:46:55.679 --> 00:46:56.914
when you're on GCP.

00:46:56.914 --> 00:47:03.153
But cost isn't the only thing.

00:47:03.153 --> 00:47:06.023
When you're thinking about
picking a cloud provider for the

00:47:06.023 --> 00:47:11.261
next decade, innovation and
the quality of the underlying

00:47:11.261 --> 00:47:14.064
infrastructure is
just as important.

00:47:14.064 --> 00:47:17.968
In fact, if you're picking that
cloud provider for a decade,

00:47:17.968 --> 00:47:21.004
innovation might actually
be the most important part,

00:47:21.004 --> 00:47:26.443
so it's fine to
look at what's next.

00:47:26.443 --> 00:47:30.714
Over the next five years, I
think we will see more change in

00:47:30.714 --> 00:47:34.518
computing and in clouds than
we've seen in the last decade or

00:47:34.518 --> 00:47:36.153
two.

00:47:36.153 --> 00:47:39.356
Literally, an explosion
of innovation.

00:47:39.356 --> 00:47:40.991
Now, why am I saying that?

00:47:40.991 --> 00:47:44.928
I'm basing this prediction on a
well-known effect in biological

00:47:44.928 --> 00:47:45.929
systems.

00:47:45.929 --> 00:47:50.500
There, once the basic
capabilities come together and

00:47:50.500 --> 00:47:54.938
start to function well,
they enable the evolution to

00:47:54.938 --> 00:47:56.406
accelerate.

00:47:56.406 --> 00:48:00.310
And we're entering a similarly
explosive period in cloud

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:02.346
computing innovation right now.

00:48:04.548 --> 00:48:08.452
And as software gets woven into
our daily lives and into your

00:48:08.452 --> 00:48:13.757
company, you all face the same
challenge that Google has faced

00:48:13.757 --> 00:48:17.594
for many years, mainly how to
rapidly create more functional

00:48:17.594 --> 00:48:19.796
applications using
these applications,

00:48:19.796 --> 00:48:24.801
operate them at scale,
cheaply, efficiently, securely.

00:48:26.703 --> 00:48:30.073
Or in short, how to create
better software faster.

00:48:32.309 --> 00:48:37.247
And the user expectations on
that software are ratcheting up

00:48:37.247 --> 00:48:41.952
rapidly, so today applications
are constantly being improved

00:48:41.952 --> 00:48:46.056
with new features, and as users,
we're expecting the application

00:48:46.056 --> 00:48:50.027
to tailor its behavior to
us the first time we use it.

00:48:50.027 --> 00:48:54.931
And good apps delight us
by predicting what we want.

00:48:54.931 --> 00:48:57.367
So for that to happen
in every application,

00:48:57.367 --> 00:49:02.306
we have to have very easy-to-use
analytics and machine learning

00:49:02.306 --> 00:49:07.044
frameworks that everyone can
embed in their applications.

00:49:07.044 --> 00:49:08.211
So that's the first client.

00:49:08.211 --> 00:49:10.981
We've got to do better
software faster.

00:49:10.981 --> 00:49:15.185
Second, NoOps application
development will become

00:49:15.185 --> 00:49:19.523
mainstream, because developers
spend way too much time

00:49:19.523 --> 00:49:22.626
administering their setups.

00:49:22.626 --> 00:49:27.931
So next it will be easy to run
even ambitious applications at

00:49:27.931 --> 00:49:30.901
scale without knowing
all the details,

00:49:30.901 --> 00:49:35.372
so that's the faster part
of better software faster.

00:49:35.372 --> 00:49:40.777
To do that, we have to automate
most of the work provisioning

00:49:40.777 --> 00:49:46.350
the running of services, and
with App Engine, dataflow,

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:53.256
Kubernetes, we're already
well on the way to that call.

00:49:53.256 --> 00:49:56.727
Last but not least, you know,
Diane mentioned that security

00:49:56.727 --> 00:50:01.665
has become more challenging
as attack surfaces continue to

00:50:01.665 --> 00:50:03.133
multiply.

00:50:03.133 --> 00:50:07.637
Very few companies
today are truly secure.

00:50:07.637 --> 00:50:09.106
Very, very few.

00:50:09.106 --> 00:50:13.243
And a significant portion of
security vulnerabilities are

00:50:13.243 --> 00:50:19.249
created by process
vulnerabilities and by human

00:50:19.249 --> 00:50:20.384
intervention.

00:50:20.384 --> 00:50:24.755
So a lot of the key of getting
ahead of the security challenge

00:50:24.755 --> 00:50:27.457
is to automate
security at scale.

00:50:27.457 --> 00:50:33.830
Now, today, security is
often focused on securing the

00:50:33.830 --> 00:50:37.134
perimeter, but next,
you know, tomorrow,

00:50:37.134 --> 00:50:42.806
we need systems for security
and not band-aids or topical

00:50:42.806 --> 00:50:45.475
antiseptics at the skin.

00:50:45.475 --> 00:50:50.313
And so security is very deeply
embedded in GCP because we have

00:50:50.313 --> 00:50:53.784
invested very heavily in
it, not just for clouds,

00:50:53.784 --> 00:50:57.020
they're also for all
other Google products.

00:50:57.020 --> 00:51:00.223
For example, we were the
first to offer two factor

00:51:00.223 --> 00:51:02.526
authentication at scale.

00:51:02.526 --> 00:51:06.062
The first to provide perfect
forward security in essence,

00:51:06.062 --> 00:51:08.298
and elliptic curve cryptography.

00:51:08.298 --> 00:51:13.503
Last December we announced data
loss prevention for Google Apps,

00:51:13.503 --> 00:51:18.275
data rest and GCP is always
encrypted by default,

00:51:18.275 --> 00:51:21.111
because we don't think you
should be forced to choose

00:51:21.111 --> 00:51:26.783
between security and performance
or security and cost.

00:51:26.783 --> 00:51:30.153
And we practice
security in depth.

00:51:30.153 --> 00:51:34.057
Our team has discovered heartbleed to name just last

00:51:34.057 --> 00:51:39.629
year or rowhammer exploits, and these investments are not slowing down.

00:51:39.629 --> 00:51:43.967
And it's not just about security, but it's also about

00:51:43.967 --> 00:51:47.170
privacy and compliance.

00:51:47.170 --> 00:51:52.175
Every Google product needs to
meet rigorous standards and --

00:51:52.175 --> 00:51:55.579
for privacy and compliance, and
our customers and our regulators

00:51:55.579 --> 00:51:59.249
expect third party verification
of these standards.

00:51:59.249 --> 00:52:03.720
And us, we regularly undergo
third party audits that verified

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:06.590
that all of our controls are
present in our data centers,

00:52:06.590 --> 00:52:10.227
in our systems,
in our operations.

00:52:12.762 --> 00:52:17.400
Now, with GCP, we're really
after building a different

00:52:17.400 --> 00:52:20.303
cloud, and to
better explain this,

00:52:20.303 --> 00:52:23.807
let me review where the
word "cloud" comes from.

00:52:23.807 --> 00:52:27.010
The first wave of cloud started
about 20 years ago with the

00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:29.012
emergence of co-located hosting.

00:52:29.012 --> 00:52:34.417
Co-located hosting location gave
you the ability to rent space

00:52:34.417 --> 00:52:40.123
rather than to build it, and
the status quo of cloud today

00:52:40.123 --> 00:52:45.128
actually shares a lot with those
private inventors and co-located

00:52:45.128 --> 00:52:47.831
data centers decades ago.

00:52:47.831 --> 00:52:52.369
The components of today's
virtual data centers match the

00:52:52.369 --> 00:52:55.205
physical building blocks
of hosted computing.

00:52:55.205 --> 00:52:57.807
You have servers, you have
discs, you have load balancers,

00:52:57.807 --> 00:53:02.445
you have CPUs, and so on, except
the virtual devices today,

00:53:02.445 --> 00:53:07.384
and so what had been a purchase
order now today is an API call,

00:53:07.384 --> 00:53:11.388
and that's a very profound
change that has benefited many,

00:53:11.388 --> 00:53:12.389
many customers.

00:53:12.389 --> 00:53:17.160
But the basic building blocks of
those virtual data centers have

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:22.299
not changed yet, and it's still
extraordinarily complex to

00:53:22.299 --> 00:53:26.202
orchestrate these building
blocks together at scale,

00:53:26.202 --> 00:53:30.173
because this model forces
developers to still think much

00:53:30.173 --> 00:53:33.543
of their time thinking about
other things other than making

00:53:33.543 --> 00:53:34.978
their application great.

00:53:34.978 --> 00:53:36.646
So do we have enough servers?

00:53:36.646 --> 00:53:38.081
Are they all patched?

00:53:38.081 --> 00:53:40.884
Did we prepurchase too
much capacity, right?

00:53:40.884 --> 00:53:44.888
In the virtualized data center
world, this never gets easier.

00:53:44.888 --> 00:53:49.893
And actually it's a bit crazy
that today's cloud is based on a

00:53:51.962 --> 00:53:57.033
physical model with all the
limitations that was created 20

00:53:57.033 --> 00:53:58.034
years ago, right?

00:53:58.034 --> 00:54:01.104
It's kind of like the virtual
data center of today still has a

00:54:01.104 --> 00:54:02.872
manual choke, right?

00:54:02.872 --> 00:54:04.574
Kind of doesn't make sense.

00:54:04.574 --> 00:54:09.713
And but, you know, I'm a little
bit embarrassed to admit that

00:54:09.713 --> 00:54:14.651
actually we used to run
Google exactly that way,

00:54:14.651 --> 00:54:16.052
the way I just described.

00:54:16.052 --> 00:54:19.522
Until about ten years ago, our
world internally looked pretty

00:54:19.522 --> 00:54:20.757
much like that.

00:54:20.757 --> 00:54:24.794
But then we realized that we
couldn't really keep up with the

00:54:24.794 --> 00:54:28.198
rate of change
that Google needed,

00:54:28.198 --> 00:54:30.600
and so we switched
to a new model,

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:33.903
distracts away all of those
servers and is based on

00:54:33.903 --> 00:54:36.773
containers, not machines.

00:54:36.773 --> 00:54:40.744
So today we use a third
wave cloud, if you want,

00:54:40.744 --> 00:54:44.381
the combination of automated
services and scalable data,

00:54:44.381 --> 00:54:48.485
and we call this
approach "serverless",

00:54:48.485 --> 00:54:52.255
because you care about your
code not -- on your application,

00:54:52.255 --> 00:54:53.556
not your servers.

00:54:53.556 --> 00:54:58.261
So while the industry is just
starting to talk about that,

00:54:58.261 --> 00:55:02.666
serverless architecture is very
deeply embedded in all of GCP.

00:55:02.666 --> 00:55:06.269
So in App Engine, for example,
App Engine lets you run code

00:55:06.269 --> 00:55:10.373
snippets, large or small,
without specifying where they

00:55:10.373 --> 00:55:14.511
run, and your application
scales all the way down to zero,

00:55:14.511 --> 00:55:18.148
so if nobody is using your
application, you don't pay.

00:55:18.148 --> 00:55:21.484
In GKE, you can run containers
without specifying the machines

00:55:21.484 --> 00:55:22.485
they run on.

00:55:22.485 --> 00:55:26.056
In dataproc, you can create
Hadoop clusters without actually

00:55:26.056 --> 00:55:27.924
worrying about the cluster.

00:55:27.924 --> 00:55:30.293
In dataflow embedder, you don't
even need to worry about the

00:55:30.293 --> 00:55:31.294
cluster.

00:55:31.294 --> 00:55:33.830
You don't even need to
package container images.

00:55:33.830 --> 00:55:36.766
You just give us your
code and it runs.

00:55:36.766 --> 00:55:39.969
So the code just runs,
and as a developer,

00:55:39.969 --> 00:55:43.506
you never manage servers.

00:55:43.506 --> 00:55:47.143
So that's the true meaning
of third wave cloud.

00:55:47.143 --> 00:55:50.647
And GCP has the most integrated
approach to that serverless

00:55:50.647 --> 00:55:54.551
architecture, so you can start
taking advantage of it without

00:55:54.551 --> 00:55:58.455
throwing away your
existing code.

00:55:58.455 --> 00:56:00.523
So now, to give you
the details about that,

00:56:00.523 --> 00:56:03.827
it's my pleasure to introduce
Brian Stevens who heads all of

00:56:03.827 --> 00:56:05.161
our product management.

00:56:05.161 --> 00:56:06.162
Welcome, Brian.

00:56:06.162 --> 00:56:07.931
[ Music ]

00:56:10.200 --> 00:56:12.469
&gt;&gt; Brian: Thanks, Urs.

00:56:12.469 --> 00:56:17.474
So as Urs was saying, this next
generation of cloud is already

00:56:21.111 --> 00:56:22.112
happening.

00:56:22.112 --> 00:56:24.414
It's already bringing out
services that are freeing users

00:56:24.414 --> 00:56:27.884
from thinking about virtual
machines and storage and blocks

00:56:27.884 --> 00:56:28.885
on discs.

00:56:28.885 --> 00:56:30.887
So instead they're starting
to focus more on their

00:56:30.887 --> 00:56:33.623
applications, their users
and then just building their

00:56:33.623 --> 00:56:34.624
business.

00:56:34.624 --> 00:56:37.527
And we have many customers that
are actually coming to GCP today

00:56:37.527 --> 00:56:38.928
for exactly that reason.

00:56:38.928 --> 00:56:40.930
So instead of filling
with infrastructure,

00:56:40.930 --> 00:56:43.733
they're using full zerop
services like App Engine and

00:56:43.733 --> 00:56:46.102
BigQuery, so I want to get
started really quickly and

00:56:46.102 --> 00:56:48.104
introduce you to one
of them right now.

00:56:51.975 --> 00:56:54.544
&gt;&gt; Video: We wanted to make this
marketing campaign for Coca-Cola

00:56:54.544 --> 00:56:56.713
something that every single
person in this world,

00:56:56.713 --> 00:56:59.249
no matter who you are, could
participate in the World Cup

00:56:59.249 --> 00:57:00.416
experience.

00:57:00.416 --> 00:57:02.986
Submit your photo, you could
be on the pitch at the opening

00:57:02.986 --> 00:57:04.854
ceremony of the World Cup.

00:57:04.854 --> 00:57:09.192
&gt;&gt; We're talking about 3 million
and a half images from over 200

00:57:09.192 --> 00:57:11.961
countries, different
formats, different users,

00:57:11.961 --> 00:57:12.996
different sources.

00:57:12.996 --> 00:57:15.598
&gt;&gt; Fans could actually find
their photos to know, okay,

00:57:15.598 --> 00:57:19.202
I was here on the flag in
the opening of the World Cup.

00:57:19.202 --> 00:57:21.604
&gt;&gt; It was a gigantic scale.

00:57:21.604 --> 00:57:25.241
The agency that was creating the
photomosaic printed in fabric,

00:57:25.241 --> 00:57:27.977
we recreated the
equivalent digitally.

00:57:27.977 --> 00:57:31.714
We did not have time to set up
an infrastructure and set up the

00:57:31.714 --> 00:57:33.683
servers and configure
the machines.

00:57:33.683 --> 00:57:35.685
By using the Google
Cloud Platform,

00:57:35.685 --> 00:57:38.188
we sent straight into
building the top of it.

00:57:38.188 --> 00:57:40.957
Without that my first
deliverable would be impossible.

00:57:40.957 --> 00:57:44.327
&gt;&gt; You have to predict what
the loads are going to be.

00:57:44.327 --> 00:57:45.495
There's a lot of uncertainty.

00:57:45.495 --> 00:57:47.230
When you look at a cloud
provider like Google,

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:49.232
the opportunity there
is for autoscaling.

00:57:49.232 --> 00:57:51.267
You don't have to worry
about disaster recovery,

00:57:51.267 --> 00:57:52.635
you don't have to
worry about backup,

00:57:52.635 --> 00:57:54.704
because it's provided
to you as a service.

00:57:54.704 --> 00:57:59.342
&gt;&gt; We wanted to send every one
that had an image in that flag a

00:57:59.342 --> 00:58:03.646
notification the very moment the
flag was opened physically in

00:58:03.646 --> 00:58:04.647
the field.

00:58:04.647 --> 00:58:07.016
We had a lot of people
doing that simultaneously.

00:58:07.016 --> 00:58:08.852
They're not taking
turns to use it,

00:58:08.852 --> 00:58:14.691
so we were monitoring the
transmission on TV and waiting.

00:58:14.691 --> 00:58:19.429
I remember clearly seeing the
spikes and just hoping please,

00:58:19.429 --> 00:58:23.533
please, scale up,
scale up, and it did.

00:58:23.533 --> 00:58:25.368
&gt;&gt; Fundamentally it gets down
to one thing and it's one word;

00:58:25.368 --> 00:58:26.502
it's trust.

00:58:26.502 --> 00:58:28.771
The same infrastructure that
powers the Google search engine

00:58:28.771 --> 00:58:31.474
and search experience is
what's powering the cloud that

00:58:31.474 --> 00:58:33.576
Coca-Cola is running on.

00:58:33.576 --> 00:58:36.613
&gt;&gt; The biggest digital
marketing initiative.

00:58:36.613 --> 00:58:40.016
&gt;&gt; To participate is like a
once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

00:58:45.955 --> 00:58:51.194
[ Applause ]

00:58:51.194 --> 00:58:53.630
&gt;&gt; So we're really lucky today
because we have the C.T.O.

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:55.265
of Coca-Cola here Alan Boehme.

00:58:57.133 --> 00:59:06.876
[ Music ]

00:59:06.876 --> 00:59:08.845
So Alan, I wanted to
ask you a few questions.

00:59:08.845 --> 00:59:11.681
So first of all, like how did
you actually decide to get

00:59:11.681 --> 00:59:13.917
started with Google
Cloud Platform?

00:59:13.917 --> 00:59:15.685
&gt;&gt; When you're
working for Coca-Cola,

00:59:15.685 --> 00:59:17.921
which is one of the largest
brands in the world,

00:59:17.921 --> 00:59:21.424
almost 2 billion servings a
day, and you have Google that is

00:59:21.424 --> 00:59:23.860
serving up over 3.5
billion searches a day,

00:59:23.860 --> 00:59:25.561
it's all about scale.

00:59:25.561 --> 00:59:28.431
And when you start talking about
putting something on the world

00:59:28.431 --> 00:59:32.335
stage for FIFA that's seen
in 207 different countries,

00:59:32.335 --> 00:59:34.871
every place in the
world is participating,

00:59:34.871 --> 00:59:37.674
you need to partner with
somebody that really understands

00:59:37.674 --> 00:59:39.442
scale, understands reliability.

00:59:39.442 --> 00:59:41.544
We can't miss,
because if we miss,

00:59:41.544 --> 00:59:43.913
it's a miss for
the entire world.

00:59:43.913 --> 00:59:45.915
&gt;&gt; So the video is
really compelling, right?

00:59:45.915 --> 00:59:47.083
You can get inspired by that.

00:59:47.083 --> 00:59:49.085
Can you tell us a little bit
more about some of the other

00:59:49.085 --> 00:59:50.553
ways that you're using GCP?

00:59:50.553 --> 00:59:53.623
&gt;&gt; So we've been advancing with
GCP and with Google in general

00:59:53.623 --> 00:59:54.624
for awhile now.

00:59:54.624 --> 00:59:56.826
We're moving into an
analytics platform.

00:59:56.826 --> 00:59:58.795
We're taking advantage of many
of the things that we heard

00:59:58.795 --> 00:59:59.796
earlier.

00:59:59.796 --> 01:00:01.798
We're moving forward
with machine learning.

01:00:01.798 --> 01:00:04.200
We're real excited about all the
new activities that are going on

01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:07.904
and the points of opportunity
that are made available to us.

01:00:07.904 --> 01:00:11.407
In addition, we have created
a relationship with Google.

01:00:11.407 --> 01:00:13.042
We've worked on security.

01:00:13.042 --> 01:00:15.678
We're a very big believer in
the software to find perimeter.

01:00:15.678 --> 01:00:18.314
Google has announced
their BeyondCorp strategy.

01:00:18.314 --> 01:00:21.284
We're partnering at a different
level that we have with many

01:00:21.284 --> 01:00:23.920
other companies in the past,
and it's to the benefit of us,

01:00:23.920 --> 01:00:25.722
our consumers and to everyone.

01:00:25.722 --> 01:00:29.625
&gt;&gt; So and from your team inside,
how is just using Cloud changed

01:00:29.625 --> 01:00:32.161
from how they do things
from five years ago?

01:00:32.161 --> 01:00:33.830
&gt;&gt; We're 130-year-old company.

01:00:33.830 --> 01:00:37.734
We're 130-year-old company.

01:00:37.734 --> 01:00:40.136
We have this culture in
IT that's been going on,

01:00:40.136 --> 01:00:43.172
not going to say 130 years,
but certainly for many,

01:00:43.172 --> 01:00:46.042
many generations, and its hard.

01:00:46.042 --> 01:00:48.044
Working with Google, working
with Google team and the

01:00:48.044 --> 01:00:50.046
platform is changing
the culture,

01:00:50.046 --> 01:00:52.048
changing the skill
sets that are required,

01:00:52.048 --> 01:00:54.217
and it's going to help make us
competitive going forward in the

01:00:54.217 --> 01:00:55.218
next century.

01:00:55.218 --> 01:00:56.219
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

01:00:56.219 --> 01:00:57.220
Well, thanks for joining us.

01:00:57.220 --> 01:01:02.225
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:01:08.765 --> 01:01:09.766
[ Applause ]

01:01:09.766 --> 01:01:11.768
&gt;&gt; Brian: So our data center,
our cloud is this really dynamic

01:01:11.768 --> 01:01:12.769
environment.

01:01:12.769 --> 01:01:13.770
Things are always changing.

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:15.772
We're constantly
improving hardware.

01:01:15.772 --> 01:01:16.773
We're improving software.

01:01:16.773 --> 01:01:18.775
And we even change things
how we do operationally,

01:01:18.775 --> 01:01:21.411
and you can't just shut down
the cloud when you're actually

01:01:21.411 --> 01:01:24.080
changing out servers and
upgrading and working on

01:01:24.080 --> 01:01:25.481
infrastructure.

01:01:25.481 --> 01:01:28.184
So we had to support the rate
of innovation that we need.

01:01:28.184 --> 01:01:31.421
We had to design agility
and change into both our

01:01:31.421 --> 01:01:34.123
architecture as well as our
operation proceedures from the

01:01:34.123 --> 01:01:35.691
beginning.

01:01:35.691 --> 01:01:38.528
And that's really the reason
why we're perhaps the only

01:01:38.528 --> 01:01:41.998
hyperscale cloud that actually
depends on live migration.

01:01:41.998 --> 01:01:46.302
And so that really allows us to
upgrade our infrastructure and

01:01:46.302 --> 01:01:48.805
our software stack and the
applications don't even know

01:01:48.805 --> 01:01:51.007
about it, because not all
applications are built in this

01:01:51.007 --> 01:01:53.676
microservice architecture where
they can -- pieces can be taken

01:01:53.676 --> 01:01:55.478
down.

01:01:55.478 --> 01:01:57.814
So now whether if we're
upgrading that server,

01:01:57.814 --> 01:02:00.616
if we're applying a patch
to a underlying host to us,

01:02:00.616 --> 01:02:03.119
we can even actually service the
power infrastructure underneath

01:02:03.119 --> 01:02:06.155
the whole cluster because we
have the spare capacity to live

01:02:06.155 --> 01:02:08.958
migrate the VMs, so applications
don't notice when they're

01:02:08.958 --> 01:02:09.959
running on GCP.

01:02:09.959 --> 01:02:14.263
And at the same time, GCP is
actually the greenest cloud in

01:02:14.263 --> 01:02:15.264
operation today.

01:02:15.264 --> 01:02:17.767
So today in the
average data center,

01:02:17.767 --> 01:02:21.604
about 40% of all power actually
goes for things other than

01:02:21.604 --> 01:02:22.605
servers.

01:02:22.605 --> 01:02:23.606
Right?

01:02:23.606 --> 01:02:24.640
And so that's just waste.

01:02:24.640 --> 01:02:27.543
And by comparison,
in our data centers,

01:02:27.543 --> 01:02:31.681
only about 10% of power goes for
anything other than that server.

01:02:31.681 --> 01:02:34.684
So you think about how
little overhead that is.

01:02:34.684 --> 01:02:37.186
And we actually continue
to get a lot better,

01:02:37.186 --> 01:02:40.890
so we now get three times the
amount of compute capacity out

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:43.526
of the same amount of power
as we did just five years ago.

01:02:43.526 --> 01:02:49.065
So as Diane was saying, to
join her and Urs on that trip,

01:02:49.065 --> 01:02:51.033
a very emotional experience.

01:02:51.033 --> 01:02:55.238
It was definitely the tenure
of my -- of my time here.

01:02:55.238 --> 01:02:59.542
And you really did expect to see
these football field size rooms

01:02:59.542 --> 01:03:01.744
full of servers, and
it was just that.

01:03:01.744 --> 01:03:04.147
It was really daunting, but it
was everything else that was

01:03:04.147 --> 01:03:06.149
around the server that
was really compelling.

01:03:06.149 --> 01:03:08.151
It was the power infrastructure.

01:03:08.151 --> 01:03:10.153
We were climbing on top of,
you know, water coolers,

01:03:10.153 --> 01:03:12.155
and just the lights
out management.

01:03:12.155 --> 01:03:14.690
Retinal eye scan like to get
inside the data center is really

01:03:14.690 --> 01:03:16.492
impressive.

01:03:16.492 --> 01:03:19.061
But one of the aha moments to
me was -- and I think, you know,

01:03:19.061 --> 01:03:21.664
everybody else at Google
takes this for granted,

01:03:21.664 --> 01:03:24.834
is when you leave this amazing
data center and you walk outside

01:03:24.834 --> 01:03:26.836
and the next one is
already being built,

01:03:26.836 --> 01:03:28.838
and you would think that
once you've, you know,

01:03:28.838 --> 01:03:31.207
delivered perfection you
would just keep cloning it,

01:03:31.207 --> 01:03:34.310
but the next data center looked
remarkably different than the

01:03:34.310 --> 01:03:35.678
one that you just left.

01:03:35.678 --> 01:03:38.347
The size of the floors, the
height of the ceilings were

01:03:38.347 --> 01:03:40.883
different, they were
going to four stories,

01:03:40.883 --> 01:03:43.519
and that just shows you the rate
of innovation that's happening

01:03:43.519 --> 01:03:45.054
in this area today.

01:03:45.054 --> 01:03:47.890
So if you didn't believe
that computing would become a

01:03:47.890 --> 01:03:52.628
utility, one visit to Iowa,
and it becomes really obvious.

01:03:52.628 --> 01:03:54.630
So we can't bring
you all to Iowa,

01:03:54.630 --> 01:03:57.266
but we actually prepared a
virtual reality tour for you

01:03:57.266 --> 01:04:00.703
today, so here is Greg Wilson,
he leads our developer app GCP

01:04:00.703 --> 01:04:01.704
team.

01:04:01.704 --> 01:04:06.542
[ Music ]

01:04:10.246 --> 01:04:12.615
&gt;&gt; Greg: Good morning.

01:04:12.615 --> 01:04:13.616
Thanks, Brian.

01:04:13.616 --> 01:04:17.587
So, as Brian was saying,
we went to a data center.

01:04:17.587 --> 01:04:19.922
A few months ago we -- our team
got together to figure out where

01:04:19.922 --> 01:04:22.558
we wanted to go for a team
retreat and we had a few

01:04:22.558 --> 01:04:23.559
