01:06:56.846 --> 01:06:57.847
soundtrack.

01:06:57.847 --> 01:06:59.849
You can meet Sandeep
who stars in the video.

01:06:59.849 --> 01:07:00.850
He's on our team.

01:07:00.850 --> 01:07:02.852
So I encourage you to come over
to the program look at that and

01:07:02.852 --> 01:07:05.087
some of our other experiences
and I hope you guys enjoy it.

01:07:05.087 --> 01:07:06.088
Thank you very much.

01:07:06.088 --> 01:07:09.258
[ Applause ]

01:07:11.460 --> 01:07:13.796
&gt;&gt; Brian: Thanks, Greg.

01:07:13.796 --> 01:07:16.565
So when you run on Google, your
traffic runs on the world's

01:07:16.565 --> 01:07:20.269
largest network backbone, right,
with direct connections to

01:07:20.269 --> 01:07:22.371
thousands of ISPs in
nearly every country.

01:07:22.371 --> 01:07:25.941
And as Urs was saying earlier,
we have 77 piering points that

01:07:25.941 --> 01:07:28.944
are in all the major metros,
and those are really important

01:07:28.944 --> 01:07:32.081
because that's actually where we
integrate directly piering with

01:07:32.081 --> 01:07:35.584
our network partners and most
recently our CDM partners and

01:07:35.584 --> 01:07:37.653
our largest customers.

01:07:37.653 --> 01:07:39.722
So speaking of that
global network,

01:07:39.722 --> 01:07:42.591
we're actually going to continue
building out regional support

01:07:42.591 --> 01:07:46.429
for a cloud platform, and
we actually just announced

01:07:46.429 --> 01:07:51.534
yesterday that we're adding two
new regions in Tokyo and Oregon.

01:07:51.534 --> 01:07:53.936
So Greg just got
to go see Oregon.

01:07:53.936 --> 01:07:55.971
[ Applause ]

01:07:55.971 --> 01:07:59.942
And that's really important,
but we're not done, right?

01:07:59.942 --> 01:08:03.546
Once we get the model
down, as we are with Tokyo,

01:08:03.546 --> 01:08:06.148
then we can go even faster.

01:08:06.148 --> 01:08:10.486
So we're actually going to
be adding ten more locations

01:08:10.486 --> 01:08:12.655
through the end of 2017.

01:08:12.755 --> 01:08:15.524
So on a hybrid called mini,
users are actually using more

01:08:15.524 --> 01:08:18.060
than one cloud, right, and that
might be because look at some of

01:08:18.060 --> 01:08:20.062
the services that
we're talking about.

01:08:20.062 --> 01:08:22.064
There's different services that
aren't available on all clouds,

01:08:22.064 --> 01:08:24.667
so they're using multiple
services that might be for --

01:08:24.667 --> 01:08:28.771
for risk or cost or other
things, or even archival.

01:08:28.771 --> 01:08:31.674
But the -- but the challenge
of that is is that all the

01:08:31.674 --> 01:08:34.710
providers, including On
Premise, Private Cloud,

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:37.213
we all build our own tools and
kind of put you in this little

01:08:37.213 --> 01:08:39.215
operational silo, right?

01:08:39.215 --> 01:08:41.217
And so we think that's,
you know, a real problem,

01:08:41.217 --> 01:08:43.953
and it just makes it harder
for users to consume multiple

01:08:43.953 --> 01:08:45.054
clouds.

01:08:45.054 --> 01:08:50.526
So Google Stackdriver is a
service for monitoring, logging,

01:08:50.526 --> 01:08:53.596
visualizing, and alerting,
across cloud services,

01:08:53.596 --> 01:08:57.266
across your resources,
across your applications.

01:08:57.266 --> 01:08:59.702
And so we wanted to get you
out of that sort of operational

01:08:59.702 --> 01:09:00.703
silo.

01:09:00.703 --> 01:09:03.038
So one of the things that we did
is we just announced beta for

01:09:03.038 --> 01:09:05.140
Google Stackdriver today,
and now it will actually be

01:09:05.140 --> 01:09:08.644
available and it will support
GCP as well as AWS with private

01:09:08.644 --> 01:09:09.645
cloud coming.

01:09:09.645 --> 01:09:12.882
[ Applause ]

01:09:12.882 --> 01:09:15.384
Thank you.

01:09:15.384 --> 01:09:18.354
So gives you that single pane
of glass that you would expect

01:09:18.354 --> 01:09:20.689
where you can, you know, issues
are sort of propagated really

01:09:20.689 --> 01:09:21.690
quickly.

01:09:21.690 --> 01:09:25.728
You can find them, visualize
them, and resolve them.

01:09:25.728 --> 01:09:29.498
Another challenge with -- with
hybrid cloud is the applications

01:09:29.498 --> 01:09:32.134
are just really married into
the underlying infrastructure.

01:09:32.134 --> 01:09:34.937
Whether it's the dependencies
they have on different operating

01:09:34.937 --> 01:09:37.006
systems at different
release levels,

01:09:37.006 --> 01:09:40.142
maybe they're building inside of
virtual machines and their app's

01:09:40.142 --> 01:09:44.813
trapped inside of VMDK or it's
up on a QEM UKVM image or it's

01:09:44.813 --> 01:09:47.550
on Amazon with AMI, so
applications really aren't that

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:50.486
lightweight and portable, and
that's a really powerful thing

01:09:50.486 --> 01:09:53.589
that Eric was saying earlier
about containers and namely

01:09:53.589 --> 01:09:54.590
Docker.

01:09:54.590 --> 01:09:56.592
It's really for the first
time giving this industry this

01:09:56.592 --> 01:09:58.594
portability at the
application level.

01:09:58.594 --> 01:10:00.596
And so when you
think about that,

01:10:00.596 --> 01:10:02.598
think about what that means
for hybrid cloud consumption,

01:10:02.598 --> 01:10:06.268
because now we're actually
helping enterprises consume

01:10:06.268 --> 01:10:09.204
containers and they're
getting more agile On Premise,

01:10:09.204 --> 01:10:12.575
at the same time they're
actually able to use support

01:10:12.575 --> 01:10:16.078
hybrid clouds, and so moreover
we really realize from our

01:10:16.078 --> 01:10:18.480
experience back in the Borg
that it's not just about the

01:10:18.480 --> 01:10:21.417
containerization, it's really
about the management of

01:10:21.417 --> 01:10:24.219
thousands of containers and how
do you deal with rollout of new

01:10:24.219 --> 01:10:27.222
applications, and that was
what Kubernetes was all about.

01:10:27.222 --> 01:10:29.291
Let's open source the
best of what we know,

01:10:29.291 --> 01:10:32.161
not just so that
hackers can have it,

01:10:32.161 --> 01:10:35.965
so that large enterprises can
consume orchestration at scale,

01:10:35.965 --> 01:10:39.068
and by -- by default be able
to support public cloud.

01:10:39.068 --> 01:10:42.204
So let me introduce now Greg,
and Greg is actually going to

01:10:42.204 --> 01:10:45.274
take us through everything
that we've been doing with both

01:10:45.274 --> 01:10:47.343
Google Stackdriver as
well as with containers.

01:10:47.343 --> 01:10:51.080
[ Music ]

01:10:53.582 --> 01:10:58.721
&gt;&gt; Greg: Thanks a lot, Brian.

01:10:58.721 --> 01:11:00.723
I'm really happy to be here
to show you today the next

01:11:00.723 --> 01:11:04.326
generation of monitoring and
logging with Google Stackdriver.

01:11:04.326 --> 01:11:07.229
Now, I cannot talk and
type at the same time,

01:11:07.229 --> 01:11:10.065
so I'm very grateful to have
Dan Belcher out on stage here to

01:11:10.065 --> 01:11:11.200
help me with this demo.

01:11:11.200 --> 01:11:15.270
Now, you heard a lot about
openness being a critical piece

01:11:15.270 --> 01:11:18.140
of everything we do at Google,
and it's really no different

01:11:18.140 --> 01:11:20.075
than monitoring and logging.

01:11:20.075 --> 01:11:23.245
So to see that in action, let's
take a look at this Stackdriver

01:11:23.245 --> 01:11:24.246
screen.

01:11:24.246 --> 01:11:26.248
I've got a Stackdriver
environment,

01:11:26.248 --> 01:11:28.250
and the first thing you'll
notice at the top is I'm

01:11:28.250 --> 01:11:30.753
monitoring multiple
GCP projects.

01:11:30.753 --> 01:11:33.522
That's a new capability,
and it's really important,

01:11:33.522 --> 01:11:35.991
because whether you're an
individual developer or you're

01:11:35.991 --> 01:11:38.794
an operational specialist,
you want to have a complete

01:11:38.794 --> 01:11:40.996
visibility across everything
that's going in your

01:11:40.996 --> 01:11:42.064
organization.

01:11:42.064 --> 01:11:44.833
You don't want your monitoring
silo'd into different parts of

01:11:44.833 --> 01:11:46.735
your organization.

01:11:46.735 --> 01:11:48.737
Down at the bottom, you'll
notice something that you

01:11:48.737 --> 01:11:50.739
probably might not
have expected to see,

01:11:50.739 --> 01:11:53.208
and that is I have a
set of AWS accounts,

01:11:53.208 --> 01:11:55.944
Stackdriver's design to
let you do monitoring,

01:11:55.944 --> 01:12:00.482
logging and alerting across
both Google Cloud and AWS.

01:12:00.482 --> 01:12:03.318
One pane of glass,
multiple clouds.

01:12:03.318 --> 01:12:05.487
So Dan is going to switch us
over to the main Stackdriver

01:12:05.487 --> 01:12:06.855
console.

01:12:06.855 --> 01:12:08.857
For this application
we're running here,

01:12:08.857 --> 01:12:10.959
this is a multi-cloud
application.

01:12:10.959 --> 01:12:16.432
I've got NGINX, and I'm sending
traffic to both AWS and GCP.

01:12:16.432 --> 01:12:18.434
So if you look
down at the bottom,

01:12:18.434 --> 01:12:20.436
you'll see some request graphs,
and you'll notice that we're

01:12:20.436 --> 01:12:24.873
currently sending about 90% of
the traffic to AWS and about 10%

01:12:24.873 --> 01:12:25.874
to Google.

01:12:25.874 --> 01:12:29.378
Now, I may be biased, but
that seems backwards to me.

01:12:29.378 --> 01:12:32.614
So I'm going to have Dan here
run a script in the background

01:12:32.614 --> 01:12:36.351
that switches that and starts
redistricting the traffic onto

01:12:36.351 --> 01:12:37.653
GCP.

01:12:37.653 --> 01:12:39.855
Now, that takes a little
bit of time to propagate,

01:12:39.855 --> 01:12:42.157
but that's okay, because it
gives me a few minutes to show

01:12:42.157 --> 01:12:44.727
you some other new
features of Stackdriver.

01:12:44.727 --> 01:12:47.229
The first thing I want to show
you is intelligent defaults.

01:12:47.229 --> 01:12:51.366
Dan is drilled into a page
specifically on our ELB

01:12:51.366 --> 01:12:53.936
monitoring, and you'll notice
I get a dashboard that's

01:12:53.936 --> 01:12:57.873
automatically showing me the
right events and metrics.

01:12:57.873 --> 01:13:01.543
It's not showing the useless
things like CPU load or memory

01:13:01.543 --> 01:13:02.544
load.

01:13:02.544 --> 01:13:07.049
It's showing me application
latency, 200 response codes.

01:13:07.049 --> 01:13:11.286
Same thing if I switch
over to the NGINX piece.

01:13:11.286 --> 01:13:14.790
I'm getting a set of charts
that are giving me intelligent

01:13:14.790 --> 01:13:15.791
defaults.

01:13:15.791 --> 01:13:18.794
Now, the key here is
what you're not seeing.

01:13:18.794 --> 01:13:23.465
Dan didn't have to go and tell
Stackdriver that ELB is a load

01:13:23.465 --> 01:13:26.068
balancer, and therefore
these are the right metrics.

01:13:26.068 --> 01:13:28.203
He didn't have to
tell it what NGINX is.

01:13:28.203 --> 01:13:32.040
Stackdriver has intelligent
defaults for a huge range of

01:13:32.040 --> 01:13:34.643
both commercial and
open source libraries,

01:13:34.643 --> 01:13:37.880
so you can just build your
application and you get the

01:13:37.880 --> 01:13:42.351
right dashboarding, the right
metrics right out of the box.

01:13:42.351 --> 01:13:45.254
You can also create
custom dashboards.

01:13:45.254 --> 01:13:47.756
In this case, maybe I'm
an operational specialist,

01:13:47.756 --> 01:13:50.859
and I don't want to drill down
into the specifics of a service.

01:13:50.859 --> 01:13:54.296
I want to aggregate all of my
network traffic across all my

01:13:54.296 --> 01:13:56.999
services into one pane of glass.

01:13:56.999 --> 01:13:59.434
And of course, we can -- you can
customize the user interface,

01:13:59.434 --> 01:14:00.435
the look and feel.

01:14:00.435 --> 01:14:04.773
Now, the next thing we want to
go to is what happens when there

01:14:04.773 --> 01:14:06.141
is a problem?

01:14:06.141 --> 01:14:08.210
Now, dashboards aren't enough.

01:14:08.210 --> 01:14:09.912
I need realtime information.

01:14:09.912 --> 01:14:12.714
I want to see what's
happening right this second.

01:14:12.714 --> 01:14:14.716
So we're going to
switch to logging,

01:14:14.716 --> 01:14:18.654
and that's another important
capability of Stackdriver.

01:14:18.654 --> 01:14:20.856
Dan is going to go ahead and hit
the play button and we're going

01:14:20.856 --> 01:14:24.193
to get playing in realtime
logging information,

01:14:24.193 --> 01:14:27.863
so as errors are logged
in either AWS or GCP,

01:14:27.863 --> 01:14:30.799
I'm getting a view
here of all of those.

01:14:30.799 --> 01:14:33.569
And in fact, there are a couple
of errors coming because we're

01:14:33.569 --> 01:14:37.806
doing the fail over from one
cloud provider to another.

01:14:37.806 --> 01:14:41.376
You can also create metrics
and alerts based on logs,

01:14:41.376 --> 01:14:44.580
so you can make sure that you
get an alert when certain error

01:14:44.580 --> 01:14:46.582
messages appear in
your log systems.

01:14:49.785 --> 01:14:51.820
But we're actually going to
leave that sort of for another

01:14:51.820 --> 01:14:52.821
day.

01:14:52.821 --> 01:14:54.823
I'm not going to actually
create one of those right now.

01:14:54.823 --> 01:14:57.726
If I go back to the
main monitoring console,

01:14:57.726 --> 01:15:01.997
we can see that -- here
we go, as the graph loads,

01:15:01.997 --> 01:15:05.701
we can see in fact the requests
on the AWS side are dropping,

01:15:05.701 --> 01:15:08.303
the requests on the
GCP side are coming up.

01:15:08.303 --> 01:15:09.638
I've got no alerts.

01:15:09.638 --> 01:15:11.640
No errors other
than that happening.

01:15:11.640 --> 01:15:13.609
Everything is fine.

01:15:13.609 --> 01:15:16.144
So today Stackdriver's
available in beta.

01:15:16.144 --> 01:15:19.648
It's a great monitoring solution
whether you're using AWS,

01:15:19.648 --> 01:15:22.317
whether you're using GCP
or whether you're using a

01:15:22.317 --> 01:15:23.719
combination.

01:15:23.719 --> 01:15:26.455
If you think about what it would
normally take to do what I just

01:15:26.455 --> 01:15:28.924
showed you, we'd have been
running through multiple

01:15:28.924 --> 01:15:30.926
different tools for
logging and monitoring.

01:15:30.926 --> 01:15:34.162
We would have one tool for
each of our cloud providers.

01:15:34.162 --> 01:15:35.664
All to do that.

01:15:35.664 --> 01:15:38.867
Stackdriver gives me
metrics, logs, dashboards,

01:15:38.867 --> 01:15:41.803
up-time monitoring
all in one system.

01:15:41.803 --> 01:15:45.007
It also includes tracing,
error reporting, debugging.

01:15:45.007 --> 01:15:47.042
We're going to have more
sessions on that throughout the

01:15:47.042 --> 01:15:47.809
conference.

01:15:47.809 --> 01:15:49.211
I encourage you to go to those.

01:15:49.211 --> 01:15:51.179
So that's monitoring
and logging.

01:15:51.179 --> 01:15:54.950
Now, let's turn to the business
of deploying and running

01:15:54.950 --> 01:15:56.418
applications.

01:15:56.418 --> 01:16:00.055
You've heard a lot about the
role of containers in this whole

01:16:00.055 --> 01:16:03.458
notion of a wave 3 cloud
application and how we know from

01:16:03.458 --> 01:16:06.561
our experience at Google
that they really are a key to

01:16:06.561 --> 01:16:08.630
managing systems at scale.

01:16:08.630 --> 01:16:11.733
This idea of moving from a world
of managing individual servers

01:16:11.733 --> 01:16:14.636
to managing applications
and services.

01:16:14.636 --> 01:16:17.372
That's why we started
the Kubernetes project.

01:16:17.372 --> 01:16:21.410
That's also why we are inviting
the industry to be part of it,

01:16:21.410 --> 01:16:25.113
because for containers to take
on the real prominence that we

01:16:25.113 --> 01:16:28.417
think they're capable of, it
will take the whole industry to

01:16:28.417 --> 01:16:30.485
come together to
make that happen.

01:16:30.485 --> 01:16:34.890
It's also why hybrid cloud was
a fundamental design point from

01:16:34.890 --> 01:16:38.760
Kubernetes from the beginning to
allow you to deploy containers

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:42.698
on one cloud or another, with
virtualization, with Bare Metal,

01:16:42.698 --> 01:16:45.834
On Prem, so Ray is
going to come up here,

01:16:45.834 --> 01:16:48.570
and we're going to give
you a demo of containers,

01:16:48.570 --> 01:16:51.073
and we're going to start with a
demo of Container Engine which

01:16:51.073 --> 01:16:53.175
is our fully hosted
Kubernetes environment.

01:16:53.175 --> 01:16:57.713
So Kubernetes is designed
to be declarative.

01:16:57.713 --> 01:17:01.116
You don't deploy applications
with this complex mess of

01:17:01.116 --> 01:17:03.952
scripting code that
runs at deployment time,

01:17:03.952 --> 01:17:06.955
and God help you if it fails and
you have to debug a partially

01:17:06.955 --> 01:17:08.023
deployed application.

01:17:08.023 --> 01:17:10.225
Kubernetes is
entirely descriptive,

01:17:10.225 --> 01:17:13.395
so Ray's going to open up
the file that describes this

01:17:13.395 --> 01:17:15.364
particular application.

01:17:15.364 --> 01:17:17.366
I'm not going to drill
into the whole thing,

01:17:17.366 --> 01:17:19.735
but you'll notice we're saying
that we need ten instances of a

01:17:19.735 --> 01:17:22.571
particular back end server,
we're defining what ports we

01:17:22.571 --> 01:17:26.308
need are open, and again what is
relevant here is what you don't

01:17:26.308 --> 01:17:27.476
see.

01:17:27.476 --> 01:17:30.779
I don't see here
N 1 standard 8s,

01:17:30.779 --> 01:17:34.349
or machine sizes or memory
sizes or disc sizes.

01:17:34.349 --> 01:17:38.820
Kubernetes will just find the
right capacity in my cluster and

01:17:38.820 --> 01:17:40.956
it will schedule
this job wherever.

01:17:40.956 --> 01:17:44.159
As a programmer, I just operate
at the level of applications and

01:17:44.159 --> 01:17:47.195
services, not at a bunch of
infrastructure that I really

01:17:47.195 --> 01:17:49.798
don't actually care
that much about.

01:17:49.798 --> 01:17:52.434
So great, he's going to exit out
of his editor and we're going to

01:17:52.434 --> 01:17:56.805
do a deployment, so he's going
to use the Kubernetes command

01:17:56.805 --> 01:18:00.208
line to deploy this
application into GKE.

01:18:00.208 --> 01:18:03.078
Now usually at an
event like this,

01:18:03.078 --> 01:18:06.314
I would plead with you to not
get your phone out and not do

01:18:06.314 --> 01:18:08.850
anything on the Wi-Fi, but we're
going to do a little running

01:18:08.850 --> 01:18:10.052
with scissors here.

01:18:10.052 --> 01:18:12.888
I'd like you to actually send
some load to this application

01:18:12.888 --> 01:18:13.889
for us.

01:18:13.889 --> 01:18:15.891
There's a URL there
on the screen.

01:18:15.891 --> 01:18:16.892
HCR.KAS.IO.

01:18:19.094 --> 01:18:20.095
Really.

01:18:20.095 --> 01:18:22.097
No, I mean that, like
please help me out,

01:18:22.097 --> 01:18:24.099
get your phones out, go
navigate to the site.

01:18:24.099 --> 01:18:27.269
While people do that -- and
start clicking that button.

01:18:27.269 --> 01:18:29.938
While you do that, we're going
to see the load go up on this

01:18:29.938 --> 01:18:33.675
application on the chart there.

01:18:33.675 --> 01:18:34.676
Okay.

01:18:34.676 --> 01:18:36.678
I give you guys an A for effort.

01:18:36.678 --> 01:18:39.781
But nobody at Google would call
150 queries per second Internet

01:18:39.781 --> 01:18:41.716
scale.

01:18:41.716 --> 01:18:43.852
Luckily, that's what load
testers are invented for,

01:18:43.852 --> 01:18:45.854
to give us sort of
a helping hand here.

01:18:45.854 --> 01:18:49.057
So Ray's going to kick off a
load tester that's going to send

01:18:49.057 --> 01:18:53.195
about 20,000 requests per
second to this service.

01:18:53.195 --> 01:18:56.298
And you'll see it start
ramping up, and sure enough,

01:18:56.298 --> 01:18:58.066
that's great, no problems.

01:18:58.066 --> 01:19:01.503
Now, if I did this
demo 18 months ago,

01:19:01.503 --> 01:19:04.973
this would have been, you know,
considered an amazing thing,

01:19:04.973 --> 01:19:07.342
like who has the ability to
build services that run at that

01:19:07.342 --> 01:19:08.343
scale?

01:19:08.343 --> 01:19:11.346
And now it's my
hello world demo.

01:19:11.346 --> 01:19:14.516
But what happens when you want
to update your application?

01:19:14.516 --> 01:19:16.685
Now, you all know
the right answer.

01:19:16.685 --> 01:19:18.086
You do a staged update.

01:19:18.086 --> 01:19:22.991
You do the 1%, 5%, 10%, you
monitor it very closely.

01:19:22.991 --> 01:19:25.760
You hope you don't
have to roll back,

01:19:25.760 --> 01:19:28.864
but in reality that's a hard
thing to do while you're serving

01:19:28.864 --> 01:19:31.233
20,000 requests per second.

01:19:31.233 --> 01:19:35.137
Kubernetes has built in the
ability to do automated scalable

01:19:35.137 --> 01:19:39.207
rollouts so it will just -- it
will slowly roll out updated

01:19:39.207 --> 01:19:41.843
versions of your
application over time.

01:19:41.843 --> 01:19:45.080
Now, to do this right, I should
make this take, you know,

01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:46.081
a day or two.

01:19:46.081 --> 01:19:48.683
We're going to -- we're going
to go ahead and condense this,

01:19:48.683 --> 01:19:50.752
so he's going to do a
rolling update command,

01:19:50.752 --> 01:19:53.788
and we're going to tell it to
really push this thing as fast

01:19:53.788 --> 01:19:54.789
as possible.

01:19:54.789 --> 01:19:57.492
So what you're going to see is
down there on the bottom of the

01:19:57.492 --> 01:19:59.494
chart, there's the
version numbers,

01:19:59.494 --> 01:20:03.098
and you're seeing as Kubernetes
is one by one replacing one

01:20:03.098 --> 01:20:07.002
instance of our application
with the updated version,

01:20:07.002 --> 01:20:11.039
so the green slowly shifts to
-- the blue shifts to green.

01:20:11.039 --> 01:20:16.044
If we keep clicking the button,
eventually you'll see -- you'll

01:20:16.044 --> 01:20:18.547
experience the new version of
the application and the button

01:20:18.547 --> 01:20:20.248
will change color.

01:20:20.248 --> 01:20:23.818
So there we are under a load
of 20,000 queries per second.

01:20:23.818 --> 01:20:26.254
We're doing a live rolling
update of the application,

01:20:26.254 --> 01:20:29.191
and you'll notice what the
QPS really just stays very,

01:20:29.191 --> 01:20:32.027
very steady at about
20,000 requests per second.

01:20:32.027 --> 01:20:35.864
Our users never even
noticed we did an update.

01:20:35.864 --> 01:20:36.865
So that's -- go ahead, yes.

01:20:36.865 --> 01:20:37.866
[ Applause ]

01:20:37.866 --> 01:20:39.868
So that's our application
running on GKE.

01:20:39.868 --> 01:20:44.873
But we talked about hybrid cloud
as being a key design goal,

01:20:49.811 --> 01:20:53.815
and one of the challenges of
hybrid is how do I bridge my On

01:20:53.815 --> 01:20:56.685
Premise infrastructure
deployments and my new cloud

01:20:56.685 --> 01:20:58.086
infrastructure deployments?

01:20:58.086 --> 01:21:00.088
They have different
tools, different language,

01:21:00.088 --> 01:21:01.756
different ways of working.

01:21:01.756 --> 01:21:03.525
Kubernetes can help with that.

01:21:03.525 --> 01:21:05.527
Ray is going to switch
over to another tab here,

01:21:05.527 --> 01:21:09.231
and this tab has the same
SDKs, same tools installed,

01:21:09.231 --> 01:21:12.534
but instead of being
pointed at a GKE cluster,

01:21:12.534 --> 01:21:15.670
it's being pointed at a cluster
right over here on the right

01:21:15.670 --> 01:21:18.106
part of the stage.

01:21:18.106 --> 01:21:20.041
That server rack over there.

01:21:20.041 --> 01:21:23.211
Our friends at Intel have
graciously loaned us a rack of

01:21:23.211 --> 01:21:24.212
servers.

01:21:24.212 --> 01:21:26.214
Those aren't even
running virtualization.

01:21:26.214 --> 01:21:28.783
Those are bare metal servers
with Kubernetes running on them.

01:21:28.783 --> 01:21:31.453
They use the latest generation
Intel Z on processors.

01:21:31.453 --> 01:21:34.489
Kubernetes is totally
optimized for that.

01:21:34.489 --> 01:21:37.659
So now he's going to
use that same command,

01:21:37.659 --> 01:21:40.562
and now the application is being
deployed over there on to our

01:21:40.562 --> 01:21:41.963
rack.

01:21:41.963 --> 01:21:43.999
Now if you go and see --
look at the load graph,

01:21:43.999 --> 01:21:47.736
you will see that we've put a
load balancer in front of GKE

01:21:47.736 --> 01:21:49.804
and in front of our On
Premise infrastructure.

01:21:49.804 --> 01:21:52.774
And you'll see seamlessly our
application is being deployed.

01:21:52.774 --> 01:21:56.077
The instances, now there's
20 versions of my application

01:21:56.077 --> 01:21:57.145
running.

01:21:57.145 --> 01:21:59.147
We're seamlessly
splitting the traffic,

01:21:59.147 --> 01:22:02.183
both serving about 20,000 QPS.

01:22:02.183 --> 01:22:03.184
So there you go.

01:22:03.184 --> 01:22:04.185
Five minutes.

01:22:04.185 --> 01:22:05.186
We took an application.

01:22:05.186 --> 01:22:06.187
We deployed it.

01:22:06.187 --> 01:22:07.722
We did a rolling update to it.

01:22:07.722 --> 01:22:11.159
We bridged that application to
our On Prem infrastructure all

01:22:11.159 --> 01:22:14.829
without having to deal with
the differences between the two

01:22:14.829 --> 01:22:15.830
environments.

01:22:16.197 --> 01:22:18.199
Thanks, Ray, that
was a great demo.

01:22:18.199 --> 01:22:19.868
[ Applause ]

01:22:22.203 --> 01:22:24.906
&gt;&gt; You heard from a few of our
customers and we have more on

01:22:24.906 --> 01:22:25.874
the way.

01:22:25.874 --> 01:22:27.976
But while we're getting
things set up on stage,

01:22:27.976 --> 01:22:30.912
we want to watch this short
video of what our customers are

01:22:30.912 --> 01:22:31.913
doing on GCP.

01:22:33.948 --> 01:22:36.685
&gt;&gt; Cloud's fascinating, because
really what it's done is it's

01:22:36.685 --> 01:22:39.888
completely changed the
speed of innovation.

01:22:39.888 --> 01:22:43.224
&gt;&gt; From hosting websites Compute
Engines to data prediction it

01:22:43.224 --> 01:22:45.593
offers the flexibility
that companies need now.

01:22:45.593 --> 01:22:48.663
It's what makes Cloud and
Google Cloud Platform very,

01:22:48.663 --> 01:22:50.699
very important to
our businesses.

01:22:50.699 --> 01:22:53.268
&gt;&gt; I grew up just absolutely
loving visual effects.

01:22:53.268 --> 01:22:56.171
When we started Atomic Fiction,
one of the most challenging

01:22:56.171 --> 01:22:58.873
parts of doing visual
effects is rendering.

01:22:58.873 --> 01:23:02.811
It takes an immense amount
of computing horsepower using

01:23:02.811 --> 01:23:06.381
Google Cloud Platform, we have
massive infrastructure off site

01:23:06.381 --> 01:23:09.217
when we need it and then
nothing when we don't.

01:23:09.217 --> 01:23:12.220
It helped us put the resources
on the artist and making sure

01:23:12.220 --> 01:23:15.523
that every penny that our client
spent made it up on the viewing

01:23:15.523 --> 01:23:16.324
screen.

01:23:16.324 --> 01:23:19.994
&gt;&gt; Khan Academy's mission is a
free world class education for

01:23:19.994 --> 01:23:21.629
anyone, anywhere.

01:23:21.629 --> 01:23:23.898
Google Cloud Platform allows
us to stay focused on learner

01:23:23.898 --> 01:23:26.634
outcomes because our
infrastructure has become this

01:23:26.634 --> 01:23:28.570
invisible layer that we don't
have to worry about anymore.

01:23:29.504 --> 01:23:31.840
&gt;&gt; Keeping track of that much
data for millions of students

01:23:31.840 --> 01:23:34.075
every day is a
challenging problem.

01:23:34.075 --> 01:23:36.578
And storing all that data and
being able to quickly act on it

01:23:36.578 --> 01:23:40.181
and look at it is really
core to our mission.

01:23:40.181 --> 01:23:42.450
&gt;&gt; The name Pocket Gems really
speaks to what we do which is

01:23:42.450 --> 01:23:44.319
make beautiful things
for your mobile device.

01:23:44.319 --> 01:23:48.289
&gt;&gt; For War Dragons in minutes or
hours you might have hundreds of

01:23:48.289 --> 01:23:50.225
thousands on players
playing your game.

01:23:50.225 --> 01:23:52.660
If you don't have the
server infrastructure,

01:23:52.660 --> 01:23:54.662
your servers are just
going to explode.

01:23:54.662 --> 01:23:57.999
Google Cloud Platform allows
us to be able to scale

01:23:57.999 --> 01:23:59.000
automatically.

01:23:59.000 --> 01:24:00.702
You don't have to
think about that.

01:24:00.702 --> 01:24:04.439
Your backend just works,
it's almost magical.

01:24:04.439 --> 01:24:08.276
&gt;&gt; Payments are the connective
tissue of economies and that

01:24:08.276 --> 01:24:10.445
really resonates with
Nomonin Anywhere.

01:24:10.445 --> 01:24:13.548
We are providing payments in
the massive underserved markets.

01:24:13.548 --> 01:24:16.785
&gt;&gt; Hosting our application on
Google Cloud Platform gives us a

01:24:16.785 --> 01:24:19.988
much higher reliability than
we could have gotten hosting

01:24:19.988 --> 01:24:21.156
ourselves.

01:24:21.156 --> 01:24:23.425
&gt;&gt; We're really able to move the
needle and make those payments

01:24:23.425 --> 01:24:25.193
available and
affordable everywhere.

01:24:25.193 --> 01:24:29.230
&gt;&gt; The mission at Spotify
is really about being a great

01:24:29.230 --> 01:24:31.533
musical companion and then
making it more personal.

01:24:31.533 --> 01:24:35.136
&gt;&gt; Moving to Google's Cloud
Platform enabled us to take use

01:24:35.136 --> 01:24:37.439
of the skill that
Google can provide.

01:24:37.439 --> 01:24:40.108
&gt;&gt; The reality is, it's quite
hard to build great data centers

01:24:40.108 --> 01:24:43.044
and that was really the driver
in to Google's Cloud Platform.

01:24:43.044 --> 01:24:46.080
The less time that we can
spend solving problems that are

01:24:46.080 --> 01:24:49.083
already solved, the more time
and energy we can spend on

01:24:49.083 --> 01:24:51.352
turning our data into value.

01:24:51.352 --> 01:24:54.823
&gt;&gt; We wanted to make this
marketing campaign for Coca-Cola

01:24:54.823 --> 01:24:57.659
something that every single
person could participate.

01:24:57.659 --> 01:25:00.462
Submit your photo, you can be on
the flag and the opening of the

01:25:00.462 --> 01:25:01.429
World Cup.

01:25:01.429 --> 01:25:03.164
&gt;&gt; It was a gigantic scale.

01:25:03.164 --> 01:25:06.734
We did not have time to set up
an infrastructure and set up the

01:25:06.734 --> 01:25:07.936
servers.

01:25:07.936 --> 01:25:11.873
By using Google Cloud Platform,
we went straight into building

01:25:11.873 --> 01:25:13.208
the top of it.

01:25:13.208 --> 01:25:15.109
&gt;&gt; Fundamentally it gets
down to one word, it's trust.

01:25:15.109 --> 01:25:18.246
The same infrastructure that
powers the Google search engine

01:25:18.246 --> 01:25:21.249
is what's powering the cloud
that Coca-Cola is running on.

01:25:21.249 --> 01:25:34.729
[ Music ]

01:25:34.729 --> 01:25:36.030
&gt;&gt; Well, I hope you enjoyed
that little sneak peek.

01:25:36.030 --> 01:25:38.967
Sorry to cut it off, but
we got to get back inside.

01:25:38.967 --> 01:25:42.504
The CTO and SVP of Walt Disney
Company is on stage right now.

01:25:42.504 --> 01:25:43.638
&gt;&gt; Whoa!

01:25:46.307 --> 01:25:50.245
&gt;&gt; So, we're really lucky
to have Mike White here.

01:25:50.245 --> 01:25:52.647
He's the CTO of
Disney Interactive.

01:25:52.647 --> 01:26:01.155
[ Applause ]

01:26:01.155 --> 01:26:02.557
Great video.

01:26:02.557 --> 01:26:03.691
Definitely an
innovation company.

01:26:03.691 --> 01:26:06.294
So how do you actually think of
the role of technology and how

01:26:06.294 --> 01:26:08.263
it applies at creating
even more innovation.

01:26:08.263 --> 01:26:11.699
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's ironic being here
where technology is very front

01:26:11.699 --> 01:26:13.167
forward.

01:26:13.167 --> 01:26:15.436
As we look at our jobs and
when Disney is at its best,

01:26:15.436 --> 01:26:19.707
technology is always
in service in the story.

01:26:19.707 --> 01:26:23.511
It's always been a key aspect
to the Walt Disney Company's

01:26:23.511 --> 01:26:25.680
approach to story telling.

01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:28.483
And over 100 --
almost 100 years,

01:26:28.483 --> 01:26:32.053
we have had innovators
that have invented,

01:26:32.053 --> 01:26:35.623
reinvented industry after
industry from travel to

01:26:35.623 --> 01:26:36.991
entertainment.

01:26:36.991 --> 01:26:40.028
So if it's our films, if it's
our parks, if it's our games,

01:26:40.028 --> 01:26:43.231
and if it's our products,
technology is always in service

01:26:43.231 --> 01:26:44.232
of the story.

01:26:44.232 --> 01:26:47.268
It's always the scaffolding but
the story telling comes first.

01:26:47.268 --> 01:26:49.304
And that's what's
really paramount to us.

01:26:49.304 --> 01:26:52.140
And as technology becomes more
and more prevalent to everyone,

01:26:52.140 --> 01:26:55.043
it also becomes more powerful,
it's super important that we

01:26:55.043 --> 01:26:58.713
have those tools and provide
those tools to our story tellers

01:26:58.713 --> 01:27:01.249
such that they can provide
the magic to our guests.

01:27:02.617 --> 01:27:04.319
&gt;&gt; So how does the
cloud fit into that?

01:27:04.319 --> 01:27:06.955
How does Google actually
help you with all of that?

01:27:06.955 --> 01:27:09.424
&gt;&gt; So, yeah, a couple of
things to provide context.

01:27:09.424 --> 01:27:12.260
So I look after consumer
products and interactive media.

01:27:12.260 --> 01:27:17.832
We are the part that lives with
the guests in their day-to-day

01:27:17.832 --> 01:27:18.866
lives.

01:27:18.866 --> 01:27:20.969
So we have 1.6 million products.

01:27:20.969 --> 01:27:24.439
We have a fabulous mobile games
team that publishes tons of

01:27:24.439 --> 01:27:27.241
games that people play millions
and millions of hours on.

01:27:27.241 --> 01:27:30.745
We also look at
our Disney stores.

01:27:30.745 --> 01:27:33.948
We have the largest with
the kids -- largest kid book

01:27:33.948 --> 01:27:35.917
publisher.

01:27:35.917 --> 01:27:38.353
And when you look
at our landscape,

01:27:38.353 --> 01:27:42.590
we need to be able to provide
more and more emerging

01:27:42.590 --> 01:27:45.627
technologies because of those
touch points that we have with

01:27:45.627 --> 01:27:46.694
our guests.

01:27:46.694 --> 01:27:49.530
We look after the digital front
doors and all of the social

01:27:49.530 --> 01:27:50.865
media as well.

01:27:50.865 --> 01:27:53.835
So with that, we need to
be hooked into the Cloud.

01:27:53.835 --> 01:27:56.504
And we've been leveraging the
cloud for sometime and cloud

01:27:56.504 --> 01:28:01.042
technologies for sometime, but
now we've partnered with Google

01:28:01.042 --> 01:28:05.747
on some neat projects and it's
really helped us get to where we

01:28:05.747 --> 01:28:10.485
need to go which was not
worrying so much about what's

01:28:10.485 --> 01:28:14.022
under the hood and having our
folks focus on the product

01:28:14.022 --> 01:28:16.190
themselves and really
the guest experience.

01:28:16.190 --> 01:28:19.460
&gt;&gt; And so obviously we
thank you for picking GCP.

01:28:19.460 --> 01:28:22.196
So what's that experience been
like working with the Cloud

01:28:22.196 --> 01:28:22.897
team?

01:28:22.897 --> 01:28:23.765
&gt;&gt; Well, it's been great.

01:28:23.765 --> 01:28:25.266
I mean, the first thing
you do, as you guys know,

01:28:25.266 --> 01:28:27.068
you take a very
scientific approach to it.

01:28:27.068 --> 01:28:32.473
You send in your most cynical
software developer to go in and

01:28:32.473 --> 01:28:34.242
meet with the Google
folks at the Googleplex.

01:28:34.242 --> 01:28:35.943
And I'm sure you guys can close
your eyes and picture that

01:28:35.943 --> 01:28:37.478
person in your head.

01:28:37.478 --> 01:28:39.080
We have them too.

01:28:39.080 --> 01:28:42.950
And so, go in there -- and
really what we were looking for

01:28:42.950 --> 01:28:43.885
was culture.

01:28:43.885 --> 01:28:46.688
You know, it's
trendy to say, well,

01:28:46.688 --> 01:28:48.990
let's fail fast and
be able to experiment,

01:28:48.990 --> 01:28:51.392
but we truly believe in that.

01:28:51.392 --> 01:28:54.295
John Lassiter at Pixar says, "be
wrong quick."

01:28:54.295 --> 01:28:56.364
And so we really want to take that approach to that.

01:28:56.364 --> 01:28:58.833
We saw that with the Google team
that we could get up and running

01:28:58.833 --> 01:29:00.001
very quickly.

01:29:00.001 --> 01:29:02.070
The second thing we
looked at was code wins.

01:29:02.070 --> 01:29:04.539
And so for us, that is a mantra
through all of our developers

01:29:04.539 --> 01:29:07.141
when we say code wins and that
means can we get up in your

01:29:07.141 --> 01:29:07.975
environment?

01:29:07.975 --> 01:29:09.110
Can we get going?

01:29:09.110 --> 01:29:12.513
And I've spent my whole career
in Silicon Valley and a lot of

01:29:12.513 --> 01:29:14.782
the folks that, you know, work
at Disney you had a little peek

01:29:14.782 --> 01:29:18.953
behind the curtain of the folks
that provide the technology for

01:29:18.953 --> 01:29:19.554
that.

01:29:19.554 --> 01:29:21.689
Have spent a lot of years in
high-tech companies and they

01:29:21.689 --> 01:29:24.392
didn't come to Disney
to build a data base.

01:29:24.392 --> 01:29:26.260
And so really what we wanted to
get out of is the conversations

01:29:26.260 --> 01:29:29.564
of plumbing and conversations
of how do you spin things up and

01:29:29.564 --> 01:29:32.500
more into how do we create these
really great product experiences

01:29:32.500 --> 01:29:33.701
for our guests.

01:29:34.268 --> 01:29:36.504
&gt;&gt; So like in our past
conversations that was stunning,

01:29:36.504 --> 01:29:37.772
I don't know if
the number was 700.

01:29:37.772 --> 01:29:39.874
The number of projects
on App Engine was huge.

01:29:39.874 --> 01:29:42.176
But what are some of the ones
that you're thinking about that

01:29:42.176 --> 01:29:44.312
actually you're most involved
with and getting excited?

01:29:44.846 --> 01:29:47.882
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so currently, we have
over 200 projects spun up.

01:29:47.882 --> 01:29:50.318
A lot of them are
coming out soon,

01:29:50.318 --> 01:29:51.786
we're super excited about that.

01:29:51.786 --> 01:29:53.988
We're going to hold that back
for larger releases with that.

01:29:53.988 --> 01:29:55.690
But we're excited about that.

01:29:55.690 --> 01:29:58.359
We do have a product that I'm
super excited about that we

01:29:58.359 --> 01:30:00.361
launched called Disney life.

01:30:00.361 --> 01:30:01.295
It was in the UK.

01:30:01.295 --> 01:30:03.898
It's an over the top service
that not only does videos and

01:30:03.898 --> 01:30:08.269
movies and television, but
also books and music and games.

01:30:08.269 --> 01:30:09.871
And so that's been huge.

01:30:09.871 --> 01:30:12.106
And the partnership with
Google has been very helpful.

01:30:12.106 --> 01:30:14.442
In fact they even participated
in our war room to launch that

01:30:14.442 --> 01:30:15.243
out.

01:30:15.243 --> 01:30:17.845
So not just to be able to have
the ability to partner with

01:30:17.845 --> 01:30:20.148
someone but not think about the
scale and knowing that they're

01:30:20.148 --> 01:30:22.016
going to be there with you
to do that has really been

01:30:22.016 --> 01:30:23.251
instrumental to us.

01:30:23.718 --> 01:30:24.652
&gt;&gt; Awesome, well,
thanks for coming.

01:30:25.119 --> 01:30:26.154
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thanks for having me.

01:30:26.154 --> 01:30:31.092
[ Applause ]

01:30:31.092 --> 01:30:35.096
&gt;&gt; So Mike and Disney aren't
that unique in that, you know,

01:30:35.096 --> 01:30:36.297
they want to go faster.

01:30:36.297 --> 01:30:39.934
And we've been working harder
to make all developers on APP

01:30:39.934 --> 01:30:41.903
Engine to be able to
be more productive.

01:30:41.903 --> 01:30:45.239
And one of the things that held
us back is to be honest was the

01:30:45.239 --> 01:30:46.674
run times and the language
support that we could bring to

01:30:46.674 --> 01:30:47.909
APP Engine.

01:30:47.909 --> 01:30:51.078
And so just the way we talked
about like doctorate containers

01:30:51.078 --> 01:30:53.915
moving the industry forward,
we've integrated containers now

01:30:53.915 --> 01:30:55.049
with APP Engine.

01:30:55.049 --> 01:30:57.318
And what's that's meant is that
we can now bring out new run

01:30:57.318 --> 01:31:00.021
times in that zero ops
environment of App Engine much

01:31:00.021 --> 01:31:02.824
quicker, so we can bring out the
latest version of java, node,

01:31:02.824 --> 01:31:04.292
and Python.

01:31:04.292 --> 01:31:07.428
So here's -- Greg's back and
he's going to show us a demo on

01:31:07.428 --> 01:31:09.063
what's happening
with APP Engine.

01:31:12.834 --> 01:31:14.902
&gt;&gt; Greg: Thanks, Brian.

01:31:14.902 --> 01:31:17.405
So next up, we're going to give
you -- for the developers in the

01:31:17.405 --> 01:31:20.041
audience a lap around
Google Cloud Platform.

01:31:20.041 --> 01:31:23.311
And along the way I'm going to
show you a few of my favorite

01:31:23.311 --> 01:31:25.546
developer productivity features.

01:31:25.546 --> 01:31:27.715
So the scenario we're
going to do this -- oh,

01:31:27.715 --> 01:31:30.117
and I've got Bret here on
stage is going to help me out.

01:31:30.117 --> 01:31:31.285
Thank you very much, Bret.

01:31:31.285 --> 01:31:33.354
We're going to show you an
application we built at a recent

01:31:33.354 --> 01:31:34.922
hack-a-thon within Google.

01:31:34.922 --> 01:31:37.325
The application is called
Photo Scavenger Hunt.

01:31:37.325 --> 01:31:38.993
It is a mobile game.

01:31:38.993 --> 01:31:42.063
It gives you a set of four clues
for things that it wants you to

01:31:42.063 --> 01:31:43.464
take pictures of.

01:31:43.464 --> 01:31:44.465
You take a picture.

01:31:44.465 --> 01:31:47.134
If it sees the object it
asked you for in the picture,

01:31:47.134 --> 01:31:48.569
you get points.

01:31:48.569 --> 01:31:50.805
The architecture of the
application is actually very

01:31:50.805 --> 01:31:51.806
simple.

01:31:51.806 --> 01:31:55.409
It is a mobile client that talks
via a rest API to a backend

01:31:55.409 --> 01:31:56.978
hosted on App Engine.

01:31:56.978 --> 01:31:59.714
That App Engine uses the
vision API to see what's in the

01:31:59.714 --> 01:32:03.451
picture, and it stores the
clues in a Mongo data base.

01:32:03.451 --> 01:32:08.456
Now App Engine has always had
support for languages like Ruby

01:32:10.725 --> 01:32:12.894
and Python and Go.

01:32:12.894 --> 01:32:17.632
But today we're adding support
for new languages like no JS,

01:32:17.632 --> 01:32:19.400
and as I said, actually, Ruby.

01:32:19.400 --> 01:32:25.873
So in this case, we've already
got the application a little bit

01:32:25.873 --> 01:32:26.507
of code.

01:32:26.507 --> 01:32:28.910
So why don't, Bret,
open up the text editor.

01:32:28.910 --> 01:32:32.947
So we're going to highlight just
a couple of lines of code here.

01:32:32.947 --> 01:32:38.786
The first is, there we go,
the call to the Vision API.

01:32:38.786 --> 01:32:41.756
You'll see that referenced down,
one line down to the Vision API.

01:32:41.756 --> 01:32:44.725
And we're using two
features of the Vision API.

01:32:44.725 --> 01:32:47.361
We're using label detection to
tell us what's in the picture,

01:32:47.361 --> 01:32:49.430
and since I don't trust
my users entirely,

01:32:49.430 --> 01:32:52.066
we're using safe search
protection to make sure there's

01:32:52.066 --> 01:32:55.736
no naughty bits coming
up into our application.

01:32:55.736 --> 01:32:57.271
We're going to run this locally.

01:32:57.271 --> 01:32:59.607
Like many of you, our developers
sometimes want to just work

01:32:59.607 --> 01:33:01.676
locally on their laptop.

01:33:01.676 --> 01:33:03.878
Maybe they're on a
plane, don't have access.

01:33:03.878 --> 01:33:07.615
So Bret's going to fire
up a local copy of Node.

01:33:07.615 --> 01:33:09.884
And we've got this little
service running locally now.

01:33:09.884 --> 01:33:12.119
Now I got this
picture of a raccoon.

01:33:12.119 --> 01:33:14.689
So he's going to use Curl
to just test out the local

01:33:14.689 --> 01:33:17.892
application and see what
he gets back as a response.

01:33:17.892 --> 01:33:22.029
And sure enough, 95.7%
confidence in an animal and all

01:33:22.029 --> 01:33:24.498
the way down to it
is a Procyonidae,

01:33:24.498 --> 01:33:26.367
which I had to look that up.

01:33:26.367 --> 01:33:31.439
And that is the family that
raccoon belongs to in a -- so I

01:33:31.439 --> 01:33:35.209
actually learned something about
biology from our demo today.

01:33:35.209 --> 01:33:37.244
Okay, so we got a local
version of the application.

01:33:37.244 --> 01:33:39.413
Now let's deploy it
up into the cloud.

01:33:39.413 --> 01:33:42.083
Now this is where normally you
get into the world of having to

01:33:42.083 --> 01:33:45.019
configure machines,
memory, load balancers,

01:33:45.019 --> 01:33:47.021
what operating
system should I use?

01:33:47.021 --> 01:33:48.656
Is my image fully patched?

01:33:48.656 --> 01:33:49.957
Not with App Engine.

01:33:49.957 --> 01:33:52.326
I just let me just
deploy the code.

01:33:52.326 --> 01:33:54.328
So he's going to do
a G-cloud deploy.

01:33:54.328 --> 01:33:56.697
Now what's happening behind
the scenes here is kind of

01:33:56.697 --> 01:33:58.132
interesting.

01:33:58.132 --> 01:34:01.068
That source code is being
deployed up into App Engine

01:34:01.068 --> 01:34:03.537
where it's getting ready
to be -- to be run.

01:34:03.537 --> 01:34:06.374
That's kind of
standard App Engine.

01:34:06.374 --> 01:34:08.909
But what's really interesting
here is for new languages like

01:34:08.909 --> 01:34:13.447
Node, Ruby, and Java 8, it's
being packaged up as a container

01:34:13.447 --> 01:34:16.250
and then it's being stored
in a private Google container

01:34:16.250 --> 01:34:18.119
registry for you.

01:34:18.119 --> 01:34:20.421
So that means I was able
to build a container-based

01:34:20.421 --> 01:34:23.991
application without even having
Docker installed on my local

01:34:23.991 --> 01:34:25.493
system.

01:34:25.493 --> 01:34:28.629
Not only that, because it's
a containerized application,

01:34:28.629 --> 01:34:31.766
it can run anywhere in
the container ecosystem.

01:34:31.766 --> 01:34:35.403
Whatever provider you want
on Prim or in a cloud.

01:34:35.403 --> 01:34:38.005
And, of course, it can be
orchestrated by Kubernetes just

01:34:38.005 --> 01:34:39.073
as well.

01:34:39.073 --> 01:34:42.243
So if you step back, what
that means is that App Engine

01:34:42.243 --> 01:34:46.480
developers can now be a full
participant in the exciting

01:34:46.480 --> 01:34:48.783
developments happening around
containers and container

01:34:48.783 --> 01:34:50.351
management.

01:34:50.351 --> 01:34:52.586
So let's actually switch
over to the console,

01:34:52.586 --> 01:34:53.854
to the cloud console.

01:34:53.854 --> 01:34:56.891
And let's take a look and
find that image that just got

01:34:56.891 --> 01:34:59.460
uploaded into GCS.

01:34:59.460 --> 01:35:02.730
So Bret's going to navigate
it over to GCS storage,

01:35:02.730 --> 01:35:06.233
and I don't have permissions
to see the storage section.

01:35:06.233 --> 01:35:07.568
That's not an error.

01:35:07.568 --> 01:35:10.004
We've used identity and access
management because I'm just a

01:35:10.004 --> 01:35:11.005
developer.

01:35:11.005 --> 01:35:13.541
I shouldn't have access to my
customer's personal images,

01:35:13.541 --> 01:35:14.542
right?

01:35:14.542 --> 01:35:18.012
So we're using ACLs and identity
and access management so that I

01:35:18.012 --> 01:35:19.613
don't have access.

01:35:19.613 --> 01:35:22.650
Now it turns out we have the
manager logged in on a different

01:35:22.650 --> 01:35:23.651
tab.

01:35:23.651 --> 01:35:28.789
And here as the manager, Bret
can go in and expand out the

01:35:28.789 --> 01:35:32.893
privileges for App Engine admin
and he could add me as a storage

01:35:32.893 --> 01:35:35.729
admin for all of those -
all of those capabilities.

01:35:35.729 --> 01:35:41.335
Now as the manager, maybe
I'm actually really paranoid.

01:35:41.335 --> 01:35:44.438
It's not paranoid, it's called
actually just being a good

01:35:44.438 --> 01:35:45.840
manager of security.

01:35:45.840 --> 01:35:49.009
And what I want to do is see
everything that's ever been

01:35:49.009 --> 01:35:50.945
changed about this project.

01:35:50.945 --> 01:35:54.849
The activity stream, new
today, gives me just that.

01:35:54.849 --> 01:35:58.018
I can see every configuration,
every policy change,

01:35:58.018 --> 01:36:00.721
every code push, every
deployment that happens to my

01:36:00.721 --> 01:36:02.256
application.

01:36:02.256 --> 01:36:05.392
This is really handy for
tracking down compliance

01:36:05.392 --> 01:36:08.796
concerns or issues and it's
really important for managers

01:36:08.796 --> 01:36:11.198
and organizations.

01:36:11.198 --> 01:36:13.234
Now while we're here, I want to
hit you -- I want to show you

01:36:13.234 --> 01:36:17.104
two productivity features that
can save you a lot of time.

01:36:17.104 --> 01:36:19.974
First, SSH from the browser.

01:36:19.974 --> 01:36:23.210
How many of you have ever had
to SSH into a VM and you have to

01:36:23.210 --> 01:36:26.580
deal with the hassle
of picking a password,

01:36:26.580 --> 01:36:29.350
recording what the password
is, configuring keys.

01:36:29.350 --> 01:36:30.985
Not with Google Cloud.

01:36:30.985 --> 01:36:34.655
You can just -- Bret can just
one click on the right side on

01:36:34.655 --> 01:36:39.093
any VM and immediately have
a secure SSH session to that

01:36:39.093 --> 01:36:41.395
machine without having
to configure anything.

01:36:41.395 --> 01:36:43.864
One click secure access.

01:36:43.864 --> 01:36:46.700
The other time saver
is the Cloud Shell.

01:36:46.700 --> 01:36:49.336
Have you ever found yourself
needing to do a quick bit of

01:36:49.336 --> 01:36:52.606
work but you don't have your
laptop handy or you don't have a

01:36:52.606 --> 01:36:55.476
machine with an SDK installed?

01:36:55.476 --> 01:36:59.346
Cloud Shell gives you as a
developer a private persistent

01:36:59.346 --> 01:37:03.584
LINUX instance that's
preinstalled in Google Cloud

01:37:03.584 --> 01:37:07.121
with the latest
version of the SDK.

01:37:07.121 --> 01:37:10.524
It's like a GCP command line
in the cloud you can access

01:37:10.524 --> 01:37:11.825
anywhere that you are.

01:37:11.825 --> 01:37:15.596
All you need is a
machine with browser.

01:37:15.596 --> 01:37:16.864
The last thing I want
to show --

01:37:16.864 --> 01:37:17.965
[ Applause ]

01:37:17.965 --> 01:37:22.269
Yes, I knew the developers
would like that.

01:37:22.269 --> 01:37:25.239
Another hard part about building
distributed application is

01:37:25.239 --> 01:37:26.574
performance tracking.

01:37:26.574 --> 01:37:29.743
Performance debugging when we
used to run on one machine was

01:37:29.743 --> 01:37:30.744
actually pretty simple.

01:37:30.744 --> 01:37:34.014
Now individual traces are
routed to different machines.

01:37:34.014 --> 01:37:37.318
And how do you actually get a
consistent view of how long a

01:37:37.318 --> 01:37:38.786
request took?

01:37:38.786 --> 01:37:42.323
Stack Driver Trace is a
distributed tracing utility that

01:37:42.323 --> 01:37:44.792
lets you track down exactly
where your server time is being

01:37:44.792 --> 01:37:45.793
spent.

01:37:45.793 --> 01:37:48.395
It gives you visualizations
like this where you can compare

01:37:48.395 --> 01:37:51.165
latency across versions
of your application.

01:37:51.165 --> 01:37:54.068
So you can see if your new
version is five times slower

01:37:54.068 --> 01:37:55.669
than your old version.

01:37:55.669 --> 01:37:59.273
Not only that, you can go down
down at the bottom and Bret will

01:37:59.273 --> 01:38:03.110
click on an individual trace
and you can see for a given user

01:38:03.110 --> 01:38:06.780
request exactly how long every
call took to service that

01:38:06.780 --> 01:38:07.781
request.

01:38:07.781 --> 01:38:11.085
So he picked a trace here and we
see there's a call to the Vision

01:38:11.085 --> 01:38:14.989
API and the Storage API.

01:38:14.989 --> 01:38:19.693
Individual traces down to the
individual application level.

01:38:19.693 --> 01:38:21.895
Okay, let's actually see
this application in action.

01:38:21.895 --> 01:38:24.431
It's been deployed,
and it's all set go.

01:38:24.431 --> 01:38:27.768
So at this point, Bret needs
to fire up the application.

01:38:27.768 --> 01:38:30.804
And let's take a look at --
it's asking him for a person,

01:38:30.804 --> 01:38:33.641
a hotel, a bank note, or sports.

01:38:33.641 --> 01:38:34.675
Bret, this is your moment.

01:38:34.675 --> 01:38:35.976
Take a selfy on stage.

01:38:43.851 --> 01:38:46.954
All right, let's see what
happens with the Vision API.

01:38:46.954 --> 01:38:53.093
Checking?

01:38:53.093 --> 01:38:54.828
Person, 92% likelihood.

01:38:54.828 --> 01:39:00.701
[ Laughter ]

01:39:00.701 --> 01:39:02.803
Thanks, Bret, that was great.

01:39:02.803 --> 01:39:04.471
That was a great demo.

01:39:04.471 --> 01:39:07.308
So, yes, please, thank Bret.

01:39:07.308 --> 01:39:10.511
[ Applause ]

01:39:10.511 --> 01:39:12.179
So, if you look at
those two demos,

01:39:12.179 --> 01:39:14.548
what you're seeing is we're
trying to take you from a world

01:39:14.548 --> 01:39:16.550
where your developers spend
their time mucking about with

01:39:16.550 --> 01:39:20.020
infrastructure and we're opting
into paths means opting into a

01:39:20.020 --> 01:39:23.424
walled garden of limited choices
to a world where your developers

01:39:23.424 --> 01:39:26.293
can just write code and your
paths participants can be

01:39:26.293 --> 01:39:28.862
connected to the whole
world of containers.

01:39:28.862 --> 01:39:29.863
Thanks very much.

01:39:29.863 --> 01:39:30.864
I'll bring Brian back up now.

01:39:30.864 --> 01:39:31.865
Thank you.

01:39:31.865 --> 01:39:38.172
[ Applause ]

01:39:38.172 --> 01:39:39.573
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Greg.

01:39:39.573 --> 01:39:42.242
So some companies were born in
the Cloud and that's the only

01:39:42.242 --> 01:39:44.044
world that they know.

01:39:44.044 --> 01:39:48.015
But then other companies,
right, don't have that luxury.

01:39:48.015 --> 01:39:50.417
They're traditional
companies right,

01:39:50.417 --> 01:39:53.854
that are moving to Cloud really
rapidly and in so doing that are

01:39:53.854 --> 01:39:56.056
actually becoming
digital businesses.

01:39:56.056 --> 01:39:58.158
But in either case, the most
successful companies are the

01:39:58.158 --> 01:40:00.894
ones that are actually skilled
in managing data scale.

01:40:00.894 --> 01:40:04.164
And GCP excels at storage and
archival but it's a lot more

01:40:04.164 --> 01:40:05.165
than that.

01:40:05.165 --> 01:40:07.167
It's really about pulling
insights from data.

01:40:07.167 --> 01:40:10.371
And that's what separates good
companies from great companies.

01:40:10.371 --> 01:40:12.940
Because great companies know how
to look at data and understand

01:40:12.940 --> 01:40:15.008
their markets,
understand their users,

01:40:15.008 --> 01:40:17.010
how people are using
their products;

01:40:17.010 --> 01:40:20.314
and they respond to
that really rapidly.

01:40:20.314 --> 01:40:22.916
So managing data scale is
obviously one of the things that

01:40:22.916 --> 01:40:24.385
makes Google Google.

01:40:24.385 --> 01:40:27.054
We built Data,
Search, Analytics,

01:40:27.054 --> 01:40:29.990
and that's what powered seven
applications of a billion users

01:40:29.990 --> 01:40:31.158
each.

01:40:31.158 --> 01:40:33.961
And we've actually been pretty
open on it publishing research

01:40:33.961 --> 01:40:36.163
papers on most of the
technologies that you see.

01:40:36.163 --> 01:40:41.235
And in the case of MapReduce
and BigTable and Dremel,

01:40:41.235 --> 01:40:44.271
just those research papers have
spawned whole industries because

01:40:44.271 --> 01:40:46.340
they've been implemented
in open source.

01:40:46.340 --> 01:40:48.642
So as a result, when people
think about big data,

01:40:48.642 --> 01:40:49.843
what do they think of first?

01:40:49.843 --> 01:40:51.845
They typically think of
open source solutions.

01:40:53.947 --> 01:40:55.949
And so now with the cloud,
our strategy is a little bit

01:40:55.949 --> 01:40:56.950
different.

01:40:56.950 --> 01:40:58.952
We're going to actually expose
all of those internal data

01:40:58.952 --> 01:41:01.488
systems as first class cloud
services and that's what

01:41:01.488 --> 01:41:04.858
actually allowed us, right, that
prior work to create an industry

01:41:04.858 --> 01:41:08.896
first around on-line storage at
the durability and the price of

01:41:08.896 --> 01:41:10.397
archival.

01:41:10.397 --> 01:41:12.933
That's what's allowed us to
bring out a full manage to-do

01:41:12.933 --> 01:41:16.737
platform but beyond that the
work that we have done inside

01:41:16.737 --> 01:41:19.306
because the new projects we
use are typically using data

01:41:19.306 --> 01:41:22.943
streaming pipelines, we brought
out data flow to integrate well

01:41:22.943 --> 01:41:26.580
with a dupe as a
fully managed service.

01:41:26.580 --> 01:41:29.883
And now with BigQuery, even
non-Googlers can process,

01:41:29.883 --> 01:41:33.654
you know, trillion
row tables in seconds.

01:41:33.654 --> 01:41:37.157
But as we do this, being
open isn't optional.

01:41:37.157 --> 01:41:39.993
We really believe that
businesses are going to choose

01:41:39.993 --> 01:41:42.129
open as the next
platform, right?

01:41:42.129 --> 01:41:44.665
This is not a point
products decision.

01:41:44.665 --> 01:41:46.633
This is about choosing
your platform.

01:41:46.633 --> 01:41:50.304
So we look for every way that we
can actually be even more open

01:41:50.304 --> 01:41:51.572
than we already are.

01:41:51.572 --> 01:41:56.310
And a big part of that is why
when we put H-space or BigTable

01:41:56.310 --> 01:41:58.145
on Cloud, we didn't
use our internal API.

01:41:58.145 --> 01:42:00.981
We adopted the open
source H-space API.

01:42:00.981 --> 01:42:02.716
And the same thing
with data flow.

01:42:02.716 --> 01:42:04.718
I mean data flow is really
compelling and we could have

01:42:04.718 --> 01:42:06.220
used that to our
proprietary advantage.

01:42:06.220 --> 01:42:09.223
Instead we said let's just
open source the whole model for

01:42:09.223 --> 01:42:11.225
configuring and
executing these runners,

01:42:11.225 --> 01:42:13.560
these data stream pipelines
and creating Apache Incubator

01:42:13.560 --> 01:42:15.996
Project.

01:42:15.996 --> 01:42:19.833
So one part of the job that I
think is most fun besides like

01:42:19.833 --> 01:42:22.669
standing here, is actually
working with customers.

01:42:22.669 --> 01:42:23.670
Right?

01:42:23.670 --> 01:42:25.672
And really working
with customers,

01:42:25.672 --> 01:42:27.641
not just from a
support perspective,

01:42:27.641 --> 01:42:29.643
but on an
engineering-on-engineering

01:42:29.643 --> 01:42:30.644
relationship.

01:42:30.644 --> 01:42:33.347
I mean, so 18 months ago, we
actually embarked working with a

01:42:33.347 --> 01:42:34.348
new customer.

01:42:34.348 --> 01:42:38.185
And it meant a lot of travel
to New York and to Sweden,

01:42:38.185 --> 01:42:39.686
and for them to Mountain View.

01:42:39.686 --> 01:42:44.191
We built a really great
relationship with Spotify.

01:42:44.191 --> 01:42:47.594
&gt;&gt; The mission of Spotify is
really about being a great

01:42:47.594 --> 01:42:50.230
musical companion and
making it more personal.

01:42:50.230 --> 01:42:52.299
It's not going to be this
streaming music experience or

01:42:52.299 --> 01:42:54.735
the online music experience,
it's going to be your music

01:42:54.735 --> 01:42:55.769
experience.

01:42:55.769 --> 01:42:59.573
&gt;&gt; We created the tools for
people to actually swim in that

01:42:59.573 --> 01:43:04.044
sea of music data and find
the information that really

01:43:04.044 --> 01:43:06.613
corresponds to why
they are there.

01:43:06.613 --> 01:43:10.150
&gt;&gt; Quite a while ago, we made
a decision to focus on data.

01:43:10.150 --> 01:43:12.152
&gt;&gt; Depending on the
complexity of the query,

01:43:12.152 --> 01:43:13.754
it could take a whole day.

01:43:13.754 --> 01:43:15.822
Sometimes it would
have to run overnight.

01:43:16.623 --> 01:43:20.227
&gt;&gt; And being able to store that
and process that has been a very

01:43:20.227 --> 01:43:23.664
big challenge.
Moving to Google's Cloud
Platform enabled us to take use

01:43:23.664 --> 01:43:25.632
of the skill that
Google can provide.

01:43:26.033 --> 01:43:28.635
&gt;&gt; The reality is it's quite
hard to build great data centers

01:43:28.635 --> 01:43:31.805
and we saw an opportunity to
spend our focus elsewhere.

01:43:31.805 --> 01:43:34.608
And that was really the driver
into Google's Cloud Platform.

01:43:35.676 --> 01:43:38.378
&gt;&gt; With Google Cloud tools, we
could re-run the same query and

01:43:38.378 --> 01:43:39.947
it might take a few
seconds, minutes.

01:43:41.582 --> 01:43:44.484
&gt;&gt; The less time that we can
spend solving problems that are

01:43:44.484 --> 01:43:48.188
already solved, like scaling our
Hadoop cluster when Google has

01:43:48.188 --> 01:43:49.790
already figured out how to
scale it for us,

01:43:49.790 --> 01:43:52.893
the more time and energy we can
spend on turning our data into

01:43:52.893 --> 01:43:54.127
value.

01:43:54.127 --> 01:43:55.729
And that's really a
game changer for us.

01:43:55.729 --> 01:43:58.332
When you're talking about a
technology like BigQuery and

01:43:58.332 --> 01:43:59.433
when you're talking
about data flow,

01:43:59.433 --> 01:44:02.469
you will feel the Spotify
product evolve making the user

01:44:02.469 --> 01:44:03.503
experience great.

01:44:04.271 --> 01:44:07.007
&gt;&gt; Once you break down the
data, you realize that there are

01:44:07.007 --> 01:44:09.476
nuances, it's not a
straightforward path.

01:44:09.476 --> 01:44:13.580
It's a mosaic rich beyond
the most popular artist.

01:44:13.580 --> 01:44:14.715
It's a lot more than that.

01:44:15.849 --> 01:44:19.987
&gt;&gt; Music can transcend
boundaries of language, culture.

01:44:19.987 --> 01:44:32.132
If it moves you then it's
there and it's there forever.

01:44:32.132 --> 01:44:37.037
&gt;&gt; So please --
[ Applause ]

01:44:37.037 --> 01:44:38.572
So please welcome Nick Harteau.

01:44:38.572 --> 01:44:41.441
The VP of Infrastructure
at Spotify.

01:44:41.441 --> 01:44:47.614
[ Music ]

01:44:47.614 --> 01:44:48.782
&gt;&gt; How's it going?

01:44:48.782 --> 01:44:50.450
&gt;&gt; Hey, Nick.
Good.

01:44:50.450 --> 01:44:52.052
&gt;&gt; Actually, before we get started with the questions,

01:44:52.052 --> 01:44:56.490
can we do another round of
applause for Brian and Greg and

01:44:56.490 --> 01:44:59.526
the engineers who helped out
for the amazing live demos?

01:44:59.526 --> 01:45:04.931
[ Applause ]

01:45:04.931 --> 01:45:07.567
I know that it's not jumping out of airplanes with the VR goggles on.

01:45:07.567 --> 01:45:08.268
&gt;&gt; It's not in the script.

01:45:08.268 --> 01:45:09.336
&gt;&gt; No, it's not in the script.

01:45:09.336 --> 01:45:10.871
It's not jumping out of
airplanes with VR goggles on,

01:45:10.871 --> 01:45:13.640
but for me as an engineer,
that was real bravery,

01:45:13.640 --> 01:45:14.975
the live demo stuff.

01:45:15.909 --> 01:45:16.610
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you.

01:45:17.044 --> 01:45:18.979
&gt;&gt; You're welcome.

01:45:18.979 --> 01:45:21.882
&gt;&gt; So, really, it's been
great working with Spotify.

01:45:21.882 --> 01:45:24.618
We've helped you but I have to
say that you've made us better

01:45:24.618 --> 01:45:25.485
as well.

01:45:25.485 --> 01:45:29.089
Today we actually handed you a
new piece of code for connecting

01:45:29.089 --> 01:45:31.458
projects securely without
having public IPs,

01:45:31.458 --> 01:45:34.027
so just another set of firsts
between the two companies so

01:45:34.027 --> 01:45:36.663
thank you, for working
with us closely.

01:45:36.663 --> 01:45:40.033
So, why did you decide to move
some of your platform to Google

01:45:40.033 --> 01:45:41.968
Cloud Platform?

01:45:42.669 --> 01:45:44.538
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean, going
back to your presentation,

01:45:44.538 --> 01:45:47.708
I think the thing that drew
us to Google over other cloud

01:45:47.708 --> 01:45:49.976
providers actually
was the data platform.

01:45:49.976 --> 01:45:55.415
At Spotify, we are -- whether
it's like A/V testing product

01:45:55.415 --> 01:45:59.753
changes or building personalized
play lists for millions of users

01:45:59.753 --> 01:46:04.424
every week, we are deeply
dependent on data to understand

01:46:04.424 --> 01:46:06.626
our users and to bring
them great user experience.

01:46:06.626 --> 01:46:09.596
And Google, as we
saw on your timeline,

01:46:09.596 --> 01:46:12.032
you guys have been a thought
leader in the data space for as

01:46:12.032 --> 01:46:13.767
far back as I can remember.

01:46:13.767 --> 01:46:17.070
And I think you've done a great
job of bringing that leadership

01:46:17.070 --> 01:46:21.174
and that expertise to GCP in
the form of like great tools and

01:46:21.174 --> 01:46:22.075
services.

01:46:22.075 --> 01:46:25.212
So the data platform more than
anything else was the game

01:46:25.212 --> 01:46:26.246
changer for us.

01:46:26.813 --> 01:46:29.616
&gt;&gt; So we know some of the engine
is already moved on top of the

01:46:29.616 --> 01:46:30.150
cloud.

01:46:30.150 --> 01:46:31.351
So just like how's
the migration going?

01:46:31.351 --> 01:46:32.919
Any challenges you've
had along the way?

01:46:33.553 --> 01:46:37.257
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean we have a pretty
big and complicated backend.

01:46:37.257 --> 01:46:42.095
I think we have 250 or so
services that power Spotify.

01:46:42.095 --> 01:46:44.631
So there's not a simple
answer to that question.

01:46:44.631 --> 01:46:46.166
You know, some of
it is pretty easy,

01:46:46.166 --> 01:46:47.400
other stuff is pretty hard.

01:46:47.400 --> 01:46:51.538
But you know, overall, I would
say it's going better than we

01:46:51.538 --> 01:46:53.473
expected.

01:46:53.473 --> 01:46:54.508
We have our set of challenges.

01:46:54.508 --> 01:46:57.043
You know we're figuring out
some stuff related to time

01:46:57.043 --> 01:46:58.345
syncrhonization.

01:46:58.345 --> 01:47:03.416
We are -- we're learning a bunch
of new API semantics in adopting

01:47:03.416 --> 01:47:04.885
the platform.

01:47:04.885 --> 01:47:06.620
But overall, it's
going pretty well.

01:47:06.620 --> 01:47:08.488
Besides the usual
stuff about, you know,

01:47:08.488 --> 01:47:10.423
changing the tires on the car
while we're driving down the

01:47:10.423 --> 01:47:10.991
highway.

01:47:12.692 --> 01:47:13.293
&gt;&gt; Easy stuff.

01:47:13.293 --> 01:47:13.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:47:14.294 --> 01:47:18.198
&gt;&gt; So we loved the tweets.
So we -- definitely that person is a --

01:47:18.198 --> 01:47:19.733
&gt;&gt; I think he's pretty happy about not having --

01:47:19.733 --> 01:47:22.502
&gt;&gt; He's inaugarated into our marketing hall of fame.

01:47:22.502 --> 01:47:23.236
So we thank him for that.

01:47:23.236 --> 01:47:24.871
So what else are you sort
of hearing from just the

01:47:24.871 --> 01:47:25.872
experience?

01:47:25.872 --> 01:47:27.974
What services are kind of
the most delightful for your

01:47:27.974 --> 01:47:28.975
engineers?

01:47:28.975 --> 01:47:30.477
&gt;&gt; Well, there's two things
I'd want to call out on the

01:47:30.477 --> 01:47:32.579
migration before we go into
the services side of it.

01:47:32.579 --> 01:47:35.916
I would say one thing is -- one
thing that we're like really

01:47:35.916 --> 01:47:38.018
concerned about and
really, you know,

01:47:38.018 --> 01:47:40.353
at the top of our minds in terms
of moving to the cloud has been

01:47:40.353 --> 01:47:41.588
performance.

01:47:41.588 --> 01:47:44.291
We're a super latency
sensitive application.

01:47:44.291 --> 01:47:48.295
We rely very much on our users
feeling like they have the

01:47:48.295 --> 01:47:51.198
world's music like right
there on their phones.

01:47:51.198 --> 01:47:53.900
So we've been really watching
the metrics very carefully as

01:47:53.900 --> 01:47:56.303
we've been moving
into the cloud.

01:47:56.303 --> 01:47:58.371
And we've been really
happy with that.

01:47:58.371 --> 01:48:01.474
The numbers that we're seeing in
terms of performance are as good

01:48:01.474 --> 01:48:03.109
as what we can deliver
from our own data centers.

01:48:03.109 --> 01:48:03.944
&gt;&gt; Cool.

01:48:03.944 --> 01:48:09.382
&gt;&gt; The other thing to pat
you on the back a little bit.

01:48:09.382 --> 01:48:12.219
One of the big unknowns for
us going into the Google

01:48:12.219 --> 01:48:14.454
partnership actually was
the customer service side.

01:48:14.454 --> 01:48:17.824
Like as I said, it's a big and
complicated migration and I knew

01:48:17.824 --> 01:48:22.095
that we would need to rely a lot
on support from Google in order

01:48:22.095 --> 01:48:22.896
to do it well.

01:48:22.896 --> 01:48:25.065
We've really been
blown away there.

01:48:25.065 --> 01:48:27.133
So thank you for the great
partnership and the great

01:48:27.133 --> 01:48:28.301
support.

01:48:28.301 --> 01:48:29.803
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great, thank you.

01:48:29.803 --> 01:48:33.874
[ Applause ]

01:48:33.874 --> 01:48:38.245
&gt;&gt; One of
Spotify's challenges was,

01:48:38.245 --> 01:48:40.780
can you imagine, is that as big
as that service is is just the

01:48:40.780 --> 01:48:42.916
pure ingestion rate
that they have.

01:48:42.916 --> 01:48:46.720
And moving from Kafka and
taking advantage of PubSub?

01:48:46.720 --> 01:48:48.655
So the ingestion
rate is amazing.

01:48:48.655 --> 01:48:50.156
Running services at
that scale is amazing.

01:48:50.156 --> 01:48:52.692
So we actually wanted to give
you a demo and show you a

01:48:52.692 --> 01:48:56.496
version of these
services in action.

01:49:01.268 --> 01:49:04.604
&gt;&gt; Okay, one last time with me.

01:49:04.604 --> 01:49:06.740
So, I think Nick mentioned this.

01:49:06.740 --> 01:49:12.612
But at its peak, Spotify is
serving something like a billion

01:49:12.612 --> 01:49:15.115
streams.

01:49:15.115 --> 01:49:16.616
I need a slide update here.

01:49:16.616 --> 01:49:19.052
I've got Eric Schmitt on stage
to help me with this demo.

01:49:19.052 --> 01:49:20.553
Not the Eric Schmitt
from earlier,

01:49:20.553 --> 01:49:22.422
but a slightly
different Eric Schmitt.

01:49:22.422 --> 01:49:24.724
You can imagine the fun
crossings of e-mail that they

01:49:24.724 --> 01:49:26.593
have.

01:49:26.593 --> 01:49:27.227
Yeah, exactly.

01:49:27.227 --> 01:49:31.798
The first step we want to --
we want to start with here is

01:49:31.798 --> 01:49:34.801
ingestion.

01:49:34.801 --> 01:49:37.971
One of the challenges that
Spotify has is how to take data

01:49:37.971 --> 01:49:41.041
and bring it in at the kind
of rates that they deal with.

01:49:41.041 --> 01:49:43.944
They use Cloud PubSub
in order to do that.

01:49:43.944 --> 01:49:47.347
Cloud PubSub is a fully managed
service for ingesting data at

01:49:47.347 --> 01:49:48.448
scale.

01:49:48.448 --> 01:49:51.318
Now what we're going to show you
here is the same sort of tools

01:49:51.318 --> 01:49:54.721
and give you a glimpse of how
Spotify analyzes their data.

01:49:54.721 --> 01:49:58.625
Now, Spotify actually treats
their customer data and privacy

01:49:58.625 --> 01:50:00.327
very, very securely.

01:50:00.327 --> 01:50:03.763
So we have generated some test
data of the same volume and the

01:50:03.763 --> 01:50:06.299
same shape that Spotify
actually encounters.

01:50:06.299 --> 01:50:10.637
So what you see here may not
exactly match what Spotify sees

01:50:10.637 --> 01:50:11.571
in production.

01:50:11.571 --> 01:50:13.006
So let's start with ingestion.

01:50:13.006 --> 01:50:16.042
This pipeline is currently
running at about 150,000

01:50:16.042 --> 01:50:19.779
to 200,000 events per second.

01:50:19.779 --> 01:50:21.915
Now this actually
is running now.

01:50:21.915 --> 01:50:23.817
And throughout the demo,
while we do this demo,

01:50:23.817 --> 01:50:28.355
we're going to continuously be
ingesting more user events at

01:50:28.355 --> 01:50:29.923
about 100,000, 200,000
events per second.

01:50:29.923 --> 01:50:32.258
They can get up to 700,000.

01:50:32.258 --> 01:50:36.329
And what these events are are
all of the interactions that

01:50:36.329 --> 01:50:38.298
people do with their mobile
application on Spotify.

01:50:38.298 --> 01:50:41.334
You hit play, you pause,
you skip, you share,

01:50:41.334 --> 01:50:42.168
you make a play list.

01:50:42.168 --> 01:50:45.672
That data is gold for Spotify
in terms of building a better

01:50:45.672 --> 01:50:48.942
service or building new
features like discovery weekly.

01:50:48.942 --> 01:50:52.879
And with PubSub, they can
ingest that data at scale,

01:50:52.879 --> 01:50:56.049
at volume without having
to manage infrastructure.

01:50:56.049 --> 01:51:00.020
Without having to maintain
their own Kafka clusters.

01:51:00.020 --> 01:51:03.356
So PubSub is globally
available, fully managed.

01:51:03.356 --> 01:51:06.493
It scales from a few events
per section up to hundreds of

01:51:06.493 --> 01:51:07.761
thousands.

01:51:07.761 --> 01:51:11.031
Now the next step after the
data has been ingested is you

01:51:11.031 --> 01:51:15.268
actually need to extract that
data and transform it into the

01:51:15.268 --> 01:51:16.569
various storage systems.

01:51:16.569 --> 01:51:18.705
So Eric's going to switch over
and we're going to show data

01:51:18.705 --> 01:51:20.073
flow.

01:51:20.073 --> 01:51:22.709
Spotify is actively working on
moving to data flow as their

01:51:22.709 --> 01:51:25.045
centralized ETO
processing system.

01:51:25.045 --> 01:51:28.114
This is a data
flow visualization,

01:51:28.114 --> 01:51:29.749
and it's running
live as we talk now.

01:51:29.749 --> 01:51:32.685
And it's taking
data, sharding it,

01:51:32.685 --> 01:51:35.955
windowing it by hour so that
they get hour-by-hour updates to

01:51:35.955 --> 01:51:39.259
the data and then ultimately
writing that data into multiple

01:51:39.259 --> 01:51:42.562
data sources, BigQuery, GCS, HTFS.

01:51:43.663 --> 01:51:46.332
And it does all this with
a fraction of the latency.

01:51:46.332 --> 01:51:49.002
What used to take an hour
now takes 15 minutes.

01:51:49.002 --> 01:51:53.039
So they're able to get much
closer to realtime access to how

01:51:53.039 --> 01:51:55.008
users are interacting
with their application.

01:51:55.008 --> 01:51:58.144
So that's our pipeline
running, and, again,

01:51:58.144 --> 01:51:59.612
this is running live
while we're on stage.

01:51:59.612 --> 01:52:02.615
We can ingest the data,
we can run a pipeline,

01:52:02.615 --> 01:52:04.751
we can store it in
the different systems.

01:52:04.751 --> 01:52:07.420
Now we actually want to find
the needles in the haystack.

01:52:07.420 --> 01:52:08.588
We want to analyze.

01:52:08.588 --> 01:52:09.956
And that's where
BigQuery comes in.

01:52:09.956 --> 01:52:13.993
Eric's written a query here that
is going to calculate the top

01:52:13.993 --> 01:52:15.962
artists that people
are streaming.

01:52:16.596 --> 01:52:19.232
Now, bear in mind, this is test
data so it may not match what

01:52:19.232 --> 01:52:21.734
billboard says the
actual top artists are.

01:52:21.734 --> 01:52:23.570
So he's going to go
ahead and run this query.

01:52:23.570 --> 01:52:26.940
And while it runs, let me tell
you that this query is going to

01:52:26.940 --> 01:52:31.845
join over a billion events of
user interaction with over 20

01:52:31.845 --> 01:52:35.882
million tracks of music
with over a million artists.

01:52:35.882 --> 01:52:40.620
And in four seconds, it
processes 22 gigs of data to

01:52:40.620 --> 01:52:45.758
come up with that -- Justin
Bieber is the top artist.

01:52:45.758 --> 01:52:49.195
Well, the demo is Bieberlicious
so far, let's plow on.

01:52:49.195 --> 01:52:52.999
But again, what's valuable
here is what you're not seeing.

01:52:52.999 --> 01:52:57.170
There's no recreating indexes
when the data goes up by a

01:52:57.170 --> 01:52:58.171
factor of ten.

01:52:58.171 --> 01:53:00.473
There's no maintaining VMs,
there's no patching operating

01:53:00.473 --> 01:53:01.574
systems.

01:53:01.574 --> 01:53:05.545
They just get to write queries
and get data on the other side.

01:53:05.545 --> 01:53:08.114
Great, so now we've
looked at the data.

01:53:08.114 --> 01:53:10.416
Let's take a look at how
Spotify might be able to do

01:53:10.416 --> 01:53:14.420
collaborative analysis with a
product like Cloud Data Lab.

01:53:14.420 --> 01:53:17.824
Cloud Data Lab gives you
these nice visual notebooks.

01:53:17.824 --> 01:53:21.461
It's based on Jupiter, the open
source project formerly known as

01:53:21.461 --> 01:53:22.962
iPython.

01:53:22.962 --> 01:53:26.666
And it allows you to combine not
just BigQuery data but because

01:53:26.666 --> 01:53:30.136
it's based on Python, you
actually can use really popular

01:53:30.136 --> 01:53:33.907
packages like Pandas
to do analytics.

01:53:33.907 --> 01:53:36.976
So at the top, you see that
-- let's just scroll back --

01:53:36.976 --> 01:53:39.646
there's that same BigQuery
query that we just used in the

01:53:39.646 --> 01:53:40.713
console.

01:53:40.713 --> 01:53:42.715
But if you go down
to the bottom,

01:53:42.715 --> 01:53:46.085
we're piping that into a Pandas
data frame in order to be able

01:53:46.085 --> 01:53:49.622
to do visualizations and
charts right within Pandas.

01:53:49.622 --> 01:53:51.624
So we made a data
frame out of it.

01:53:51.624 --> 01:53:52.659
We asked Pandas to plot it.

01:53:52.659 --> 01:53:55.762
And you see we've got a
pretty typical fat head,

01:53:55.762 --> 01:53:57.830
long tail distribution of music.

01:53:57.830 --> 01:54:00.300
And that got Eric
and I thinking.

01:54:00.300 --> 01:54:03.169
You know, maybe there's another
more valuable way to look at

01:54:03.169 --> 01:54:05.405
this than just top plays.

01:54:05.405 --> 01:54:08.441
Maybe we should do further
analysis and take into account

01:54:08.441 --> 01:54:10.677
the number of tracks
that you have,

01:54:10.677 --> 01:54:13.079
not just the number of plays.

01:54:13.079 --> 01:54:16.015
Data Lab allows me to do this
sort of interactive what-if

01:54:16.015 --> 01:54:19.719
analysis in a visual notebook
way that could be shared with

01:54:19.719 --> 01:54:20.753
other users.

01:54:20.753 --> 01:54:24.791
So we've added more on -- we've
extended this BigQuery to

01:54:24.791 --> 01:54:27.994
calculate what we call how
interesting an artist is by

01:54:27.994 --> 01:54:29.762
taking into account how
many tracks there are.

01:54:29.762 --> 01:54:32.165
So he's going to run the rest
of our analysis through to

01:54:32.165 --> 01:54:33.166
completion.

01:54:36.002 --> 01:54:38.271
And this query -- this
analysis will be running live.

01:54:38.271 --> 01:54:41.874
Again, data is being streamed,
data flow is continuing to pump

01:54:41.874 --> 01:54:42.875
that data in.

01:54:42.875 --> 01:54:44.210
We're getting realtime looks.

01:54:44.210 --> 01:54:47.413
And by this, it looks like
Nirvana and Torii Amos and Bob

01:54:47.413 --> 01:54:50.550
Dylan are more interesting than
Justin Bieber so all is right

01:54:50.550 --> 01:54:51.985
with the world.

01:54:51.985 --> 01:54:53.353
[ Applause ]

01:54:53.353 --> 01:54:56.689
So, one last thing.

01:54:56.689 --> 01:55:00.560
What about summarizing the data
and building dashboards for your

01:55:00.560 --> 01:55:04.597
managers to get realtime
live information?

01:55:04.597 --> 01:55:08.835
Data Studio 360 is a new service
that allows you to do just that.

01:55:08.835 --> 01:55:10.937
To create beautiful
dashboard visualizations.

01:55:10.937 --> 01:55:14.040
And it works not only with Google Cloud Services like

01:55:14.040 --> 01:55:17.510
BigQuery, but it also works with
Google Analytics so you can

01:55:17.510 --> 01:55:19.579
combine your business data
with your web traffic data.

01:55:19.579 --> 01:55:23.283
And it works with Google Sheets
so if you have spreadsheets with

01:55:23.283 --> 01:55:27.186
ad hoc data, all of that can
be brought into Data Studio.

01:55:27.186 --> 01:55:30.657
So here's our version
of the dashboard here.

01:55:30.657 --> 01:55:32.525
And you'll notice it's showing
you the number of events,

01:55:32.525 --> 01:55:34.994
the number of unique users,
the number of unique tracks.

01:55:34.994 --> 01:55:38.531
We've got some very nice
visualizations of charts about

01:55:38.531 --> 01:55:41.934
where -- whether somebody
is searching or browsing.

01:55:41.934 --> 01:55:47.874
So, we're going to extend this
-- this dashboard and get some

01:55:47.874 --> 01:55:50.109
access to geographic
information.

01:55:50.109 --> 01:55:52.912
Because one of the things we
know about users is when they

01:55:52.912 --> 01:55:54.814
registered, what city
did they register.

01:55:54.814 --> 01:56:04.691
So Eric is going to drop a map
control onto our dashboard.

01:56:04.691 --> 01:56:05.191
Great.

01:56:05.191 --> 01:56:08.127
And he's going to connect
that map up to two pieces of

01:56:08.127 --> 01:56:10.029
information.

01:56:10.029 --> 01:56:15.401
He's going to tell it --
he's going to tell it --

01:56:15.401 --> 01:56:16.969
proof that
these are live demos.

01:56:20.773 --> 01:56:21.774
Okay, got an issue.

01:56:21.774 --> 01:56:26.479
What you would have seen is he
would connect that data up to

01:56:26.479 --> 01:56:29.615
our user information, be able
to identify the city and state.

01:56:29.615 --> 01:56:30.616
Oh, there it goes.

01:56:30.616 --> 01:56:32.719
See, I just needed
to be patient.

01:56:32.719 --> 01:56:33.820
Connect it up to the user.

01:56:33.820 --> 01:56:36.189
He's going to tell it that
the information that we're

01:56:36.189 --> 01:56:39.726
interested in looking at is the
city that the user is registered

01:56:39.726 --> 01:56:43.096
in and that the data we want
is the number of streams that

01:56:43.096 --> 01:56:44.097
they've listened to.

01:56:44.097 --> 01:56:47.633
And then as he does this, you'll
see the data start to propagate

01:56:47.633 --> 01:56:49.535
in in real time with a map.

01:56:49.535 --> 01:56:51.704
So it automatically showed
me a map of the U.S.

01:56:51.704 --> 01:56:53.573
The circles start to bubble in.

01:56:53.573 --> 01:56:57.076
He can drill down and say yeah,
I really care about California.

01:56:57.076 --> 01:56:59.679
And, again, he didn't have
to build that, all built in.

01:56:59.679 --> 01:57:02.348
So there you have, ingestion,
processing, analysis,

01:57:02.348 --> 01:57:03.549
a map dashboarding.

01:57:03.549 --> 01:57:08.054
[ Applause ]

01:57:08.054 --> 01:57:09.889
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Eric.
That was a great demo.

01:57:09.889 --> 01:57:12.692
So, again, we're trying to take
you from a world where your data

01:57:12.692 --> 01:57:15.695
scientists have to babysit
infrastructure and spend 80% of

01:57:15.695 --> 01:57:19.298
their time on that instead of on
insights to a world where your

01:57:19.298 --> 01:57:22.502
data people can just
get the insights.

01:57:22.502 --> 01:57:23.503
Thanks very much.

01:57:23.503 --> 01:57:27.473
I'll turn it back over to Brian.

01:57:33.112 --> 01:57:33.980
&gt;&gt; So, pretty exciting.

01:57:33.980 --> 01:57:36.949
For the past few years we've
been working on sort of what's

01:57:36.949 --> 01:57:37.583
next.

01:57:37.583 --> 01:57:38.184
Right?

01:57:38.184 --> 01:57:42.855
And for us that was, can you
actually start with raw data and

01:57:42.855 --> 01:57:44.457
get insights out of raw data?

01:57:44.457 --> 01:57:48.494
So can you look at just pixels
and understand what the object

01:57:48.494 --> 01:57:49.829
is?

01:57:49.829 --> 01:57:54.167
Can you look inside -- teach a
computer to actually listen to

01:57:54.167 --> 01:57:56.702
just sounds, raw sounds, and
understand what the spoken word

01:57:56.702 --> 01:57:58.471
is?

01:57:58.471 --> 01:58:01.974
Or can you have a computer study
the game of Go and actally be

01:58:01.974 --> 01:58:04.210
able to beat the world champion?

01:58:04.210 --> 01:58:06.846
So two or three years ago, the
answer to all three of those

01:58:06.846 --> 01:58:07.847
would have been no.

01:58:07.847 --> 01:58:08.848
Right?

01:58:08.848 --> 01:58:11.150
But now we can actually say yes.

01:58:11.150 --> 01:58:13.619
So at Google, we've been rapidly
building machine learning into

01:58:13.619 --> 01:58:14.620
all of our products.

01:58:14.620 --> 01:58:15.621
Right?

01:58:15.621 --> 01:58:18.090
And that's why Android Voice
Recognition has gotten so good.

01:58:18.090 --> 01:58:21.894
That's why Google Translate can
translate over 100 languages.

01:58:21.894 --> 01:58:24.697
And that's why, you know, you're
actually seeing what we're doing

01:58:24.697 --> 01:58:27.200
with Google Photos where you can
recognize now it's upwards of

01:58:27.200 --> 01:58:30.136
tens of thousands of objects.

01:58:30.136 --> 01:58:32.738
So on GCP, we're going to give
you that very same machine

01:58:32.738 --> 01:58:35.741
learning technology so
that you can use it, right?

01:58:35.741 --> 01:58:38.544
So whether you want to use
the entire solutions around

01:58:38.544 --> 01:58:40.313
vision-speech translate.

01:58:40.313 --> 01:58:42.849
Or whether you actually want
to train your own models using

01:58:42.849 --> 01:58:44.584
Tensor Flow.

01:58:44.584 --> 01:58:47.086
So one of the very
same Googlers, right,

01:58:47.086 --> 01:58:50.456
that started this whole dupe
craze by becoming one of the

01:58:50.456 --> 01:58:53.626
first authors to map reduce is
actually leading our machine

01:58:53.626 --> 01:58:54.727
learning efforts.

01:58:54.727 --> 01:59:00.366
So my pleasure to welcome Jeff
Dean, the lead of Googlebrain.

01:59:00.366 --> 01:59:13.412
[ Applause ]

01:59:13.412 --> 01:59:15.615
&gt;&gt; All right, thanks, Brian.

01:59:15.615 --> 01:59:18.951
So I think machine learning
is actually one of the most

01:59:18.951 --> 01:59:20.920
important things that's
happening in computing.

01:59:20.920 --> 01:59:23.489
I think it's comparable in
importance to the rise of the

01:59:23.489 --> 01:59:26.259
personal computer, with the
internet, or cloud computing,

01:59:26.259 --> 01:59:27.727
or mobile devices.

01:59:27.727 --> 01:59:30.463
It's really going to transform
not just computer science,

01:59:30.463 --> 01:59:32.865
but lots and lots
of other industries.

01:59:32.865 --> 01:59:36.802
And for us at Google, we've been
using deep learning for the last

01:59:36.802 --> 01:59:37.737
several years.

01:59:37.737 --> 01:59:40.206
And it's really kind of the
secret sauce we've been putting

01:59:40.206 --> 01:59:42.174
behind more and more products.

01:59:42.174 --> 01:59:45.478
You can see kind of our
trajectory of incorporating it

01:59:45.478 --> 01:59:47.880
into more and more
different kinds of areas.

01:59:47.880 --> 01:59:51.384
And the breadth of different
areas that this kind of

01:59:51.384 --> 01:59:55.721
technology is applicable to is
pretty surprising and startling.

01:59:55.721 --> 01:59:59.091
And when we started out in 2012
using deep learning in some of

01:59:59.091 --> 02:00:02.194
our products, it was complicated
and kind of difficult.

02:00:02.194 --> 02:00:05.131
And we built some infrastructure
systems to make it a bit eaiser.

02:00:05.131 --> 02:00:08.634
We're now on our second
generation system and we've made

02:00:08.634 --> 02:00:09.635
it a lot easier.

02:00:09.635 --> 02:00:13.873
And that's why you see that
rise in the different teams,

02:00:13.873 --> 02:00:18.010
that have been able to apply
this to their products.

02:00:18.010 --> 02:00:21.914
So one of the things that can
make it easy is perhaps you want

02:00:21.914 --> 02:00:25.418
to use pretrained models that
just work for a variety of

02:00:25.418 --> 02:00:28.988
different kinds of domains and
you want to be able to use them

02:00:28.988 --> 02:00:30.256
through very simple APIs.

02:00:30.256 --> 02:00:33.526
So we've had the cloud translate
API for quite a while now.

02:00:33.526 --> 02:00:37.530
It allows you to basically give
text in one language and we give

02:00:37.530 --> 02:00:40.132
you back corresponding
text in another language.

02:00:40.132 --> 02:00:42.335
We think it's the best
translate service in the world.

02:00:42.335 --> 02:00:44.270
It's very simple to use.

02:00:44.270 --> 02:00:48.140
And people have liked
that for quite a while.

02:00:48.140 --> 02:00:51.344
More recently, we introduced
the Cloud Vision API, which is,

02:00:51.344 --> 02:00:53.646
again, a very simple API to use.

02:00:53.646 --> 02:00:56.048
You give an image, we give you
back all kinds of good stuff.

02:00:56.048 --> 02:01:00.519
So, you know, this is the kind
of information we can give you

02:01:00.519 --> 02:01:02.788
from the raw pixels of an image.

02:01:02.788 --> 02:01:06.559
So let me give you a demo just
over the kinds of stuff you can

02:01:06.559 --> 02:01:08.227
get from Cloud Vision.

02:01:08.227 --> 02:01:15.067
So if we go here -- it would
be more helpful if my demo was

02:01:15.067 --> 02:01:17.903
up.

02:01:17.903 --> 02:01:19.305
Let's see.

02:01:19.305 --> 02:01:25.077
Uh-oh.

02:01:25.077 --> 02:01:25.978
There we go, sorry.

02:01:27.480 --> 02:01:31.584
What we've done here is
we've taken about 100,000

02:01:31.584 --> 02:01:34.987
images and run them all
through the Cloud Vision API.

02:01:34.987 --> 02:01:39.825
And so each vertex in this
cluster graph is essentially one

02:01:39.825 --> 02:01:40.693
image.

02:01:40.693 --> 02:01:43.496
And if I zoom in here, you
know, you see images flying by.

02:01:43.496 --> 02:01:46.132
Let's just kind of
pick one of them.

02:01:46.132 --> 02:01:48.801
I have a precanned
one, because he's cute.

02:01:48.801 --> 02:01:50.202
A little doggy.

02:01:50.202 --> 02:01:54.006
So if you run this
image through,

02:01:54.006 --> 02:01:56.976
this is the kind of data that
you can get from the Cloud

02:01:56.976 --> 02:01:58.544
Vision API.

02:01:58.544 --> 02:02:02.314
So let's just walk through a few
of the different kinds of data

02:02:02.314 --> 02:02:04.116
you might be able to
extract for images.

02:02:04.116 --> 02:02:06.218
We have another cute
animal, that's the internet.

02:02:06.218 --> 02:02:07.286
So we have a cat.

02:02:07.286 --> 02:02:09.321
We tell you it's a cat.

02:02:09.321 --> 02:02:11.791
But more importantly, we tell
you it's a British short hair

02:02:11.791 --> 02:02:13.959
and maybe a semilong hair.

02:02:13.959 --> 02:02:16.429
So we can give you pretty fine
grain information about what --

02:02:16.429 --> 02:02:20.032
about what this kind
of image contains.

02:02:20.032 --> 02:02:25.471
Another thing that's pretty
useful for images is, you know,

02:02:25.471 --> 02:02:27.907
the visual world often
has lots of text in it.

02:02:27.907 --> 02:02:32.244
And traditionally, it's been a
big barrier to using text that

02:02:32.244 --> 02:02:35.014
you see in images because you
can't actually extract it very

02:02:35.014 --> 02:02:36.015
well.

02:02:36.015 --> 02:02:38.484
But the Vision API can actually
just give you the ASCII text we

02:02:38.484 --> 02:02:40.086
find in the image.

02:02:40.086 --> 02:02:44.890
And, you know, when you make the
call, you'll get back, you know,

02:02:44.890 --> 02:02:49.361
a very structured kind of thing
here where we tell you the

02:02:49.361 --> 02:02:54.800
labels and there's the actual
text for the thing as an ASCII

02:02:54.800 --> 02:02:57.002
string and you can do
whatever you want with that.

02:02:58.537 --> 02:03:02.141
Faces are another kind of
important thing for photographs

02:03:02.141 --> 02:03:03.109
that people take.

02:03:03.109 --> 02:03:07.379
So it's actually pretty nice
that we can identify where faces

02:03:07.379 --> 02:03:09.014
are in the images.

02:03:09.014 --> 02:03:13.686
We can tell you if a particular
face is happy or sad.

02:03:13.686 --> 02:03:15.154
You know, some of the
people here are happy.

02:03:15.154 --> 02:03:15.921
Not everyone.

02:03:15.921 --> 02:03:21.093
And again, that comes back as
like pretty structured j-sign

02:03:21.093 --> 02:03:22.695
information.

02:03:22.695 --> 02:03:25.131
Another kind of important thing
is people often take photographs

02:03:25.131 --> 02:03:29.135
of sort of popular
landmarks around the world.

02:03:29.135 --> 02:03:31.971
And so, you knowm, here's
a picture of a stadium,

02:03:31.971 --> 02:03:35.941
we'd like to know where that is
so we can click on the landmark

02:03:35.941 --> 02:03:38.778
detection facility and that
tells us it's Citi Field,

02:03:38.778 --> 02:03:41.347
Stadium, gives us the
longitude, latitude.

02:03:41.347 --> 02:03:43.549
That turns out to be in New York
because that's where the Mets

02:03:43.549 --> 02:03:44.383
play.

02:03:44.383 --> 02:03:47.553
And finally, sometimes it's
useful to be able to look at a

02:03:47.553 --> 02:03:51.023
logo or an image and understand
what logos appear in it.

02:03:51.023 --> 02:03:56.929
And so we can tell you that that
image is the Android 2.2 logo.

02:03:57.997 --> 02:03:59.498
Great.

02:03:59.498 --> 02:04:00.032
Okay.

02:04:00.032 --> 02:04:02.368
So that's kind of a tour of the
kinds of things you can do with

02:04:02.368 --> 02:04:03.202
the Vision API.

02:04:03.202 --> 02:04:05.437
If you have lots of visual
imagery, you can run it through,

02:04:05.437 --> 02:04:08.674
you know, Vision API,
cluster it, manipulate it,

02:04:08.674 --> 02:04:10.009
extract information from it.

02:04:10.009 --> 02:04:12.511
And we think that's a really
powerful kind of concept for a

02:04:12.511 --> 02:04:14.747
lot of kinds of things
you might want to do.

02:04:16.182 --> 02:04:18.284
We're not going to
stop at vision, though.

02:04:18.284 --> 02:04:21.687
We've had a really good speech
recognition system for quite a

02:04:21.687 --> 02:04:25.891
while on our android phones and
we're now exposing this as an

02:04:25.891 --> 02:04:27.092
additional API.

02:04:27.092 --> 02:04:28.894
It's again very simple to use.

02:04:28.894 --> 02:04:32.731
You essentially just give us an
audio wav form and we give you

02:04:32.731 --> 02:04:34.834
back a transcript of
what is being said.

02:04:34.834 --> 02:04:37.036
And it works in, you know,
dozens of different languages,

02:04:37.036 --> 02:04:41.440
actually more than 50 languages,
and it basically allows you to

02:04:41.440 --> 02:04:44.844
convert audio to text using
machine learning under the

02:04:44.844 --> 02:04:45.644
covers.

02:04:45.644 --> 02:04:49.849
One of the really nice things
is it can work in both a batch

02:04:49.849 --> 02:04:52.518
mode, if you just have a lot
of text or audio that's already

02:04:52.518 --> 02:04:54.653
been recorded, but it could
also work in a streaming mode.

02:04:54.653 --> 02:04:56.789
And so someone could be
speaking into a phone,

02:04:56.789 --> 02:04:59.825
you send that to the cloud and
we give you back a transcript in

02:04:59.825 --> 02:05:00.926
real time.

02:05:00.926 --> 02:05:03.796
So we think that's
pretty powerful.

02:05:03.796 --> 02:05:06.732
So those pretrain models are a
really good way to get started

02:05:06.732 --> 02:05:09.368
using the power of machine
learning and building insights

02:05:09.368 --> 02:05:11.437
into the data you
might already have.

02:05:11.437 --> 02:05:15.808
Let me show you now,
to switch gears a bit,

02:05:15.808 --> 02:05:19.845
let's talk about how you might
want to sort of design and train

02:05:19.845 --> 02:05:21.814
your own machine learn models.

02:05:21.814 --> 02:05:23.916
So we've been doing this over
the last five or six years.

02:05:23.916 --> 02:05:29.555
Now, TensorFlow is a system that
we open sourced in November of

02:05:29.555 --> 02:05:30.489
last year.

02:05:30.489 --> 02:05:34.193
It's already the number one
repository for GitHub for

02:05:34.193 --> 02:05:37.897
machine learning in five months.

02:05:37.897 --> 02:05:38.397
So we're pretty happy with that.

02:05:38.397 --> 02:05:40.032
The external community has
been very receptive to this.

02:05:40.733 --> 02:05:43.702
You know, I think one of the
nice things about TensorFlow's

02:05:43.702 --> 02:05:45.471
release was we put together a
bunch of tutorials that come

02:05:45.471 --> 02:05:49.108
with it that show you how to do
different kinds of machine learning tasks.

02:05:49.108 --> 02:05:51.744
And that's been pretty
well receive I think.

02:05:51.744 --> 02:05:56.181
So we originally designed it
as a system and platform to be

02:05:56.181 --> 02:05:59.118
flexible enough for trying lots
of different kinds of machine

02:05:59.118 --> 02:06:00.586
learning ideas and research.

02:06:00.586 --> 02:06:05.324
But also then be robust enough
to take it and then scale those

02:06:05.324 --> 02:06:09.061
ideas to real world data set
sizes and then move them to

02:06:09.061 --> 02:06:11.263
deployment in real products
in the same system.

02:06:11.263 --> 02:06:14.466
So we want really easy and
flexible ways of expressing

02:06:14.466 --> 02:06:17.937
machine learning models and then
move them to production really

02:06:17.937 --> 02:06:18.938
easily.

02:06:18.938 --> 02:06:24.543
So, the outside reception to the
TensorFlow open source project

02:06:24.543 --> 02:06:25.544
has been really great.

02:06:25.544 --> 02:06:29.848
There's already 800 repositories
on get help alone that use

02:06:29.848 --> 02:06:31.850
TensorFlow in some way.
I'll just show you one example.

02:06:31.850 --> 02:06:37.189
So there's a really cool paper
from a couple of universities in

02:06:37.189 --> 02:06:42.127
Germany where you can give an
image and a painting and then it

02:06:42.127 --> 02:06:44.763
will render that image in
the style of that painter.

02:06:44.763 --> 02:06:46.699
So this is Van
Gogh's Starry Night.

02:06:46.699 --> 02:06:50.035
Plus that image of
the Boston skyline,

02:06:50.035 --> 02:06:53.906
and it's rendered in the style
of Van Gogh but it's the Boston

02:06:53.906 --> 02:06:56.608
skyline, which presumably
Van Gogh didn't get to see.

02:06:56.608 --> 02:07:00.346
And that's all
expressed in TensorFlow.

02:07:00.346 --> 02:07:02.815
There's an easy repository that
you can download it and use.

02:07:02.815 --> 02:07:06.185
So the power of TensorFlow is
that people can use it to do all

02:07:06.185 --> 02:07:08.220
kinds of different things
and people already are.

02:07:08.220 --> 02:07:15.627
So one of the things that
we found is that it's really

02:07:15.627 --> 02:07:19.164
important to bring the power of
machine learning to all kinds of

02:07:19.164 --> 02:07:22.568
different users both
internally and now externally.

02:07:22.568 --> 02:07:25.270
So I'm really happy to announce that we're introducing a

02:07:25.270 --> 02:07:28.307
cloud machine learning product
based around TensorFlow.

02:07:28.307 --> 02:07:32.011
So really this product will
allow you to understand your

02:07:32.011 --> 02:07:35.280
data, visualize it, prepare
it for machine learning,

02:07:35.280 --> 02:07:37.950
using the other tools that
already exist in our cloud,

02:07:37.950 --> 02:07:42.087
and then you can use the power
of our system to solve your

02:07:42.087 --> 02:07:46.025
problems and your particular
contacts or problems.

02:07:46.025 --> 02:07:50.195
So, and then we allow you to
take those models that you've

02:07:50.195 --> 02:07:52.965
developed and deploy
them at scale.

02:07:52.965 --> 02:07:55.034
So enough talking about that.

02:07:55.034 --> 02:07:57.336
Let's actually see how this
might work in practice with a

02:07:57.336 --> 02:07:59.371
demo.

02:07:59.371 --> 02:08:01.140
So if we go back over here.

02:08:01.140 --> 02:08:06.278
What I have here is, again,
another Data Lab Notebook.

02:08:06.278 --> 02:08:09.314
So the Data Lab Notebook
style is a really easy way to

02:08:09.314 --> 02:08:13.218
collaborate on having multiple
people work on trying to figure

02:08:13.218 --> 02:08:15.287
out a good machine learning
model for a particular problem

02:08:15.287 --> 02:08:16.388
you might have.

02:08:16.388 --> 02:08:22.261
What we've done here is put in
this notebook we have downloaded

02:08:22.261 --> 02:08:25.564
some data from our Criteo
Kaggle competition.

02:08:25.564 --> 02:08:29.301
So this is just a data set
of 10 gigabites in size.

02:08:29.301 --> 02:08:30.702
45 million records.

02:08:30.702 --> 02:08:32.838
These are a few of
the records there.

02:08:32.838 --> 02:08:37.676
And what you see is it has a
bunch of integer fields plus

02:08:37.676 --> 02:08:38.610
some string fields.

02:08:38.610 --> 02:08:41.046
These are actually hashed
for anonymization for the

02:08:41.046 --> 02:08:42.014
competition.

02:08:42.014 --> 02:08:45.684
But these might be things like
the city the user was in or

02:08:45.684 --> 02:08:46.585
something.

02:08:46.585 --> 02:08:49.621
And then we're going to try
to predict whether or not this

02:08:49.621 --> 02:08:52.891
particular set of other
variables resulted in a click or

02:08:52.891 --> 02:08:53.959
not.

02:08:53.959 --> 02:08:56.562
So our goal was to take all of
the other data in the record and

02:08:56.562 --> 02:08:58.597
predict is this a click or not?

02:08:58.597 --> 02:09:01.333
And you can imagine, you know,
that kind of scenario is pretty

02:09:01.333 --> 02:09:04.369
common in a lot of settings
where you might have customers

02:09:04.369 --> 02:09:06.038
and you want to predict if
they're going to buy something

02:09:06.038 --> 02:09:09.575
this month or you might have
patients and predict if they

02:09:09.575 --> 02:09:11.143
might want follow-up
visits or something.

02:09:11.143 --> 02:09:16.849
So, one of the things in Data
Lab is that it allows you to

02:09:16.849 --> 02:09:20.085
really easily get a sense of
the kind of data that exists.

02:09:20.085 --> 02:09:23.255
You know, you can get histograms
for each of the fields in your

02:09:23.255 --> 02:09:24.189
data set.

02:09:24.189 --> 02:09:26.792
You can understand which ones
might have missing values and so

02:09:26.792 --> 02:09:27.726
on.

02:09:27.726 --> 02:09:34.166
But integrated into this, we
sat down -- and a few machine

02:09:34.166 --> 02:09:36.168
learning colleagues and I sat
down on a whiteboard and we

02:09:36.168 --> 02:09:39.238
designed a model that we want to
try for this particular problem.

02:09:39.238 --> 02:09:41.673
So in this particular case,
we're going to use enrollment,

02:09:41.673 --> 02:09:45.544
because those work pretty
well for a lot of problems.

02:09:45.544 --> 02:09:48.380
And we're going to take
the string data here,

02:09:48.380 --> 02:09:51.683
put it through an embedding,
take the numeric data,

02:09:51.683 --> 02:09:54.153
the integer of that field,
concatenate all of that data

02:09:54.153 --> 02:09:57.890
together as a set of features
and then put a single layer

02:09:57.890 --> 02:10:01.760
neural network followed by a
single logistic unit at the top

02:10:01.760 --> 02:10:03.962
to predict yes or no for click.

02:10:03.962 --> 02:10:06.632
So that's our training label.

02:10:06.632 --> 02:10:09.434
And then we're going to have an
optimizer that's going to try to

02:10:09.434 --> 02:10:10.669
optimize this path.

02:10:10.669 --> 02:10:12.538
So that's kind of what we came
up with at the white board.

02:10:12.538 --> 02:10:17.743
This, I won't delve in too much,
but this is kind of the code you

02:10:17.743 --> 02:10:20.345
would write in a TensorFlow
to express such a model.

02:10:20.345 --> 02:10:22.514
So, essentially,
you have a graph.

02:10:22.514 --> 02:10:26.251
We're going to have some routine
in this little bit of code that

02:10:26.251 --> 02:10:30.656
creates input to read the
records from the training data

02:10:30.656 --> 02:10:31.757
set.

02:10:31.757 --> 02:10:33.492
We're going to create those
hidden layers I talked about

02:10:33.492 --> 02:10:36.061
with the rectified linear units
and then we're going to create

02:10:36.061 --> 02:10:38.664
our final logistic unit and
then create a training procedure

02:10:38.664 --> 02:10:42.534
that's going to optimize the
loss of that logistic unit.

02:10:42.534 --> 02:10:46.872
So this is like one of those
routines, create a hidden layer.

02:10:46.872 --> 02:10:49.641
So you get the raw sense.

02:10:49.641 --> 02:10:51.343
You know, it's not
two lines of code,

02:10:51.343 --> 02:10:53.111
but it's not so complicated.

02:10:53.111 --> 02:10:58.050
So one of the great things about
this being TensorFlow is that

02:10:58.050 --> 02:11:00.085
you can actually run this
locally on your laptop.

02:11:00.085 --> 02:11:01.787
So many of you
probably flew here,

02:11:01.787 --> 02:11:04.656
and you might not have internet
access on the plane home.

02:11:04.656 --> 02:11:08.994
So you can actually just run
your TensorFlow model locally.

02:11:08.994 --> 02:11:11.196
So you can -- that's the
command you would run.

02:11:11.196 --> 02:11:16.768
And then after a little while,
we will have a trained model.

02:11:16.768 --> 02:11:19.071
And this is a graph
of training over time.

02:11:19.071 --> 02:11:21.840
So this is the number of
seconds that our model has been

02:11:21.840 --> 02:11:22.841
training.

02:11:22.841 --> 02:11:26.311
The blue line is our loss
on the training data set.

02:11:26.311 --> 02:11:29.948
And the yellow line is the
error on the validation side.

02:11:29.948 --> 02:11:31.950
And we want the
error to go down.

02:11:31.950 --> 02:11:33.952
And sure enough at the beginning
of training, it was not so good,

02:11:33.952 --> 02:11:35.821
and then it's gradually
been creeping down.

02:11:35.821 --> 02:11:39.491
And obviously being able to
predict this metric better could

02:11:39.491 --> 02:11:41.893
be quite important in the
particular application you might

02:11:41.893 --> 02:11:42.894
have in mind.

02:11:42.894 --> 02:11:44.463
So that's great.

02:11:44.463 --> 02:11:49.635
And after about 30,000 seconds,
we got down to an error of .304.

02:11:49.635 --> 02:11:52.971
So, great.

02:11:52.971 --> 02:11:56.341
So let me show you how you
make this use our cloud machine

02:11:56.341 --> 02:11:57.409
learning product.

02:11:57.409 --> 02:12:00.279
You take that command line
before and you add dash,

02:12:00.279 --> 02:12:01.246
dash cloud.

02:12:04.316 --> 02:12:06.652
And you can also
optionally tell us hey,

02:12:06.652 --> 02:12:08.687
how many replicas do you
want to use for this model.

02:12:08.687 --> 02:12:11.156
So in this case, we want to use
20 replicas so we're going to

02:12:11.156 --> 02:12:13.759
get 20 way parallelism in
optimizing this particular

02:12:13.759 --> 02:12:15.027
model.

02:12:15.027 --> 02:12:20.098
And now, we get pretty much
the same looking curve,

02:12:20.098 --> 02:12:20.999
which is good.

02:12:20.999 --> 02:12:29.107
And it goes down to about, what is that, .306, 304 at the end.

02:12:29.107 --> 02:12:31.777
I'll show you a
comparative graph.

02:12:31.777 --> 02:12:36.982
But it did it in about .19 seconds.

02:12:36.982 --> 02:12:39.785
So that's the difference between
kind of a full day before you

02:12:39.785 --> 02:12:42.587
kind of get the answer to your
experiment versus, you know,

02:12:42.587 --> 02:12:44.289
20 minutes, 30
minutes, you know,

02:12:44.289 --> 02:12:47.826
you go get a cup of coffee, come
back, and now you can iterate.

02:12:47.826 --> 02:12:50.896
The same way the Spotify team
was talking about how they get

02:12:50.896 --> 02:12:54.499
much faster itteration and are
able to sort of explore their

02:12:54.499 --> 02:12:57.569
data and make insights
much more quickly,

02:12:57.569 --> 02:13:00.072
this is the same kind of
thing for machine learning.

02:13:00.072 --> 02:13:03.909
And here you can see graphs
of the local processing and

02:13:03.909 --> 02:13:06.044
instances per second.

02:13:06.044 --> 02:13:08.747
A 10 replica version in yellow
and a 20 replica version in

02:13:08.747 --> 02:13:10.982
green.

02:13:10.982 --> 02:13:15.287
And just to kind of show you,
this is the magnitude that you

02:13:15.287 --> 02:13:19.991
get in improvement of error
over time if you use the single

02:13:19.991 --> 02:13:22.260
replica versus the 10
replica and the 20 replica.

02:13:22.260 --> 02:13:26.298
And if I switch this to stats
which is a kind of update to the

02:13:26.298 --> 02:13:29.301
model, you see they're
almost on top of each other.

02:13:29.301 --> 02:13:31.937
It just happens
20 times as fast.

02:13:31.937 --> 02:13:33.672
So that's great.

02:13:33.672 --> 02:13:36.174
So we now have a trained model.

02:13:36.174 --> 02:13:37.075
What do we want to do with it?

02:13:37.075 --> 02:13:40.812
Well, one thing we might want to
do is actually deploy that to a

02:13:40.812 --> 02:13:44.850
production setting and see
do we actually observe real

02:13:44.850 --> 02:13:45.984
improvements.

02:13:45.984 --> 02:13:49.521
So what I have here is an
open source load testing tool,

02:13:49.521 --> 02:13:54.025
and it's pointed at making
requests to get predictions to

02:13:54.025 --> 02:13:57.929
one of the models
that we trained.

02:13:57.929 --> 02:14:00.966
And you see we're getting about
-- we're simulating 100 users

02:14:00.966 --> 02:14:03.769
kind of concurrently using some
service and we're doing about 12

02:14:03.769 --> 02:14:06.405
requests per second.

02:14:06.405 --> 02:14:10.041
So one of the real nice things
is to go from, you know,

02:14:10.041 --> 02:14:13.645
the idea you had to a deployed
model and then if it's

02:14:13.645 --> 02:14:16.515
successful, to not have to
worry about scaling anything.

02:14:16.515 --> 02:14:18.884
You don't want to have
any sort of you know,

02:14:18.884 --> 02:14:21.186
ops required to add capacity.

02:14:21.186 --> 02:14:24.723
So let's simulate 100
times as many users now.

02:14:24.723 --> 02:14:27.826
And i'm just going to hit
reload on the refresh tool.

02:14:27.826 --> 02:14:32.397
And all of a sudden,
we're hatching new users,

02:14:32.397 --> 02:14:35.434
apparently they're --
they need hatching.

02:14:35.434 --> 02:14:40.472
So what you see is the latency
is hovering pretty steadily.

02:14:40.472 --> 02:14:44.276
Requests per second are ramping
up, and all of a sudden,

02:14:44.276 --> 02:14:46.812
you know, in maybe 10
or 15 more seconds,

02:14:46.812 --> 02:14:51.316
we're going to go from low-X to
100-x without any work on our

02:14:51.316 --> 02:14:52.617
part required.

02:14:52.617 --> 02:14:55.320
And that's kind of the
experience that we think you

02:14:55.320 --> 02:14:58.690
need for getting machine
learning and trying out lots of

02:14:58.690 --> 02:15:01.026
ideas, iterating
quickly on the ideas,

02:15:01.026 --> 02:15:04.763
and then taking that idea and
moving it into a production

02:15:04.763 --> 02:15:07.499
setting without
any muss or fuss.

02:15:07.499 --> 02:15:12.904
So that's what we have there.

02:15:12.904 --> 02:15:13.371
[ Applause ]

02:15:13.371 --> 02:15:18.477
Yeah.

02:15:18.477 --> 02:15:20.612
So we think that's going to
be pretty exciting for lots of

02:15:20.612 --> 02:15:21.146
people.

02:15:21.146 --> 02:15:23.782
And we need kind of early
users of this system to give us

02:15:23.782 --> 02:15:27.118
feedback so we can get it to
be really robust and solid,

02:15:27.118 --> 02:15:31.089
and even easier to use when
we go to general availability.

02:15:31.089 --> 02:15:33.058
But it's in alpha as of today.

02:15:33.058 --> 02:15:37.762
So we have ready to use machine
learn models, translate, vision,

02:15:37.762 --> 02:15:39.130
and the cloud speech ATI.

02:15:39.130 --> 02:15:41.500
This is an area we'll
be building out more of.

02:15:41.500 --> 02:15:43.702
We think there's a lot more that
can be done in this particular

02:15:43.702 --> 02:15:44.703
area.

02:15:44.703 --> 02:15:48.673
And then what I showed you today
is our cloud machine learning

02:15:48.673 --> 02:15:51.376
product, which works in
collaboration with all of our

02:15:51.376 --> 02:15:53.645
other products where you're
already storing your data in our

02:15:53.645 --> 02:15:54.646
cloud.

02:15:54.646 --> 02:15:56.615
You want to start doing some
data lab exploration and then

02:15:56.615 --> 02:15:58.783
you want to train
machine learning models.

02:15:58.783 --> 02:16:02.754
And using TensorFlow, you're not
locked into our cloud platform.

02:16:02.754 --> 02:16:06.691
You can take that same model,
run it on your desktop or on

02:16:06.691 --> 02:16:10.262
your own premises, deploy
it on your own premises,

02:16:10.262 --> 02:16:12.264
deploy it in our cloud,
it's very flexible,

02:16:12.264 --> 02:16:16.067
and allows you to really move
however -- decide how you want

02:16:16.067 --> 02:16:18.436
to use this thing
in your business.

02:16:18.436 --> 02:16:21.473
So we're really
excited about this.

02:16:21.473 --> 02:16:25.977
So now the kind of tools we
give you are really the kinds of

02:16:25.977 --> 02:16:28.213
things that allow you to do
basic things like store and

02:16:28.213 --> 02:16:29.214
analyze the data.

02:16:29.214 --> 02:16:32.150
These kinds of tools are really
going to be allowing you to get

02:16:32.150 --> 02:16:35.887
an understanding of your data
and really give you the insights

02:16:35.887 --> 02:16:37.455
you need to build the
products you want.

02:16:37.455 --> 02:16:38.890
So, thank you.

02:16:38.890 --> 02:16:43.094
[ Applause ]

02:16:43.094 --> 02:16:44.529
&gt;&gt; And I'll welcome back Brian.

